{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_35", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"git diff --check\ngit status --short\nrg -n 'raw_rs[12]_mask = 3|alu_[ab]_sel = 4|\\\\.fwd_mem_wb\\\\(' cores/bench/test cores/bench/rtl\ncat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md\"", "aggregated_output": " M cores/bench/rtl/core.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/core_pkg.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/forward_unit.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/test/test_mul_pipe.py\n M cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py\n?? cores/bench/test/test_forward_unit.py\n# Predecoded arithmetic/load WB forwarding\n\nImplemented the proposed local optimization without architectural deviations:\n\n- Raw source masks now have four bits: captured RF, younger EX/MEM arithmetic,\n  older MEM/WB arithmetic, and older MEM/WB load data.\n- The older result type is sampled from `ex_mem_w.ctrl.mem_to_reg` alongside\n  the prospective producer tags. Younger priority, x0 exclusion, trap\n  suppression, multiply exclusion, and `next_reg_write` qualification remain\n  unchanged.\n- Registered complete ALU masks have five bits, with PC/immediate at bit 4.\n  EX uses independent flat masked ORs for raw operands and complete ALU\n  operands, fed directly by both existing MEM/WB data registers.\n- WB's architectural mux, MEM/WB multiply reconstruction and register inputs,\n  divider arithmetic/capture, predictor, stalls, reset/flush priorities, and\n  the NRET=1 retirement interface are unchanged.\n\nTests extend the existing operand monitor to check four/five-bit one-hot\nmasks, actual raw/ALU values, alternating older result types, both source\nports, same-rd priority, immediate/AUIPC overrides, and retained invalid WB\ndata. Directed programs cover arithmetic/load/all multiply variants/division,\nillegal instructions, misaligned loads/stores/jumps, and held branch/store/\nALU/division consumers under simultaneous bus stalls. Existing assertions and\nexact-cycle trace hashes are retained. Added `test_forward_unit.py` exhausts\n262,144 tag/enable/type combinations on both operand ports against the original\nthree-source priority formula. Updated the multiply unit-test wrapper for the\nnew EX ports and mask widths, and included the new pipeline tests in ALTOPS.\n\nLocal validation:\n\n- Required Verilator lint command: PASS, no warnings.\n- `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench JOBS=8 bash formal/run_all.sh`:\n  PASS on the first attempt, 53 passed / 0 failed (standard ALTOPS, NRET=1).\n  No RTL fixes or formal retries were needed.\n- `NRET=1 make cosim TARGET=bench`: PASS, both selftest bus modes and CoreMark\n  CRC/UART/bounds validation. The plain Makefile invocation defaults its build\n  to NRET=2 and failed to compile the single-channel harness; supplying the\n  declared NRET=1 fixes the invocation without changing contract files.\n- Canonical CoreMark through the evaluator's `run_coremark_ipc`: PASS,\n  10 iterations / exactly 4,230,003 marker-bracketed cycles with both buses\n  stalled. All evaluator validation checks remain intact.\n- Cocotb: PASS across all 11 runner cases, including real arithmetic, ALTOPS,\n  exhaustive forwarding control, and the original exact-cycle checks.\n  The initial run passed seven runner cases; the multiply wrapper needed\n  the new EX port names, and the new coverage check identified a missing\n  immediately dependent division case. Those test-only issues are corrected;\n  all four affected real/ALTOPS multiply/pipeline runners passed on rerun.\n\n`python3 -m tools.eval.gowin bench --all-options` completed all three vendor\nplacements:\n\n| Placement | Fmax (MHz) |\n| --- | ---: |\n| 0 | 66.806 |\n| 1 | 70.019 |\n| 2 | 70.019 |\n\nMedian Fmax is 70.019 MHz, for 165.529433 iterations/sec at the unchanged\nCoreMark cycle count. This DOES NOT satisfy the required fitness strictly\nabove 182.46424 iterations/sec. Area is 5,513 logic cells, 1,218 registers,\n40 LUT-RAMs, 4 BSRAMs, and 2 DSPs. Placement 0's worst paths include retained\nWB load data through branch recovery to ID validity/prediction; placements\n1/2 are limited by divider recurrence, with operand/address paths also among\nthe worst paths. The hypothesis passes all local correctness checks\nbut regresses measured timing; the implementation is left in place for\nevaluation, without unrelated divider or predictor changes.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
