{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_95", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'git diff --check; git status --short; cat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md'", "aggregated_output": " M cores/bench/rtl/core.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py\n?? cores/bench/test/test_if_queue.py\n# Implementation\n\nImplemented the two-entry fall-through fetch FIFO using fixed 65-bit head and\ntail payload registers. Decode selects only head or the live response. The\nrequest PC advances independently on response acceptance, and buffered PCs and\npredictions are retained. Head and tail payload enables are exactly\n`head_valid ^ stall` and `head_valid && (!tail_valid || !stall)`; neither is\nqualified by ready, acceptance, flush, or redirect. Reset clears both payloads.\nRecovery installs the redirect PC and clears occupancy independently of holds.\n\nBoth late-use detection and prospective forwarding now compare the selected\ndecode instruction. Hazard control receives logical fetch availability. The\npredictor algorithm, arithmetic, backend registers, retirement width, memory\nports, and RVFI ports are unchanged.\n\nThere are no architectural deviations from the specified queue. Decode\nvalidity retains the existing `!(flush || redirect)` expression: hazard flush\nnow includes logical unavailability, so queued instructions remain valid while\nthe physical instruction bus is stalled. This also avoids a redundant direct\navailability qualification on the decode-valid path.\n\nTests use an independent two-element list FIFO and stage-advance oracle,\nincluding retirement-valid checks on every cycle. Ready-bus timing snapshots\nremain unchanged. Stalled lockstep snapshots were replaced by that oracle.\nPrediction snapshots distinguish executed branches from speculative fetches.\nRetained-WB stalls now trigger from active data requests. Directed tests cover\nrecovery at every occupancy, wrong-path store/divider cancellation, queued\nissue during response stalls, and dependent loads/multiplies. The standalone\nfetch test covers all transitions, invalid payload capture, long full holds,\nsaved prediction changes in either slot, resets, and 2,500 randomized events.\n\n# Qualificat\n…[1174 of 4246 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:37104ddd70f95100]…\n71.714 MHz, median 71.714 MHz.\n  Resources: 5,502 logic cells, 1,346 registers, 40 LUT-RAMs, four BSRAMs,\n  two DSPs. Final evaluator fitness is 180.14 iterations/second (unrounded\n  180.13790796), a 3.2664% gain over 174.44. This is BELOW the required\n  182.46424 threshold; champion replacement is not claimed.\n- Final exact-source formal rerun: 53 passed, zero failures, including register\n  consistency, legality, ordered retirement, and liveness. No formal failure\n  or counterexample occurred; no formal fix retries were needed.\n\n# Concerns and scope\n\nThe cycle forecast is reproduced, but the prototype's 73.832 MHz median and\n185.45810 iterations/second were not reproduced. Gowin timing varied across\nequivalent validity/control descriptions; the final implementation retains the\nbest measured median. No real-M formal-deep run was\nperformed; real arithmetic RTL is unchanged and its unit/cosimulation checks\npassed. No correctness properties, formal depths, evaluator files, or other\ncore directories were edited.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
