{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_90","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"git diff --check && sed -n '1,18p' cores/bench/implementation_notes.md && sed -n '47,70p' cores/bench/implementation_notes.md\"","aggregated_output":"# Asymmetric pipelined multiplication\n\nImplemented the requested datapath and availability schedule. Performance\nacceptance has **not** been met locally: the best completed measurement was\n51.510 MHz median and 4,194,434 marker-bracketed cycles, giving 122.8056 rather\nthan the required fitness strictly above 128.11408. Final verification\npassed, and a repeat FPGA evaluation reproduced these measurements.\n\n## Implementation\n\n- `mul_pipe.sv` computes four explicitly signed 17x17 partial products and\n  packs their signed 34-bit results as `{LL,LH,HL,HH}`. Low limbs are\n  nonnegative; high-limb signedness follows MULH/MULHSU/MULHU. MUL uses\n  unsigned limbs. A 16-bit carry-save compressor and 16-bit addition produce\n  the early low result, selected directly into EX/MEM for MUL.\n- EX/MEM owns the 136-bit partial-product field. It follows the existing\n  reset, memory hold, invalid/bubble and divider-drain priorities. Fully\n  forwarded original operands remain in the existing RVFI fields.\n- All original multiply edge and seeded differential vectors moved intact\n  from `test_alu.py` to `test_mul_pipe.py`. The new adapter instantiates the\n  actual EX and MEM stages, including their registers, and checks over\n  100,000 additional streamed operation cases, cross-limb carries, signed\n  extremes, early lows for every signedness, phase alignment, hold, bubble,\n  reset, and original operands. Both real and ALTOPS builds pass.\n- Pipeline tests check ALU/branch/JALR/load-address/store-address/store-data\n  consumers, both source ports, overlapping same-rd writers, x0, load-to-MUL,\n  MUL-to-DIV, independent and dependent multiplies, raw-index aliases, both\n  bus stalls, retained WB data, reset, squashed operations, exact RVFI\n  operands/results/order, and exact-once memory acceptance. They also assert\n  that a consumer cannot enter EX while a newer matching high-half result\n  is unavailable, and that an idle unready dmem bus cannot stall multiplies.\n- Final cosim passed selftest and all three random programs in both\n  bus modes (eight full RVFI comparisons), plus stalled CoreMark. Final\n  retained-source repeat passed with `elfs_tested: 5`. The standalone build requires\n  `NRET=1 make cosim TARGET=bench`; bare make defaults its C++ harness to\n  NRET=2 and initially failed to build. No contract file was changed.\n- CoreMark used the canonical 2K, 10-iteration configuration, both stall\n  flags, and MMIO timing markers. It passed memory bounds, the literal\n  `Correct operation validated.`, and CRCs e9f5/e714/1fd7/8e3a/fcaf.\n\n## Mapping and performance\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
