{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_7", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-003-r3s2.yaml; cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/.agent.hyp-20261001-003-r3s2.last'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261001-003-r3s2\ntitle: \"Bimodal prediction with a short validity-only recovery path\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  Both prior predictor attempts passed formal and cosim and reduced bracketed\n  CoreMark cycles from 4,491,485 to 4,202,246. The round-two predictor scored\n  92.92 at 39.046 MHz on the older divider; it did not include the completion\n  retiming now present in the 103.54 champion. This is evidence for a roughly\n  6.9 percent throughput benefit at constant frequency, not evidence that\n  prediction has already failed on the current champion. However, the current\n  worst reported path crosses forwarding, EX branch resolution, IF's\n  redirect-controlled instruction-to-NOP mux, hazard detection, and ID/EX\n  payload registers. Straight transplantation risks lengthening this newly\n  exposed recovery path. Implement the demonstrated predictor with recovery\n  confined to PC selection and instruction validity so that prediction can\n  save branch slots without routing late branch resolution through decode.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add one direct-control-flow predictor to the current retimed-divider core:\n  a 128-entry two-bit bimodal direction table and exact immediate targets for\n  conditional branches and JAL, integrated with a short validity-only squash\n  path. Keep the existing pipeline depth and nine-cycle DIV/REM scheduling.\n  This is a predictor proposal; the recovery changes are its timing-aware\n  integration, not an independent arithmetic or memory optimization.\n\n  Index the direction table by fetch PC[8:2], reset counters to weakly taken,\n  and implement each entry's saturating transition locally under its decoded\n  update enable. Fetch needs only the selected counter MSB. Avoid a second\n  indexed counter read mux and shared read-modify-write datapath, following\n  the prior Gowin timing lesson. Predecode raw imem_data for legal BRANCH\n  funct3 values {0,1,4,5,6,7} and JAL. Form exact sign-extended B/J targets\n  from the current PC and immediate. Predict aligned branches from the\n  counter and alig\n…[6533 of 9605 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:2eca7f3fe4fc5eb4]…\nand unpredicted JALR, aliasing at 512-byte PC spacing, same-index\n      lookup/update, predictor reset, illegal and misaligned transfers.\n      Stress correct/mispredicted branches with imem stalls, load dependencies,\n      older dmem holds and DIV/REM completion. Put stores, loads, DIV/REM and\n      illegal instructions in squashed slots to verify inert invalid payloads,\n      no wrong-path memory requests or divider launch, no repeated training,\n      and exact-once ordered RVFI with correct operands and next PCs. Check\n      that a trained correctly predicted branch or JAL adds no redirect bubble\n      with ready buses, while retaining every existing correctness test.\nWrote and schema-validated [hyp-20261001-003-r3s2.yaml](/srv/hwebench/clones/gpt-6-astra_xhigh-v2-rep1/cores/bench/worktrees/hyp-20261001-003-r3s2-hypgen/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-003-r3s2.yaml).\n\nProposes bimodal prediction with a shorter recovery path. Expected fitness gain: **7%**, medium confidence; not yet measured.", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
