{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_59", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport json, yaml, jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-001-r13s0.yaml')\ns='''id: hyp-20261001-001-r13s0\ntitle: \\\"Local divisor registers for each existing radix-4 phase\\\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 174.44 iterations/second at median Gowin Fmax\n  73.790 MHz and 4,230,003 bracketed CoreMark cycles. The r6s1 improvement\n  removed quotient/remainder feedback selection with dedicated phase banks,\n  but D and 3D still come from shared global registers. Later micro_opt\n  attempts repeatedly exposed divisor-to-remainder paths when another cone\n  was shortened. In particular r9s0 exposed the first recurrence, and r10s0\n  reported divisor_q to remainder_bank[1] as its worst path. Localize these\n  remaining shared arithmetic operands without changing the recurrence.\nhypothesis: |\n  Extend the divider's existing phase-local state organization to divisor\n  operands. Replace the one globally distributed divisor_q with seven\n  32-bit divisor_bank[0:6] registers, one per recurrence phase. Replace the\n  shared triple_divisor_q with six 34-bit triple_bank[1:6] registers. Each\n  radix-4 phase reads only its own statically indexed D and 3D values.\n  This is a register-distribution micro-optimization, with no new execution\n  stage, iteration, bypass, prediction policy or arithmetic algorithm.\n\n  On the existing accepted launch edge, capture divisor_magnitude into\n  divisor_bank[0] alongside quotient_bank[0] and remainder_bank[0]. Keep\n  the two-bit launch recurrence and signed-magnitude conversion unchanged.\n  When phase p advances under active_q && \"'!complete_q && iteration_q==p,\n  transfer divisor_bank[p] into divisor_bank[p+1] if p<6, on the same edge\n  that writes quotient_bank[p+1] and remainder_bank[p+1]. Use the original\n  enabled-bank timing; do not update every copy on every launch or shift\n  them unconditionally. This makes the banks distinct sequential state\n  rather than identical flops that synthesis can trivially merge.\n\n  During phase zero, compute the full 34-bit 3D from divisor_bank[0] and\n  capture it into triple_bank[1] using the original phase-zero enable.\n  Phase zero still uses its existing narrow combinational d1+d2 for its\n  own three radix-4 digits. For each phase p from 1 through 5, transfer\n  triple_bank[p] into triple_bank[p+1] on the same enabled edge as the\n  corresponding quotient/remainder and divisor transfer. Phase six uses\n  the final local copies and needs no further divisor bank. Keep reset\n  values zero for all new registers and preserve reset'\"'s highest priority.\n\n  In phase p form large_d from divisor_bank[p][31:P] when P<32, and form\n  d1 from its original P-bit low slice. Keep d2 as a shift. For p>0, take\n  d3 from triple_bank[p][W-1:0]. Preserve the original C, N, P and W\n  constants, exact widened subtraction/borrow logic, quotient digit bits,\n  one-hot remainder selection and large-divisor bypass. All 32 divisor\n  bits and all 34 triple bits remain semantically available; do not drop\n  high bits used by the large-divisor guard or overflow-sensitive trials.\n  Allow ordinary synthesis to prune bits proven unused in an early bank.\n\n  Keep the completion/sign-correction logic, zero-divisor override,\n  INT_MIN/-1 behavior, result retention and start/busy/done/accept protocol\n  unchanged. Hold all bank state during a blocked completion. Preserve\n  the real divider's nine-cycle EX occupancy and the identical ALTOPS\n  schedule. The RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS arithmetic branch needs no change.\n  Preserve the observed divisor_q and triple_divisor_q names as read-only\n  aliases of bank zero and triple bank one if existing arithmetic tests\n  use them; neither alias may introduce a dynamic phase-selection mux.\n\n  The expected benefit is reduced divisor fanout and shorter local routes\n  into the existing parallel trial subtractors. The first D bank drives\n  only phase zero and preparation of its successor, rather than all seven\n  phases. Later copies travel beside the matching remainder prefix. The\n  raw storage cost is up to 362 extra bits before pruning: six additional\n  D banks and five additional 3D banks. There are no added CoreMark cycles.\n  The risk is extra placement/routing pressure or a return to the unchanged\n  branch/forwarded-ALU timing limit. The six-percent forecast is speculative\n  with low confidence; measure vendor timing before crediting a gain.\n\n  Preserve existing independent prefix-division assertions and exact\n  arithmetic tests. Add checks that the bank consumed at each phase equals\n  the originally captured magnitude and, after phase zero, its full 3D.\n  Exercise consecutive requests with different divisors, all signedness\n  combinations, high-bit divisors and every large_d boundary. Include D=0,\n  D=1, INT_MIN/-1, reset at each phase, rejected starts while busy, launch\n  delayed by older memory, and completion held across dmem stalls. Verify\n  the unchanged nine-cycle schedule in real and ALTOPS builds.\n\n  Run unchanged full-core formal, real DIV/REM tests and RVFI cosimulation,\n  retaining decoder legality, alignment traps, nret=1 IO, forwarding and\n  retirement/progress checks. ALTOPS formal does not prove the new real\n  bank routing; retain real-arithmetic tests and formal-deep when feasible.\n  Canonical stalled CoreMark must pass all CRCs, validation text, bounds\n  and timing-marker checks and should remain at 4,230,003 bracketed cycles.\n  Evaluate all three Gowin placement options. Acceptance still requires\n  fitness strictly above 182.46424 iterations/second, about 77.183 MHz at\n  unchanged cycles; retain the champion unless that margin is measured.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/div_unit.sv\n    description: |\n      Bank D and registered 3D by existing recurrence phase, transfer them\n      under the predecessor phase's enabled edge and consume static local\n      slices in subtraction and large-divisor checks. Preserve arithmetic,\n      widths, reset, launch, completion, sign correction and ALTOPS schedule.\n  - file: test/test_div_unit.py\n    description: |\n      Preserve exact-result and independent prefix-division checks. Add\n      local-bank validity checks at each consuming phase, changing-divisor\n      back-to-back requests, large-divisor boundaries, reset cancellation,\n      rejected busy starts and held completion in real and ALTOPS builds.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Preserve all assertions and exact-cycle expectations. Exercise mixed\n      DIV/REM dependencies, forwarded operands, consecutive distinct divisor\n      requests and simultaneous bus stalls; check original RVFI operands,\n      results, memory effects and exact-once ordered retirement.\n'''\np.write_text(s)\nh=yaml.safe_load(s)\njsonschema.validate(h,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('VALID',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
