{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_79", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-001-r13s0.yaml')\nh=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text())\nh['title']='Prepare and distribute divider operands to pairs of recurrence phases'\nh['motivation']='''The champion scores 174.44 iterations/second at 73.790 MHz and\n4,230,003 bracketed CoreMark cycles. The r6s1 improvement gave each\nquotient/remainder phase dedicated state, but all phases still read\nglobal D and 3D registers. The r9s0 and r10s0 micro_opt trials exposed\ndivisor-to-remainder timing paths, and r9s1 showed that preparing the\nfirst narrow 3D alone merely moved the limit to EX arithmetic. Test\ncoordinated preparation and distribution of these same divider operands,\nwith a small bank shared by each pair of later recurrence phases.\n'''\nh['hypothesis']='''Localize the existing divider's D and 3D operands in groups of recurrence\nphases, and prepare phase zero's narrow 3D during operand capture. This\nis one micro_opt hypothesis about registered divisor-operand preparation\nand fanout. Keep the existing radix-4 equations, pipeline boundaries,\nnine-cycle DIV/REM occupancy and all other CPU logic unchanged.\n\nRetain the existing phase numbering p=0..6, with consumed prefix lengths\nC=2,8,12,16,20,24,28. Define a static bank selector\nBANK = p==0 ? 0 : 1 + 2*((p-1)/2).\nThus phase zero uses D bank 0; phases 1/2 share bank 1; phases 3/4\nshare bank 3; phases 5/6 share bank 5. Implement four 32-bit D banks\nat indices 0,1,3,5 and three 34-bit 3D banks at indices 1,3,5.\nSparse constant-index arrays are sufficient; unused elements must never\nbe read by execution. No dynamic bank mux or scoreboard is introduced.\n\nOn accepted launch, capture divisor_magnitude in D bank 0. Also capture\nlaunch_triple_q = {2'b0,divisor_magnitude[7:0]} +\n                  {1'b0,divisor_magnitude[7:0],1'b0}\nas an explicitly unsigned ten-bit value. Phase zero has P=8 and W=10;\nits existing large_d bypass discards truncated divisor multiples when\nany divisor bit above bit 7 is set. Therefore this is the exact required\n3D for that phase. Replace only its combinational d1+d2 with this saved\nvalue. Keep the original two-bit launch recurrence and magnitude/sign\nlogic, so no extra launch cycle or ALTOPS shortcut is added.\n\nOn phase zero's original enabled edge, copy D bank 0 to bank 1 and\ncapture full 34-bit 3D from bank 0 into triple bank 1. This is the\nexisting full-3D preparation slot, in parallel with the first recurrence.\nOn phase two's enabled edge, copy D/3D bank 1 into bank 3. On phase\nfour's enabled edge, copy bank 3 into bank 5. Each transfer uses exactly\nactive_q && \"'!complete_q && iteration_q==p, alongside the original\nquotient/remainder successor write. A group receives its divisor before\nits first consuming phase and retains it through its second phase.\nDo not write every bank simultaneously at launch: their distinct enabled\nupdates prevent trivial synthesis merging and limit launch fanout.\n\nUse divisor_bank[BANK] for the phase'\"'s large_d guard and d1 low slice;\nkeep d2 as a shift. For p>0 use triple_bank[BANK][W-1:0] for d3; for\np=0 use launch_triple_q. Preserve all existing C/N/P/W constants, all\n32 D bits and 34 3D bits, widened parallel trial subtractions and borrow\nconventions, quotient digits, one-hot remainder selection and the\nlarge-divisor bypass. Reset every implemented bank and launch_triple_q\nto zero. Keep reset's priority, ignored starts while busy and the\naccepted-start qualification exactly unchanged.\n\nPreserve final sign correction, division by zero, INT_MIN/-1, result\nretention and start/busy/done/accept behavior. Bank enables must prevent\nupdates during held completion. Keep the real and ALTOPS schedule\nidentical; the ALTOPS arithmetic branch itself remains unchanged. Retain\nobservation-only divisor_q and triple_divisor_q aliases of banks 0 and 1\nfor the existing independent arithmetic checks. Do not use a dynamically\nselected observation alias in any execution calculation.\n\nThe timing rationale is to remove phase zero's serial 3D addition and\nreduce each later divisor register's arithmetic fanout from six phases\nto two. Four D banks, three full 3D banks and a ten-bit launch value add\n174 theoretical state bits before pruning. This avoids the larger\nplacement cost of one complete divisor copy per individual phase.\nForwarded ALU/address logic and other divider paths can still limit Fmax.\n\nAn isolated prototype of this exact paired-phase implementation measured\nGowin options 0/1/2 at 68.717, 73.527 and 73.527 MHz, for a 73.527 MHz\nmedian. Option zero used 5,415 LUT4s and 1,384 registers, with unchanged\n40 LUT RAMs, four BSRAMs and two DSPs. Its worst path was retained WB\nload selection through EX arithmetic into data memory. At unchanged\n4,230,003 cycles this projects to 173.8226 iterations/second, roughly\n0.35 percent below 174.44. This is not an accepted improvement; the\ninteger zero-percent forecast reflects a near-neutral measured result,\nnot an unobserved gain above the acceptance threshold. Fully local\nprepared banks measured 72.457 MHz, and one shared later bank measured\n70.328 MHz, supporting the paired grouping within this one hypothesis.\n\nThe prototype passed the unchanged pipeline runner, ALTOPS multiply\npipeline runner and both real/ALTOPS divider runners, four pytest cases\ncovering the existing internal cocotb suites. Preserve every assertion\nand exact-cycle check. Extend the independent prefix-division oracle to\ncheck the local bank when each phase consumes it, using the original\ncaptured magnitude rather than another DUT bank as the reference.\nCover changing divisors across consecutive requests, all signedness\ncombinations, high-bit and large_d boundary divisors, D=0, D=1,\nINT_MIN/-1, reset during every phase, rejected busy starts and held\ncompletion. Check launch/training dependencies and forwarded operands\nunder both memory-bus stalls with exact RVFI retirement and side effects.\n\nFull unchanged formal, RVFI cosimulation and canonical CoreMark remain\nimplementation gates. ALTOPS formal does not prove the real bank routing;\nretain real-arithmetic tests and non-ALTOPS formal-deep when feasible.\nPreserve nret=1 IO, illegal-default decoding, alignment traps, forwarding,\nprogress and exact-once retirement. Keep all CRC/UART/bounds/marker checks.\nExpected CoreMark cycles remain 4,230,003 for the same stall sequence.\nAcceptance still requires fitness strictly above 182.46424 iter/sec,\nabout 77.183 MHz at that cycle count. Retain the champion unless the\nrequired greater-than-4.6-percent margin is actually measured.\n'''\nh['expected_impact']={'fitness_delta_pct':0,'confidence':'low'}\nh['changes'][0]['description']='''Prepare the ten-bit phase-zero 3D at launch; distribute full D and 3D\nthrough static banks for phase groups {0}, {1,2}, {3,4}, {5,6}. Transfer\nbanks only on phases 0,2,4 and consume local slices in the original\ntrial subtractions and large-divisor guard. Preserve all arithmetic,\nwidths, reset, sign/corner cases, completion behavior and ALTOPS schedule.\n'''\ns=yaml.safe_dump(h,sort_keys=False,width=88)\np.write_text(s)\njsonschema.validate(h,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nassert type(h['expected_impact']['fitness_delta_pct']) is int\nprint('VALID',p)\nprint('Projected fitness:',73.527e6*10/4230003)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
