{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_67", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-001-r14s0.yaml')\nd={\n'id':'hyp-20261001-001-r14s0',\n'title':'Reset register-file validity instead of distributed RAM contents',\n'category':'micro_opt',\n'motivation':'''The champion scores 174.44 iterations/sec at 73.790 MHz and 4,230,003\nbracketed CoreMark cycles. Earlier forwarding/mux refinements r11s0 and\nr12s0 did not exceed the acceptance margin, and r13s0's divisor fanout\nchange regressed. The register file still synchronously clears every\n32-bit storage word despite its distributed-RAM attribute. Preserve\nreset-visible zero values with per-register written bits so the data\nRAM itself can use a simpler write port without a bulk-reset path.\n''',\n'hypothesis':'''Replace the register file's synchronous bulk data reset with a 32-bit\nwritten bitmap. Keep the same 32x32 distributed-RAM array and the exact\npublic clock/reset/read/write interface. This is one local storage and\nreset-control micro-optimization; no pipeline or ISA change is involved.\n\nOn reset clear written_q to zero. Otherwise, a write with w_en and\nw_addr\"'!=0 sets written_q[w_addr]. Update regs[w_addr] only when !reset,\nw_en and w_addr!=0. The regs array must have no reset loop or reset\nassignment. Reset takes priority over both an architectural write and\nsetting its written bit; x0'\"'s bit is never set. Existing data may remain\nphysically in RAM after reset but must never become architecturally\nvisible until the corresponding register is written again.\n\nFor each read port retain this exact priority: return zero for x0;\notherwise return w_data on a same-cycle write-first hit; otherwise return\nregs[read_addr] if written_q[read_addr] is set, and zero if it is clear.\nPreserve the original combinational bypass during reset as well: reset\nsuppresses writes on the clock edge, but does not newly gate the existing\nw_en-qualified read bypass. Both ports must independently implement the\nsame rules. Never clear the RAM incrementally, stall the CPU to reset it,\nchange instruction timing, or let unknown/unwritten storage escape.\n\nThe intended benefit is removing an unsupported bulk-clear operation\nfrom the distributed data array while retaining every visible reset\nvalue. Gowin may infer fewer distributed RAM resources and a simpler\nwrite/reset network. The cost is a small resettable bitmap and read-zero\nqualification; these can instead lengthen decode, so vendor measurement\nis essential. Retain the distributed-RAM inference attribute and portable\nSystemVerilog; add no vendor-only primitive or synthesis-only behavior.\n\nAn isolated prototype of these exact nested read priorities passed the\nexisting register-file runner and both pipeline runners with unchanged\nassertions. Gowin placement options 0/1/2 measured 68.250, 76.260 and\n76.260 MHz, median 76.260 MHz. Option zero used 5,281 LUT4s, 1,233\nregisters and 32 LUT RAMs, versus the champion's 5,422, 1,214 and 40.\nBSRAM and DSP counts remained four and two. At unchanged CoreMark cycles\nthis projects 180.2836 iter/sec, about 3.35 percent above the champion.\nThis is below the 4.6-percent acceptance margin, not an accepted gain.\nThe integer three-percent forecast reflects this preliminary measurement.\nFull-core formal, cosimulation and a fresh canonical CoreMark run remain\nrequired implementation gates, not completed prototype validations.\n\nRetain and extend existing tests to dirty every nonzero register, reset\nagain, then verify all 32 read as zero through both ports. Exercise reset\ncoincident with writes, x0 writes, repeated reset, distinct and identical\nread addresses, writes immediately after reset, uninitialized registers,\nand same-cycle bypass before the first stored write. Check a write to one\nregister cannot expose stale data in any other register. Exercise WB\nwrite-first dependencies, held operands and both buses stalled in pipeline\ntests. Prove/reset-check the architectural read behavior independently of\nthe physical RAM contents; do not initialize the unreset array in formal\nto make the property pass.\n\nKeep the nret=1 IO, decoder legality, RVFI operands/order, traps, original\nforwarding and hold priorities, and real/ALTOPS arithmetic unchanged.\nRun the unchanged standard formal and RVFI cosimulation gates and retain\nall canonical CoreMark CRC/UART/bounds/marker checks. Expect exactly\n4,230,003 bracketed cycles with the same stall sequence. Acceptance needs\nfitness strictly above 182.46424 iter/sec, approximately 77.184 MHz at\nthat cycle count; retain the champion unless that margin is measured.\n''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':3,'confidence':'medium'},\n'changes':[\n {'file':'rtl/reg_file.sv','description':'''Replace the bulk reset of regs with a resettable written bitmap. Keep\nthe distributed array unreset, gate its write port during reset, and\nmask unwritten reads to zero below the original x0 and write-first\nbypass priorities. Preserve the public interface and cycle behavior.\n'''},\n {'file':'test/test_reg_file.py','description':'''Preserve every existing assertion. Add dirty-then-reset coverage of\nall registers and both read ports, reset/write collisions, x0 writes,\nfirst-write bypass and selective visibility of newly written registers.\nVerify reset zeroes architectural values without inspecting RAM contents.\n'''},\n {'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'''Preserve existing assertions and exact-cycle checks. Exercise post-reset\nunwritten source registers, WB-to-ID write-first dependencies and held\nconsumers under simultaneous instruction/data bus stalls; check exact\nRVFI operands, results, memory effects, traps and retirement ordering.\n'''}]}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(d,sort_keys=False,default_flow_style=False,width=90))\njsonschema.validate(d,json.load(open('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json')))\nprint('Replaced hypothesis; schema valid')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_67", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-001-r14s0.yaml')\nd={\n'id':'hyp-20261001-001-r14s0',\n'title':'Reset register-file validity instead of distributed RAM contents',\n'category':'micro_opt',\n'motivation':'''The champion scores 174.44 iterations/sec at 73.790 MHz and 4,230,003\nbracketed CoreMark cycles. Earlier forwarding/mux refinements r11s0 and\nr12s0 did not exceed the acceptance margin, and r13s0's divisor fanout\nchange regressed. The register file still synchronously clears every\n32-bit storage word despite its distributed-RAM attribute. Preserve\nreset-visible zero values with per-register written bits so the data\nRAM itself can use a simpler write port without a bulk-reset path.\n''',\n'hypothesis':'''Replace the register file's synchronous bulk data reset with a 32-bit\nwritten bitmap. Keep the same 32x32 distributed-RAM array and the exact\npublic clock/reset/read/write interface. This is one local storage and\nreset-control micro-optimization; no pipeline or ISA change is involved.\n\nOn reset clear written_q to zero. Otherwise, a write with w_en and\nw_addr\"'!=0 sets written_q[w_addr]. Update regs[w_addr] only when !reset,\nw_en and w_addr!=0. The regs array must have no reset loop or reset\nassignment. Reset takes priority over both an architectural write and\nsetting its written bit; x0'\"'s bit is never set. Existing data may remain\nphysically in RAM after reset but must never become architecturally\nvisible until the corresponding register is written again.\n\nFor each read port retain this exact priority: return zero for x0;\notherwise return w_data on a same-cycle write-first hit; otherwise return\nregs[read_addr] if written_q[read_addr] is set, and zero if it is clear.\nPreserve the original combinational bypass during reset as well: reset\nsuppresses writes on the clock edge, but does not newly gate the existing\nw_en-qualified read bypass. Both ports must independently implement the\nsame rules. Never clear the RAM incrementally, stall the CPU to reset it,\nchange instruction timing, or let unknown/unwritten storage escape.\n\nThe intended benefit is removing an unsupported bulk-clear operation\nfrom the distributed data array while retaining every visible reset\nvalue. Gowin may infer fewer distributed RAM resources and a simpler\nwrite/reset network. The cost is a small resettable bitmap and read-zero\nqualification; these can instead lengthen decode, so vendor measurement\nis essential. Retain the distributed-RAM inference attribute and portable\nSystemVerilog; add no vendor-only primitive or synthesis-only behavior.\n\nAn isolated prototype of these exact nested read priorities passed the\nexisting register-file runner and both pipeline runners with unchanged\nassertions. Gowin placement options 0/1/2 measured 68.250, 76.260 and\n76.260 MHz, median 76.260 MHz. Option zero used 5,281 LUT4s, 1,233\nregisters and 32 LUT RAMs, versus the champion's 5,422, 1,214 and 40.\nBSRAM and DSP counts remained four and two. At unchanged CoreMark cycles\nthis projects 180.2836 iter/sec, about 3.35 percent above the champion.\nThis is below the 4.6-percent acceptance margin, not an accepted gain.\nThe integer three-percent forecast reflects this preliminary measurement.\nFull-core formal, cosimulation and a fresh canonical CoreMark run remain\nrequired implementation gates, not completed prototype validations.\n\nRetain and extend existing tests to dirty every nonzero register, reset\nagain, then verify all 32 read as zero through both ports. Exercise reset\ncoincident with writes, x0 writes, repeated reset, distinct and identical\nread addresses, writes immediately after reset, uninitialized registers,\nand same-cycle bypass before the first stored write. Check a write to one\nregister cannot expose stale data in any other register. Exercise WB\nwrite-first dependencies, held operands and both buses stalled in pipeline\ntests. Prove/reset-check the architectural read behavior independently of\nthe physical RAM contents; do not initialize the unreset array in formal\nto make the property pass.\n\nKeep the nret=1 IO, decoder legality, RVFI operands/order, traps, original\nforwarding and hold priorities, and real/ALTOPS arithmetic unchanged.\nRun the unchanged standard formal and RVFI cosimulation gates and retain\nall canonical CoreMark CRC/UART/bounds/marker checks. Expect exactly\n4,230,003 bracketed cycles with the same stall sequence. Acceptance needs\nfitness strictly above 182.46424 iter/sec, approximately 77.184 MHz at\nthat cycle count; retain the champion unless that margin is measured.\n''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':3,'confidence':'medium'},\n'changes':[\n {'file':'rtl/reg_file.sv','description':'''Replace the bulk reset of regs with a resettable written bitmap. Keep\nthe distributed array unreset, gate its write port during reset, and\nmask unwritten reads to zero below the original x0 and write-first\nbypass priorities. Preserve the public interface and cycle behavior.\n'''},\n {'file':'test/test_reg_file.py','description':'''Preserve every existing assertion. Add dirty-then-reset coverage of\nall registers and both read ports, reset/write collisions, x0 writes,\nfirst-write bypass and selective visibility of newly written registers.\nVerify reset zeroes architectural values without inspecting RAM contents.\n'''},\n {'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'''Preserve existing assertions and exact-cycle checks. Exercise post-reset\nunwritten source registers, WB-to-ID write-first dependencies and held\nconsumers under simultaneous instruction/data bus stalls; check exact\nRVFI operands, results, memory effects, traps and retirement ordering.\n'''}]}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(d,sort_keys=False,default_flow_style=False,width=90))\njsonschema.validate(d,json.load(open('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json')))\nprint('Replaced hypothesis; schema valid')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "Replaced hypothesis; schema valid\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
