{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_43", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport json\nfor l in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n r=json.loads(l)\n if r['id']=='hyp-20261001-002-r3s1':\n  print(r['hypothesis']['hypothesis'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "Restructure the combined fetch/decode boundary into independently\ncontrolled payload and validity state. Flushes and missing fetches\nshould create bubbles by changing validity, while decoded instruction\ndata flows independently of the late EX redirect. This is one\nstructural change to the pipeline's bubble/kill representation and\nregister control. Preserve stage count, single-issue retirement,\nbranch resolution timing, the existing one-slot taken-branch bubble,\nload-use timing and divider scheduling. No IPC loss is intended.\n\nIn if_stage, drive out.instr directly from imem_data and out.pc from\nthe PC register, including cycles that are invalid or being killed.\nRemove the instruction-to-NOP substitution for both redirect and\ninstruction-bus stalls. Keep fetch validity separate and keep reset\nand redirect priority on PC updates exactly as today: a legal redirect\nmust update PC even when io_imemReady is low. A failed or misaligned\nredirect retains the existing trap and sequential-PC behavior.\n\nIn id_stage, separate updates of reg_q.valid from every other ID/EX\nfield. Reset may still initialize the complete payload. After reset,\npayload fields capture decoded raw instruction, immediate, register\naddresses and values whenever !stall, independently of flush and\nin.valid. If stalled they hold. The valid bit alone follows the\nexisting reset/flush/hold/capture priority: reset or flush clears it;\notherwise !stall captures in.valid; otherwise it holds. Do not use\nredirect, flush, or redirect-qualified in.valid as a payload reset,\nzeroing mux or capture enable. Capturing wrong-path data with valid=0\nis intentional and must never create an architectural action.\n\nDecouple load-use detection from the instruction-to-NOP mux it formerly\ndepended on. Compare raw instruction source fields against the valid\nload in ID/EX, with rd!=0, qualifying the fetch comparison only with\nimem_ready. Retain the existing conservative use of both source fields.\nIn particular, do not qualify this comparison with a fetch-valid bit\nthat contains redir\n…[2220 of 5292 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:96bdb36faae6229e]…\nvider completion followed by a taken branch. Check\nexact RVFI PC/order/operand traces, store acceptance counts, reset\nbehavior and the existing ten-cycle ready-bus retirement-gap bound.\nRun existing unit tests, unchanged formal checks, and CoreMark cosim\nwith both bus stalls, canonical CRCs and the existing timing markers.\n\nExpect the same 4,491,485 CoreMark cycles for the identical bus-stall\nsequence. Any difference needs a cycle-trace explanation; no frontend\nthroughput improvement is assumed. Measure median vendor Fmax and\nactual bracketed iterations/cycle. Acceptance requires fitness above\n108.30284, approximately 48.65 MHz if cycles stay unchanged. A 15 percent\ngain, approximately 119.1 fitness at 53.5 MHz, is an unmeasured forecast.\nMain risks are the remaining branch-to-valid path or divider recurrence\nbecoming limiting, routing variability, and accidental use of stale\ninvalid controls. Inspect Gowin paths to verify the intended structural\ncut; simulation alone cannot establish the frequency benefit.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
