{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_58","type":"agent_message","text":"The independently captured address moved the timing limit to the divider and also regressed. The strongest tested option is retaining EX payload data while clearing bubble controls. I’m finalizing that hypothesis and checking all three placement options; the results so far do not support a gain above the 4.6% acceptance threshold.\n"}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_59", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-001-r15s0.yaml')\np.write_text('''id: hyp-20261001-001-r15s0\ntitle: \\\"Retain EX/MEM payload data when injecting an inert bubble\\\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 174.44 iterations/sec at 73.790 MHz and 4,230,003\n  bracketed CoreMark cycles. Earlier r3s0/r3s1 work removed wide payload\n  clearing from frontend recovery, but ex_stage still clears the complete\n  EX/MEM bundle for invalid EX slots and every unfinished division cycle.\n  That drives wide ALU, multiplier-partial and store-data zeroing logic\n  from bubble controls. Test payload retention at this existing boundary\n  while explicitly suppressing every bubble's control effects.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace only ex_stage's whole-bundle assignment in the\n  execute_busy || \"'!in.valid bubble arm. Instead of reg_q <= '\"'0, assign\n  reg_q.valid <= 1'b0 and reg_q.ctrl <= '0, leaving every other EX/MEM\n  field unassigned in that arm so it retains its preceding value. Keep\n  the complete ctrl_t clear, not merely valid or reg_write. This is one\n  local sequential-control micro-optimization; no stage, operation,\n  execution latency, hazard rule or memory transaction is added.\n\n  Preserve the existing priority exactly: reset clears the entire bundle;\n  dmem stall holds the entire bundle; div_done transfers div_metadata_q\n  and the division result; execute_busy or invalid EX inserts the inert\n  control bubble; otherwise capture the complete original normal payload.\n  Do not apply this transformation to reset, ID/EX, MEM/WB, divider banks\n  or divider metadata. Leave all normal data assignments and their\n  arithmetic/forwarding expressions unchanged.\n\n  In particular, retain alu_result, mul_partials, write_data, rd and RVFI\n  metadata through a bubble. Clearing ctrl guarantees mem_read=mem_write=\n  reg_write=mem_to_reg=0 and alu_op=ALU_ADD in that bubble. The retained rd\n  must not become a prospective producer: mem_stage.next_reg_write derives\n  from the cleared control, so it stays false. Valid must be zero even\n  though data remains nonzero. Existing memory valid/control qualification\n  prevents requests; WB cannot write or retire the bubble. The inactive\n  address/data bus and invalid RVFI payload may differ, but every accepted\n  request and valid retirement must remain identical.\n\n  This is distinct from capturing a new arbitrary payload on bubble edges:\n  the proposed payload is held, while its complete control is cleared.\n  Preserve MEM/WB's independent hold_wb rule, including retained forwarding\n  data after valid clears. Do not add current-valid gating to WB forwarding\n  or alter saved ID/EX operand masks. A held division continues to use its\n  captured request metadata, and its completed result retains the existing\n  priority over bubble insertion. Keep every trap, decoder default, source\n  priority, nret=1 port, ALTOPS formula and real arithmetic operation intact.\n\n  The intended benefit is replacing per-bit bubble zeroing on the wide EX\n  results with register holding, allowing native clock-enable mapping and\n  reducing control-dependent data muxing. The explicit control clear keeps\n  invalid retained data inert. Area and routing may improve, but enable\n  fanout and unchanged divider/branch paths can prevent any Fmax gain.\n  Judge mapped vendor timing, not an assumed advantage from RTL form.\n\n  An isolated prototype of exactly this change passed both existing pipeline\n  pytest runners, including the real arithmetic suite and the ALTOPS multiply\n  suite, with unchanged assertions and exact-cycle expectations. Initial\n  Gowin placement option 1 measured 73.685 MHz, 5,396 LUT4s and 1,214 registers,\n  with unchanged 40 LUT RAMs, four BSRAMs and two DSPs. Its worst path still\n  ran from retained WB selection through EX/forwarding into fetch recovery.\n  At unchanged CoreMark cycles that projects roughly 174.19 iter/sec, close\n  to but below the champion. Therefore the forecast is zero percent with\n  low confidence, not a claim of an accepted improvement. Complete the\n  remaining placement options and CoreMark validation before judging fitness.\n\n  Preserve every existing assertion. Add directed sequences that leave\n  nonzero ALU/store/multiply payloads immediately before invalid-slot and\n  division-busy bubbles. Check bubble valid and complete controls are zero,\n  payload retention, no false producer matches from retained rd, no extra\n  store/load acceptance and no extra writeback or retirement. Exercise\n  back-to-back DIV/REM, completion followed by a dependent consumer, load\n  and multiply interlocks, redirect squashes, reset during a held division,\n  and simultaneous instruction/data stalls. Check stable active memory\n  requests and exact RVFI source/result/memory/PC/order fields. Do not relax\n  any existing mask-hold, exact-cycle, trap, progress or arithmetic checks.\n\n  Run unchanged full-core standard formal and RVFI cosimulation, and retain\n  canonical CoreMark CRC/UART/bounds/timing-marker validation. Unit-test\n  success alone does not establish full formal correctness. Expect exactly\n  4,230,003 bracketed CoreMark cycles for the same stall sequence. Acceptance\n  requires fitness strictly above 182.46424 iter/sec, approximately 77.184\n  MHz median Gowin Fmax at that cycle count. Retain the champion unless the\n  greater-than-4.6-percent gain is actually measured.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 0\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      In the execute_busy-or-invalid bubble arm, replace whole-reg_q clearing\n      with valid=0 and complete ctrl='0; retain every other payload field.\n      Preserve reset, dmem hold, divider completion and normal capture priorities,\n      all arithmetic, forwarding and the divider request/metadata machinery.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Keep all existing assertions and cycle expectations. Add nonzero-payload\n      bubble tests covering inert controls, retained destination tags, no false\n      forwarding or memory side effects, division completion, reset cancellation,\n      both bus stalls and exact-once RVFI retirement with original operands.\n''')\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\nh=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text());jsonschema.validate(h,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Final candidate written; schema validation passed.')\nPY\npython3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom tools.eval.gowin import build_all,summarize\nimport json\nroot=Path('/srv/hwebench/tmp/867d4c2f/afcd9b6c/r15s0_hold')\nold=json.loads((root/'timing_results.json').read_text())\nr=sorted(old+build_all(Path.cwd(),root/'rtl','fpga/core_bench_si.sv',root/'timing',options=(0,2)),key=lambda x:x['place_option'])\n(root/'timing_results_all.json').write_text(json.dumps(r,indent=2));print(json.dumps(summarize(r),indent=2),flush=True)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
