{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_55", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-001-r7s0.yaml')\np.write_text('''id: hyp-20261001-001-r7s0\ntitle: \\\"Separate the registered load/store address from the general ALU result\\\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 153.96 at median Gowin Fmax 64.576 MHz and\n  4,194,434 bracketed CoreMark cycles. Its worst reported path runs\n  from MEM/WB through forwarding and u_alu into data memory. Prior\n  micro_opt work successfully shortened operand selection, and the\n  latest divider improvement shifted the bottleneck out of division.\n  Detailed vendor timing of a local result-mux prototype identifies\n  the multiplier DSP in this path. mem_stage drives dmem_addr directly\n  from the general EX/MEM alu_result, so every ALU operation feeds\n  the memory address cone even though loads and stores need only ADD.\n  A dedicated registered effective address should remove the multiply\n  result selection from that cone without changing execution timing.\nhypothesis: |\n  Split only the load/store address value from the general ALU-result\n  bus at the existing EX/MEM register boundary. Add a 32-bit\n  effective_addr field to ex_mem_t. On each normal valid EX advance,\n  set reg_q.effective_addr <= rs1 + in.imm, where rs1 is the existing\n  fully forwarded raw source and imm is the saved decoded immediate.\n  This is a local datapath and mux-fanout optimization. It adds no\n  stage, memory request, execution cycle, or instruction interlock.\n\n  The dedicated address adder must take those operands directly,\n  independent of ctrl.alu_op, the multiplier, divider result and\n  general ALU output selection. Do not derive the address from\n  alu_result and do not add an ALU/address selection mux after the\n  address register. Sharing identical addition logic is acceptable\n  only if the final memory address remains independent of operation\n  result selection. Use 32-bit modular addition, preserving all high\n  address bits and the two low byte-offset bits.\n\n  In mem_stage use in.effective_addr consistently for dmem_addr,\n  byte-lane masks, word/halfword alignment checks, load extraction\n  shift and aligned RVFI memory address. Keep reg_q.alu_result <=\n  in.alu_result for the ordinary nonload WB and forwarding path.\n  Keep original store data, load extension, mask qualification,\n  trap-suppressed writes, memory request enables and MEM/WB retention.\n  For every valid load/store, the new address equals the existing\n  ALU_ADD result exactly. Nonmemory instructions issue no memory\n  request; their otherwise unused address bus value may change.\n  Preserve full address width; never exploit benchmark address\n  limits to truncate or alias accesses.\n\n  effective_addr belongs to the complete EX/MEM payload. Hold it\n  with the memory operation during dmem backpressure, reset it with\n  the bundle and clear it when EX/MEM becomes a bubble. The division\n  metadata bundle is already cleared at launch, so division transfer\n  produces zero in this unused field and never issues a memory\n  operation. Preserve exact-once acceptance and retirement, load-use\n  and prospective forwarding rules, branch recovery, x0 behavior,\n  default-illegal decoding, nret=1 and top-level IO. Leave the general\n  ALU, M-extension arithmetic, ALTOPS formulas, nine-cycle divider\n  scheduling, predictor and register file unchanged.\n\n  CoreMark should retain 4,194,434 marker-bracketed cycles for the\n  same bus-stall sequence, so the gain must come from measured median\n  vendor Fmax. Forecast an unmeasured ten percent gain with medium\n  confidence. Acceptance requires fitness strictly above 161.04216,\n  approximately 67.55 MHz at unchanged cycles. Main risks are extra\n  adder/register routing, synthesis merging the cones again, or\n  divider and general ALU feedback timing limiting the new clock.\n  Judge the unchanged three-option Gowin median times measured\n  iterations/cycle, not the disappearance of a single critical path.\n\n  Validate load/store addresses and RVFI byte lanes for all access\n  widths, sign extensions and alignment faults; forwarded bases from\n  ALU, loads, MUL and completed DIV; positive/negative offsets and\n  32-bit wraparound in unit tests; same-rd producer priority; and\n  multi-cycle memory stalls. Compare request addresses only while a\n  load/store request is active and preserve exact instruction and\n  memory-acceptance cycles. Run existing pipeline and RVFI cosim\n  gates, standard formal, then stalled CoreMark with all canonical\n  CRCs, validation text, memory bounds and timing markers intact.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 10\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: |\n      Add a 32-bit effective_addr field to ex_mem_t, distinct from the\n      general alu_result and from the RVFI-only aligned memory address.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Capture fully forwarded rs1 plus the decoded immediate directly\n      into effective_addr at the existing EX/MEM boundary. Hold, reset\n      and clear it with its instruction. Preserve general ALU result,\n      jump/link, divider completion, metadata and forwarding behavior.\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Drive dmem_addr and all address-dependent byte-lane, alignment,\n      load extraction and RVFI memory-address logic from effective_addr.\n      Keep the general alu_result transfer into MEM/WB unchanged, along\n      with memory enables, store data, traps, load extension and holds.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Retain existing assertions and add directed load/store address\n      checks with ALU/load/MUL/DIV-forwarded bases, all byte/half/word\n      lanes, negative offsets and alignment traps. Exercise dmem holds,\n      simultaneous imem stalls and same-rd producers, checking accepted\n      addresses, RVFI masks and exact-once retirement independently.\n''')\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\nx=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text());jsonschema.validate(x,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Updated focused LSU address hypothesis; schema validation passed.')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
