{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_63", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-001-r8s0.yaml')\nx={\n'id':'hyp-20261001-001-r8s0',\n'title':'Register the complete writeback value before forwarding',\n'category':'micro_opt',\n'motivation':'''The champion scores 174.44 at 73.790 MHz and 4,230,003 bracketed\nCoreMark cycles. A fresh option-zero Gowin run reproduced 71.996 MHz.\nSeveral nearly tied worst paths start at MEM/WB load data or its\nmem_to_reg selector and traverse WB selection, EX operand selection,\nand arithmetic before reaching data memory. The reported longest\npath also traverses EX forwarding and branch recovery. Prior micro_opt\nwork registered forwarding masks and removed serial EX source muxes,\nbut WB still selects two separately registered 32-bit values on every\nforwarding path. Moving that selection across the existing MEM/WB\nboundary targets the remaining mux without changing result latency.\n''',\n'hypothesis':'''Capture the final architectural destination value in one MEM/WB\nregister bank instead of selecting load versus arithmetic data after\nMEM/WB. This is one local mux-retiming optimization, with no new\npipeline stage, instruction latency, predictor, or interlock.\n\nIn mem_stage, replace the assignment to reg_q.alu_result with:\n  reg_q.alu_result <= in.ctrl.mem_to_reg ? load_data\n                     : is_multiply ? multiply_result : in.alu_result;\nHere load_data is the existing correctly aligned and sign/zero-extended\nload value. Preserve the existing MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU reconstruction\nand RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS handling. EX/MEM in.alu_result retains its\ncurrent meaning; only the MEM/WB field now carries the complete WB\nvalue. JAL/JALR link and completed DIV/REM values already arrive through\nin.alu_result and must pass through unchanged. In wb_stage, drive\nw_data directly from in.alu_result, leaving write enable and destination\naddress unchanged. Keep the old read_data capture if needed for a small\npatch, but no functional consumer may still select it in WB; synthesis\ncan prune the unused data bank. Do not change raw mem_rdata or any RVFI\nmemory fields, nor move load alignment or extension out of MEM.\n\nPreserve reset initialization and hold_wb behavior exactly: during a\ndata-memory stall, clear only MEM/WB.valid and retain the entire unified\nvalue and producer metadata. WB's valid-qualified architectural write\nenable must remain separate from the retained value used for forwarding.\nDo not gate the value by valid, reg_write, rd\"'!=0, or memory ready. Preserve\nmem_next_reg_write, prospective source-mask priority, x0 handling,\nload/multiply-use bubbles, divider draining/completion, misalignment\ntraps, and exact-once channel-0 retirement with nret=1. The forwarding\ninputs in core and the regfile write-first bypass automatically receive\nthe newly registered value through the existing wb_w_data wire.\n\nThis removes the load/arithmetic mux from WB-to-EX forwarding, WB-to-ID\nwrite-first bypass, and the register-file write-data path, and removes\none unused registered 32-bit data bank from the timed netlist. MEM gains\na result-selection mux before its existing register, but load extraction\nand multiply reconstruction remain separated from subsequent EX work\nby that boundary. The main risks are longer MEM result setup time,\nmultiplier reconstruction becoming critical, another near-critical\nbranch/divider path limiting the benefit, and physical routing changes.\nCoreMark cycles should remain exactly 4,230,003 with the same bus-stall\nsequence. The preliminary forecast is unmeasured; acceptance requires\nfitness above 182.46424, about 77.185 MHz at that cycle count.\n\nValidate ready-bus and stalled execution with loaded values feeding ALU,\nbranch, JALR, load/store addresses, store data, multiply and division.\nCover byte/half/word sign and zero extension, successive loads, load\nfollowed by ALU or multiply, overlapping same-rd writers, a dependent\ninstruction held behind an older stalled memory operation, and reset.\nCompare original RVFI operands, final rd_wdata, request addresses,\nbyte masks and exact-once ordered retirement. Run all unchanged formal\nand cosimulation gates and canonical stalled CoreMark validation,\nincluding CRCs, UART text, memory bounds and timing markers. Measure\nmedian Gowin Fmax across all evaluator placement options.\n'\"''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':8,'confidence':'medium'},\n'changes':[\n{'file':'rtl/mem_stage.sv','description':'''Select extended load data, reconstructed multiply result, or the\nordinary EX/MEM result before capturing reg_q.alu_result. This existing\nMEM/WB field becomes the complete writeback value. Preserve all\nmetadata, memory behavior, reset, trap suppression and retained-data\nhold semantics, including the unchanged ALTOPS multiply path.\n'''},\n{'file':'rtl/wb_stage.sv','description':'''Drive w_data directly from the unified MEM/WB alu_result field,\nremoving the post-register mem_to_reg mux. Keep w_en and w_addr\nunchanged, and retain values for forwarding even when valid is clear.\n'''},\n{'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'''Retain all existing assertions and add directed mixed-producer tests\nfor load extensions, ALU/multiply/divide/link writeback, same-rd\npriority and dependent consumers during prolonged dmem holds. Check\nRVFI source/destination values and exact retirement under both buses'\nbackpressure, including forwarding from retained invalid WB data.\n'''}]}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(x,sort_keys=False,default_flow_style=False))\njsonschema.validate(x,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Updated and schema-validated',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
