{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_49","type":"agent_message","text":"The registered fetch queue also misses the timing target, and its extra recovery cycle fails the unchanged liveness check. I’m revising the hypothesis to replace ID/EX with a three-entry in-order instruction queue. Queued operands will track WB writes; this should absorb fetch stalls without adding an execution stage or putting a response mux ahead of decode. The final design remains unmeasured."}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_50", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-002-r12s1.yaml <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261001-002-r12s1\ntitle: \\\"Replace ID/EX with a WB-snooping three-entry in-order instruction queue\\\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 174.44 iter/sec at 73.790 MHz and 4,230,003 CoreMark\n  cycles. Structural attempts r10s1 and r11s1 demonstrated that fetching\n  during backend holds saves cycles: their one- and two-entry fetch buffers\n  took 4,022,833 and 3,981,061 cycles. But r11s1 reached only 71.714 MHz and\n  180.14 iter/sec, below the 182.46424 acceptance threshold. Selecting saved\n  versus live instructions before decode and register-file reads adds delay.\n  Move the elasticity across decode and operand capture instead: enlarge\n  the existing ID/EX storage, retaining its execution-stage boundary.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace the single ID/EX register with a three-entry, strictly in-order\n  instruction queue: one fixed head that is the EX instruction, and two\n  waiting entries. Fetch, decode and register-file reads operate on the\n  live instruction-bus response before enqueue. EX always reads the fixed\n  head register. This is one structural change to the decode/execute\n  boundary, with no extra pipeline stage, wider issue, cache or predictor\n  change. Two waiting slots provide the buffering capacity that r11s1\n  demonstrated was useful, without its raw-instruction selection mux in\n  front of decode, immediate generation and register-file reads.\n\n  Each entry holds the existing decoded instruction metadata, PC, immediate,\n  destination and source addresses, original instruction, saved prediction,\n  and two captured register operand values. These values represent the\n  latest committed register state, not speculative results from older\n  queued instructions. While an entry waits, snoop every actual WB write:\n  when wb_w_en && wb_w_addr\"'!=0 matches either saved source address, update\n  that source value from wb_w_data. All retiring writers are older than\n  every queued instruction because execution and retirement remain ordered.\n  This makes WB snooping sufficient without a rename map, producer tokens,\n  reservation stations or out-of-order execution. Suppressed writes from\n  illegal or misaligned instructions must never update saved operands.\n\n  Preserve the existing WB write-first behavior for incoming operands.\n  On a queue shift, apply the same-edge WB update to the payload being\n  promoted, rather than relying on a nonblocking assignment to its old\n  slot; otherwise the promoted instruction can miss the last older write.\n  Apply snoops during backend and instruction-bus stalls as well as normal\n  advancement. x0 stays zero. The head'\"'s committed operand snapshots may\n  update while held, but its instruction/control/PC stay stable. Once a\n  divider request launches, continue using its already captured operands\n  and metadata, independently of later queue operand snoops.\n\n  Keep EX's one-hot register/EX-MEM/MEM-WB operand selection, including\n  youngest-writer priority and PC/immediate selection. Forward only from\n  older executed producers in EX/MEM and MEM/WB. Compute the head's source\n  masks prospectively across the queue-advance edge, from the source tags\n  of the instruction that will occupy the head and the producer stages\n  that will exist after that edge. Register the masks before EX arithmetic;\n  do not add a source-index comparator followed by a mux to the EX critical\n  path. Unlike the current held ID/EX masks, refresh the masks when older\n  stages advance while the queue head waits. Preserve retained MEM/WB\n  data on genuine dmem holds, and distinguish architectural WB write enable\n  from eligibility of retained forwarding data. An older invalid pipeline\n  bubble must not override the saved committed operand value.\n\n  A queued consumer can become head behind an immediately preceding load\n  or multiply, but cannot issue until the required result is available.\n  Detect this from the head's source tags and the older late producer in\n  EX/MEM, not from raw io_imemData. Inject an inert EX/MEM bubble while the\n  producer advances into MEM/WB, then issue using the registered WB source\n  mask. Preserve the existing one-cycle dependency cost and the priority\n  of older memory backpressure. Conservative raw-source comparisons may\n  be retained; source-use filtering is not part of this hypothesis. Never\n  forward an effective address or multiply partial product as register\n  data. Gate divider launch, branch resolution, training, memory effects\n  and EX/MEM advancement with actual head issue and operand availability.\n\n  Fetch acceptance is independent of head issue: accept a ready response\n  whenever the queue has free space or the head leaves on the same edge.\n  Let dequeue mean that this head really transfers to EX/MEM, including a\n  completed divider transfer; starting a division does not dequeue it.\n  Dmem backpressure, a pending late operand, and an active divider retain\n  the head, while available tail space can still capture new instructions.\n  Full simultaneous dequeue/enqueue sustains one instruction per cycle.\n  If empty, an accepted response becomes the registered head on that edge,\n  exactly where the baseline would have captured ID/EX. Do not add a\n  separate registered IF/ID stage or combinationally execute a live response.\n\n  Use fixed head and waiting-slot registers, shift younger entries only\n  across a clock edge, and keep EX's head payload free of an output mux.\n  Separate wide payload capture/shift and operand-snoop enables from\n  occupancy and kill logic. It is safe to sample invalid incoming payloads,\n  but never overwrite a valid held payload or mark an unaccepted response\n  valid. Track occupancy explicitly and exercise all four occupancies.\n  The request PC advances only for an accepted bus response. Compute the\n  existing exact direct-target/agree prediction using that request PC and\n  response, and store predicted_taken at enqueue. Do not recompute saved\n  predictions as entries advance through the queue.\n\n  On EX recovery, the resolving head still advances exactly once; kill\n  every younger queued instruction and any same-edge wrong-path response,\n  install redirect_target in the request PC, and admit no younger side\n  effects. Recovery retains priority over an instruction-bus stall. Reset\n  empties the queue, cancels division and initializes state deterministically.\n  Preserve the decoder's default-illegal validation, all alignment traps,\n  existing arithmetic and ALTOPS formulas, nine-cycle division scheduling,\n  nret=1 ports, memory handshakes and monotonic exact-once channel-0 RVFI.\n  Keep the unchanged liveness bound; no formal-depth or wrapper changes.\n\n  This final instruction-queue design is an unmeasured hypothesis. It must\n  not inherit timing or correctness claims from other queue placements.\n  During hypothesis exploration, an isolated two-entry predecoded fetch\n  buffer preserved 3,981,061 cycles and passed eight architectural cosims,\n  5,000 independent FIFO transactions and all 53 unchanged formal checks,\n  but reached only 68.831 MHz. Rotating raw and decoded fetch queues also\n  missed the timing target. A separate registered two-entry fetch queue\n  took 4,068,383 cycles at 72.513 MHz and failed liveness despite passing\n  the CoreMark CRCs and architectural cosims. A four-entry registered fetch\n  queue took 4,030,415 cycles at 70.673 MHz. These results motivate replacing\n  ID/EX rather than adding storage ahead of it. None is the proposed design.\n\n  The six-percent forecast assumes recovering approximately the prior\n  two-slot buffering cycle benefit while keeping frequency near the\n  champion's 73.790 MHz. At 3,981,061 cycles the acceptance threshold needs\n  more than 72.640127 MHz. Principal risks are WB-snoop fanout and register\n  input muxing, incorrect prospective masks during partial advancement,\n  and unintended extra load-use or divider cycles. Measure actual cycles\n  and median Gowin Fmax; reject this hypothesis if the product does not\n  exceed 182.46424 iter/sec. Do not count RVFI-only optimizations as gains.\n\n  Validate the queue and snooping against an independent list-based model,\n  then run the unchanged complete formal suite, full RVFI cosimulation,\n  existing exact M-extension tests and canonical stalled CoreMark with\n  all five CRCs, UART validation, bounds and timing-marker checks. Explicitly\n  exercise the unchanged ready-bus retirement bound around branch recovery,\n  late-result dependencies and division. Update obsolete microarchitectural\n  test snapshots while retaining architectural and progress assertions.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: |\n      Define the queued decoded payload and three-entry occupancy state as\n      needed. Preserve the external and downstream RVFI payload contracts;\n      source snapshots and head forwarding masks have distinct roles.\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Replace the lone ID/EX register with a fixed execution head and two\n      waiting slots. Decode and read operands from the live bus response\n      before enqueue. Implement independent enqueue/dequeue, ordered shifts,\n      full simultaneous transfer, WB-snooped source snapshots including\n      promotion-edge writes, recovery and reset. Register prospective head\n      forwarding and ALU-source masks; expose occupancy and head readiness.\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Advance request PC on actual queue acceptance rather than backend\n      lockstep advancement. Preserve the existing predictor and save each\n      accepted response's prediction. Give recovery priority over acceptance\n      and bus stalls; keep live instruction data independent of recovery.\n  - file: rtl/forward_unit.sv\n    description: |\n      Generate prospective one-hot sources for the next execution head from\n      the actual post-edge older producer stages. Refresh choices when\n      those producers drain while the head waits; retain youngest-writer,\n      x0, trap-suppression and retained-WB semantics without EX tag compares.\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: |\n      Separate fetch queue capacity from execution readiness. Interlock the\n      queue head against older load/multiply results in EX/MEM and maintain\n      dmem and divider priorities. Hold head on a dependency while older\n      stages drain, and admit independent responses into free waiting slots.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Consume the registered queue head only when operands and downstream\n      capacity permit. Expose exact dequeue/advance qualification, keep a\n      division in the head through completion, suppress launch on unresolved\n      operands, and emit inert bubbles during dependency waits. Preserve\n      arithmetic, captured divider metadata and one-shot branch training.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Wire acceptance, capacity, head issue, late-result readiness, recovery,\n      actual WB snoops and prospective forwarding across the queue boundary.\n      Preserve EX/MEM, MEM/WB, memory ports and the exact nret=1 RVFI shape.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Add coverage for multiple queued same-rd writers, newest forwarding,\n      WB writes while a queue is held and on promotion edges, suppressed\n      writes from illegal/misaligned producers, x0, load/multiply consumers,\n      divider launch/completion, both bus stalls and wrong-path side effects.\n      Preserve full RVFI comparison and unchanged bounded-progress checks;\n      update only obsolete lockstep scheduling expectations.\n  - file: test/test_issue_queue.py\n    description: |\n      Independently model accepted decoded instructions and committed-register\n      snapshots. Check all occupancies, full dequeue/enqueue, long holds,\n      per-source WB snooping, shift-edge writes, exact PC/prediction order,\n      flush/reset and no entry duplication or loss. Verify masks against\n      the producer stages after each edge rather than mirroring RTL equations.\nYAML\npython3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-002-r12s1.yaml')\nd=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text())\njsonschema.validate(d,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nassert d['id']=='hyp-20261001-002-r12s1' and d['category']=='structural'\nassert type(d['expected_impact']['fitness_delta_pct']) is int\nassert -50<=d['expected_impact']['fitness_delta_pct']<=50\nprint('Schema valid:',p)\nprint('Final title:',d['title'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
