{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n d=json.loads(line)\n print(d['id'], d.get('category'), d.get('outcome'), d.get('fitness'), d.get('cycles'), d.get('fmax_mhz'), d.get('title'))\n if d.get('category')=='structural' and d['id'] not in ('hyp-20261001-002-r1s1','hyp-20261001-002-r12s1'):\n  h=d.get('hypothesis',{})\n  print((h.get('hypothesis','') if isinstance(h,dict) else h)[:4500])\n  if d.get('implementation_notes'): print('NOTES',d['implementation_notes'][:3000])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_10", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n d=json.loads(line)\n print(d['id'], d.get('category'), d.get('outcome'), d.get('fitness'), d.get('cycles'), d.get('fmax_mhz'), d.get('title'))\n if d.get('category')=='structural' and d['id'] not in ('hyp-20261001-002-r1s1','hyp-20261001-002-r12s1'):\n  h=d.get('hypothesis',{})\n  print((h.get('hypothesis','') if isinstance(h,dict) else h)[:4500])\n  if d.get('implementation_notes'): print('NOTES',d['implementation_notes'][:3000])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "baseline-bench-a1f7684 micro_opt improvement 12.12 4491485 5.442 Baseline retest for cores/bench\nhyp-20261001-001-r1s0 micro_opt regression 13.22 4491485 5.936 Shared radix-4 combinational divider for RV32M\nhyp-20261001-002-r1s1 structural improvement 84.6 4491485 37.999 Replace the combinational divider with a bounded iterative execution unit\nhyp-20261001-003-r1s2 predictor regression 13.32 4202246 5.596 Small bimodal predictor with immediate direct targets\nhyp-20261001-001-r2s0 micro_opt regression 97.11 4491485 43.615 Two parallel radix-4 digits per iterative divider cycle\nhyp-20261001-002-r2s1 structural improvement 103.54 4491485 46.505 Retime divider completion using first-nibble work during operand capture\nRepartition blocking DIV/REM into three phases: operand capture with\nfirst-nibble division, seven registered four-bit restoring iterations,\nand a final sign-correction/transfer phase. Keep the existing start,\nbusy, done, accept interface and exact nine-cycle instruction schedule.\nThe single architectural change is divider stage retiming: the full\nunsigned quotient and remainder must be registered before sign\ncorrection, with the cycle recovered by moving the first four quotient\nbits into launch. Retain the existing four-binary-step recurrence for\nall remaining digits.\n\nOn an accepted launch, compute the same 32-bit unsigned magnitudes A\nand D as today, preserving INT_MIN as 0x80000000. The first dividend\nnibble N=A[31:28] is at most 15. If any D[31:4] bit is set, its quotient\nnibble is necessarily zero and its remainder is N. Otherwise compute\nthe four-bit quotient q4 and remainder r4 of N divided by D[3:0] using\nfour explicitly small restoring steps starting with zero remainder.\nThese steps need only four-bit trial values and five-bit unsigned\nsubtract/borrow results; their intermediate trial values cannot exceed\nthe already consumed prefix of N. Use explicit narrow widths rather\nthan a full-width divider or synthesis of / and %. With D=0 this same\nsmall restoring network yields q4=15 and r4=N, matching\n…[46469 of 49541 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:f54d88c18875f49d]…\nnment checks, JALR recovery and one training event per\nadvancing branch. No buffered instruction may execute, train,\naccess data memory or retire before reaching its normal stage.\n\nPreserve load/multiply dependency bubbles, youngest-producer\nforwarding priority, retained MEM/WB forwarding data during data\nstalls, blocking divider operand capture and nine-cycle occupancy,\nexact real and ALTOPS arithmetic, decoder legality, misalignment\ntraps and all external memory handshakes. Do not chang\nhyp-20261001-003-r11s2 predictor regression 174.44 4230003 73.79 Register the alternate successor for direct-branch recovery\nhyp-20261001-001-r12s0 micro_opt regression 165.53 4230003 70.019 Predecode load versus arithmetic writeback forwarding before EX\nhyp-20261001-002-r12s1 structural regression 176.4 3976169 70.141 Replace ID/EX with a WB-snooping three-entry in-order instruction queue\nhyp-20261001-003-r12s2 predictor regression 175.88 4230003 74.396 Register both predictor feedback sinks without adding recovery latency\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"agent_message","text":"The last three structural attempts saved roughly 5–6% in CoreMark cycles, but their added muxing reduced Fmax enough to miss acceptance. I’m focusing on the recurring writeback-to-EX critical path: resolve older results into the operand registers before execution, reducing EX forwarding from three sources to two without adding a pipeline stage."}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_12", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml, json\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-002-r13s1.yaml')\nh={\n'id':'hyp-20261001-002-r13s1',\n'title':'Resolve older forwarding at operand capture and keep only the youngest EX bypass',\n'category':'structural',\n'motivation':'''The champion reaches 174.44 iter/sec at 73.790 MHz and 4,230,003 CoreMark cycles. Structural fetch and instruction queues in r10s1, r11s1 and r12s1 reduced cycles but added selection and control delay, missing the 4.6 percent acceptance margin. Recent timing repeatedly identifies the retained MEM/WB value through forwarding and the integer ALU as limiting. Move the older forwarding boundary into the existing ID/EX operand registers, reducing the live EX operand network without adding instruction latency.''',\n'hypothesis':'''Retarget the older-producer forwarding path into decode operand capture. At each accepted ID/EX edge, select the actual result of the current MEM instruction into each captured source value when that older producer is the newest matching writer. Otherwise capture the existing write-first register-file value. The MEM result is the same pre-register value that will enter MEM/WB on this edge, including load extraction, multiply reconstruction, normal ALU or jump data, and trap suppression. Do not use the current MEM/WB output for this prospective older producer: that would be one instruction too old.\n\nKeep only two live sources in EX: the saved operand and the younger EX/MEM arithmetic result. Compute and register the younger producer selections in decode, preserving youngest-writer priority and x0. A matching younger load or multiply must still create the existing dependency bubble and must never expose its address or partial product as data. Compute ALU PC/immediate selection with the registered source selections, keeping the source and ALU selection as parallel masked terms rather than serial muxes. Raw forwarded operands must still feed branches, stores, division and RVFI.\n\nPreserve retained operands on every backend hold. If ID/EX is held while its younger bypass source is about to disappear, first capture that source into the corresponding saved operand and clear its bypass selection; ordinary dmem stalls hold EX/MEM, while a launched division uses its separately captured original operands. Do not add a combinational EX result-to-ID operand path: the incoming older MEM result has already crossed EX/MEM. Preserve all current instruction scheduling, load/multiply dependency bubbles, blocking division latency, branch prediction and recovery, and memory-request behavior. Keep decode payload enables independent of late redirects, with validity-only squash.\n\nThis is a single structural change to operand storage and bypass placement. It is an unmeasured hypothesis, with an expected eight percent fitness improvement from higher Fmax at unchanged CoreMark cycles. Acceptance requires more than 182.46424 iter/sec, approximately 77.183 MHz if cycles remain 4,230,003. Main risks are a new memory-result-to-ID/EX critical path, extra operand input muxing and unchanged branch or divider paths limiting the gain. Measure median Gowin timing and actual bracketed cycles; do not infer success from cell count.\n\nPreserve exact nret=1 ports, channel-0 retirement order, decoder legality, traps, all real and ALTOPS arithmetic and identical division scheduling. Validate against existing unit tests and unchanged full formal and RVFI cosimulation, plus canonical stalled CoreMark CRCs, UART validation, bounds and timing markers. Add directed load/multiply/ALU-to-consumer cases, two older writers to the same destination, x0, false raw-source matches, simultaneous bus stalls, held branch/store operands and division launch/completion. No evaluator, wrapper, formal-depth or invariant changes.''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':8,'confidence':'low'},\n'changes':[\n{'file':'rtl/core_pkg.sv','description':'Replace three-source EX forwarding masks with saved-operand versus youngest-EX/MEM selection fields, and adapt parallel PC/immediate ALU source masks. Preserve all architectural and RVFI metadata.'},\n{'file':'rtl/mem_stage.sv','description':'Expose the ready pre-MEM/WB architectural result and write qualification, including extracted loads, reconstructed multiplies and trap suppression. Preserve retained MEM/WB and bus behavior.'},\n{'file':'rtl/forward_unit.sv','description':'Produce separate older-result capture selections and registered younger bypass selections with youngest-writer priority, x0 suppression and late-result eligibility.'},\n{'file':'rtl/id_stage.sv','description':'Resolve older MEM results into source operand registers on capture; retain or materialize younger bypass values correctly across holds. Keep payload capture independent of redirect squash and preserve accepted-instruction timing.'},\n{'file':'rtl/ex_stage.sv','description':'Use two-source raw operand and parallel ALU-source selection, removing the live MEM/WB source from ordinary EX arithmetic, branches, store data and divider launch. Preserve divider metadata and all advancement rules.'},\n{'file':'rtl/core.sv','description':'Connect pre-WB result capture and youngest bypass control without changing pipeline stage count, hazards, retirement or top-level nret=1 ports.'},\n{'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'Add directed newest-producer and older-result capture coverage for arithmetic, loads, every multiply variant, branches, stores, JALR and division under both bus stalls. Retain exact RVFI, arithmetic, progress and ready-bus timing checks.'}\n]}\np.parent.mkdir(parents=True,exist_ok=True)\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(h,sort_keys=False,width=85))\nimport jsonschema\njsonschema.validate(yaml.safe_load(p.read_text()),json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Wrote and schema-validated',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_12", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml, json\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-002-r13s1.yaml')\nh={\n'id':'hyp-20261001-002-r13s1',\n'title':'Resolve older forwarding at operand capture and keep only the youngest EX bypass',\n'category':'structural',\n'motivation':'''The champion reaches 174.44 iter/sec at 73.790 MHz and 4,230,003 CoreMark cycles. Structural fetch and instruction queues in r10s1, r11s1 and r12s1 reduced cycles but added selection and control delay, missing the 4.6 percent acceptance margin. Recent timing repeatedly identifies the retained MEM/WB value through forwarding and the integer ALU as limiting. Move the older forwarding boundary into the existing ID/EX operand registers, reducing the live EX operand network without adding instruction latency.''',\n'hypothesis':'''Retarget the older-producer forwarding path into decode operand capture. At each accepted ID/EX edge, select the actual result of the current MEM instruction into each captured source value when that older producer is the newest matching writer. Otherwise capture the existing write-first register-file value. The MEM result is the same pre-register value that will enter MEM/WB on this edge, including load extraction, multiply reconstruction, normal ALU or jump data, and trap suppression. Do not use the current MEM/WB output for this prospective older producer: that would be one instruction too old.\n\nKeep only two live sources in EX: the saved operand and the younger EX/MEM arithmetic result. Compute and register the younger producer selections in decode, preserving youngest-writer priority and x0. A matching younger load or multiply must still create the existing dependency bubble and must never expose its address or partial product as data. Compute ALU PC/immediate selection with the registered source selections, keeping the source and ALU selection as parallel masked terms rather than serial muxes. Raw forwarded operands must still feed branches, stores, division and RVFI.\n\nPreserve retained operands on every backend hold. If ID/EX is held while its younger bypass source is about to disappear, first capture that source into the corresponding saved operand and clear its bypass selection; ordinary dmem stalls hold EX/MEM, while a launched division uses its separately captured original operands. Do not add a combinational EX result-to-ID operand path: the incoming older MEM result has already crossed EX/MEM. Preserve all current instruction scheduling, load/multiply dependency bubbles, blocking division latency, branch prediction and recovery, and memory-request behavior. Keep decode payload enables independent of late redirects, with validity-only squash.\n\nThis is a single structural change to operand storage and bypass placement. It is an unmeasured hypothesis, with an expected eight percent fitness improvement from higher Fmax at unchanged CoreMark cycles. Acceptance requires more than 182.46424 iter/sec, approximately 77.183 MHz if cycles remain 4,230,003. Main risks are a new memory-result-to-ID/EX critical path, extra operand input muxing and unchanged branch or divider paths limiting the gain. Measure median Gowin timing and actual bracketed cycles; do not infer success from cell count.\n\nPreserve exact nret=1 ports, channel-0 retirement order, decoder legality, traps, all real and ALTOPS arithmetic and identical division scheduling. Validate against existing unit tests and unchanged full formal and RVFI cosimulation, plus canonical stalled CoreMark CRCs, UART validation, bounds and timing markers. Add directed load/multiply/ALU-to-consumer cases, two older writers to the same destination, x0, false raw-source matches, simultaneous bus stalls, held branch/store operands and division launch/completion. No evaluator, wrapper, formal-depth or invariant changes.''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':8,'confidence':'low'},\n'changes':[\n{'file':'rtl/core_pkg.sv','description':'Replace three-source EX forwarding masks with saved-operand versus youngest-EX/MEM selection fields, and adapt parallel PC/immediate ALU source masks. Preserve all architectural and RVFI metadata.'},\n{'file':'rtl/mem_stage.sv','description':'Expose the ready pre-MEM/WB architectural result and write qualification, including extracted loads, reconstructed multiplies and trap suppression. Preserve retained MEM/WB and bus behavior.'},\n{'file':'rtl/forward_unit.sv','description':'Produce separate older-result capture selections and registered younger bypass selections with youngest-writer priority, x0 suppression and late-result eligibility.'},\n{'file':'rtl/id_stage.sv','description':'Resolve older MEM results into source operand registers on capture; retain or materialize younger bypass values correctly across holds. Keep payload capture independent of redirect squash and preserve accepted-instruction timing.'},\n{'file':'rtl/ex_stage.sv','description':'Use two-source raw operand and parallel ALU-source selection, removing the live MEM/WB source from ordinary EX arithmetic, branches, store data and divider launch. Preserve divider metadata and all advancement rules.'},\n{'file':'rtl/core.sv','description':'Connect pre-WB result capture and youngest bypass control without changing pipeline stage count, hazards, retirement or top-level nret=1 ports.'},\n{'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'Add directed newest-producer and older-result capture coverage for arithmetic, loads, every multiply variant, branches, stores, JALR and division under both bus stalls. Retain exact RVFI, arithmetic, progress and ready-bus timing checks.'}\n]}\np.parent.mkdir(parents=True,exist_ok=True)\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(h,sort_keys=False,width=85))\nimport jsonschema\njsonschema.validate(yaml.safe_load(p.read_text()),json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Wrote and schema-validated',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "Wrote and schema-validated cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-002-r13s1.yaml\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
