ew address equals the existing\nALU_ADD result exactly. Nonmemory instructions issue no memory\nrequest; their otherwise unused address bus value may change.\nPreserve full address width; never exploit benchmark address\nlimits to truncate or alias accesses.\n\neffective_addr belongs to the complete EX/MEM payload. Hold it\nwith the memory operation during dmem backpressure, reset it with\nthe bundle and clear it when EX/MEM becomes a bubble. The division\nmetadata bundle is already cleared at launch, so division transfer\nproduces zero in this unused field and never issues a memory\noperation. Preser\n…[12187 of 15259 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:5a8407f2ddb091a1]…\non policy.\n\nValidate the mask invariant against the old forwarding formula and test\narithmetic/load/multiply/divide producers, both consumer operands, x0,\nsame-rd younger priority, immediate ALU operations and AUIPC. Specifically\nalternate load and arithmetic producers across EX/MEM and MEM/WB so a\none-stage error in the sampled mem_to_reg is detected. Exercise a WB\nproducer followed by an unrelated stalled memory instruction and a held\ndependent branch/store/ALU consumer; check retained data after WB valid\nclears. Include illegal instructions and misaligned memory/jump traps.\nRetain all current assertions and cycle checks, standard ALTOPS formal,\nreal arithmetic unit tests and RVFI cosimulation with both buses stalled.\nCanonical CoreMark should remain at 4,230,003 marker-bracketed cycles for\nthe same stall sequence, with every CRC/UART/bounds check intact. Score\nall three Gowin placement options; acceptance requires fitness strictly\nabove 182.46424 iterations/sec, approximately 77.183 MHz at those cycles.\n\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
