{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_81", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-002-r15s1.yaml')\nd=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text())\nd['title']='Retimed operand capture behind a one-entry elastic fetch slot'\nd['motivation']='''Structural r10s1 saved 207,170 CoreMark cycles with a one-entry fetch\nbuffer, but its 72.497 MHz median missed the acceptance margin. Larger\nbuffers and moving selection past decode did not resolve the timing cost.\nThe champion repeatedly exposes older WB load forwarding through EX\narithmetic and branch recovery as limiting paths. Retiming the older\nbypass and PC/immediate selection into operand capture can recover the\nclock margin needed to make a small elastic frontend worthwhile. This\nproposal restructures the fetch/decode/execute boundary while retaining\nall backend arithmetic, predictor behavior and retirement width.\n'''\nd['hypothesis']='''Build one elastic operand-admission path: one fall-through instruction\nslot ahead of decode, with completed older results and PC/immediate\nselection captured before the existing ID/EX boundary. EX then selects\nbetween a prepared operand register and the immediately older EX/MEM\nresult. Keep single issue, single retirement, the existing load/multiply\nbubble, and nine-cycle division. Do not add an execution stage.\n\nThe fetch slot holds only PC, instruction and the saved predicted_taken\nbit. Decode sees the slot when occupied, otherwise the live response.\nSelect payload solely from registered occupancy. Hazards and prospective\nforwarding compare the selected instruction's raw source fields, never\nthe independently advancing physical bus response. Logical availability\nis head_valid || io_imemReady. Preserve conservative raw-source interlocks;\nsource-use filtering and late store-data forwarding are not included.\n\nOutside reset/recovery, with stall denoting the selected decode head's\nordinary hold condition, accept a physical response when\nio_imemReady && (\"'!head_valid || !stall). Advance the request PC and its\nexisting prediction only on acceptance. On an empty consumed response,\npass directly to ID/EX without storing a second copy. On an empty held\nresponse, fill the slot. Consuming an occupied slot replaces it with an\naccepted response or empties it; a full held slot remains stable.\nSeparate wide payload capture from validity: head_load = head_valid XOR\nstall, and when head_load capture {request_pc, imem_data, prediction}.\nThis can sample invalid responses harmlessly. On a normal consuming edge\nhead_valid_next = head_valid && fetch_accept; on a held edge an accepted\nresponse sets head_valid, otherwise retain it. Reset clears occupancy\nand payload; redirect overrides the PC hold and clears slot/ID validity.\nWrong-path payload captures cannot execute. Predictions are saved on\nfetch acceptance rather than recomputed when dequeued.\n\nExpose the final pre-register MEM writeback value and valid/trap-qualified\nproducer tag. The value is the same load, reconstructed multiply or\nnormal result that WB will write after this edge. Implement the value\nselection with mutually exclusive masked sources: load_data when\nmem_to_reg, multiply_result when !mem_to_reg && is_multiply, otherwise\nin.alu_result. Keep real and ALTOPS multiplication selection consistent.\nProducer qualification is in.valid && trap_adjusted_reg_write && rd!=0;\na misaligned load must not override an older architectural writer.\n\nFor each decoded source, set capture_rs? when this MEM producer matches.\nCapture raw rs?_val = capture_rs? ? mem_capture_data : rf_rs?_data.\nThe register file keeps its existing WB write-first behavior, so the\nfallback includes an already-retiring producer. In addition capture two\nprepared ALU operands independently from these same original inputs:\n  alu_a_val = (PC & mask(is_auipc))\n            | (mem_capture_data & mask(!is_auipc && capture_rs1))\n            | (rf_rs1_data & mask(!is_auipc && !capture_rs1));\n  alu_b_val = (imm & mask(alu_src))\n            | (mem_capture_data & mask(!alu_src && capture_rs2))\n            | (rf_rs2_data & mask(!alu_src && !capture_rs2)).\nHere mask(c) replicates c to 32 bits. These exclusive capture terms avoid\nserial older-forwarding and ALU-source priority muxes. Keep raw values\nseparately for branches, stores, divider launch and RVFI. Use normal\nID/EX payload enables, independent of recovery; flush validity separately.\n\nRegister the prospective match against the current EX producer, using\nexisting trap-qualified next_reg_write and excluding multiply as today.\nFor each raw EX operand, choose EX/MEM.alu_result on this younger match,\notherwise the corrected captured rs?_val. For ALU operands, enable that\nsame younger bypass only when the operand is not PC/immediate, otherwise\nuse alu_a_val/alu_b_val. Each EX data selection is a two-input mux.\nRemove the MEM/WB data source from all EX raw and ALU operand muxes.\nThe youngest producer retains priority even when both older stages write\nthe same register. Do not bypass a load address or multiply partials:\nthe unchanged late-result interlock ensures the next advancing consumer\ncaptures the completed MEM result instead. x0 remains hardwired zero.\n\nA real data-memory hold freezes the consumer operand registers and masks\ntogether, with EX/MEM also held. Unready load data must never be captured\nas an accepted consumer. Keep MEM/WB retained-data behavior; no removal\nof its hold logic is required. Division captures fully resolved operands\nonce and retains them while older stages drain. Its completion and the\nfollowing consumer keep the existing forwarding/advancement schedule.\nInvalid stale producer payloads must not qualify for the new capture\nbypass. Preserve misalignment suppression and the fallback value when a\nwould-be writer traps. Keep raw RVFI source values even for fields that\nare not architectural execution operands.\n\nKeep nret=1 and every external port unchanged. Preserve decoder legality,\nall real/ALTOPS arithmetic, memory request semantics, exact-once ordered\nretirement, reset cancellation, wrong-path suppression and the unchanged\nformal liveness bound. Predictor, divider internals, multiplier latency,\nregister-file write timing and memory architecture remain unchanged.\nThis is a structural timing/buffering hypothesis; RVFI-only work cannot\nsupply the proposed fitness improvement.\n\nThe final masked-capture prototype passed eight full-RVFI architectural\ncosims: selftest and three random programs, each with and without both\nbus stalls. Canonical stalled CoreMark took 4,022,833 bracketed cycles,\nwith all CRCs, validation text and bounds checks passing. Vendor timing\nand complete formal qualification for this exact refinement are pending\nat this draft. A serial-capture version of the same one-slot design\nmeasured 70.826/73.307/73.307 MHz, median 73.307 MHz and 182.22730 iter/sec:\n4.464 percent above 174.44, narrowly below the required 4.6 percent.\nThat is supporting evidence for the architecture, not a qualifying score.\nThe masked capture is intended to remove the remaining selection depth;\ndo not inherit the earlier variant'\"'s timing or formal result.\n\nMeasure median Gowin Fmax over all three placement options times measured\niterations/cycle. At 4,022,833 cycles, acceptance needs more than\n73.402317 MHz and 182.46424 iter/sec. The five-percent forecast has low\nconfidence until the exact implementation is measured. Main risks are\nMEM result-to-ID capture delay, decode/head selection fanout, and the\nunchanged first divider recurrence becoming the frequency limit.\nRun the unchanged complete formal suite, real arithmetic tests and full\narchitectural cosim. Add directed tests of every fetch-slot transition,\nsaved predictions, reset/recovery while full, older WB and MEM writers,\nyounger same-rd priority, late results, trap-suppressed producers and\nheld operands. Preserve architectural checks when adapting internal\nforwarding-shape and fetch-scheduling assertions.\n'''\nd['expected_impact']={'fitness_delta_pct':5,'confidence':'low'}\nd['changes']=[\n{'file':'rtl/if_stage.sv','description':'Add one fall-through PC/instruction/prediction slot with independent response acceptance, logical head availability, and payload capture separated from occupancy/recovery. Preserve the existing predictor and no extra latency when empty.'},\n{'file':'rtl/mem_stage.sv','description':'Expose the final pre-register writeback value with exclusive masked load/multiply/normal-result selection and valid, nonzero, trap-qualified producer information. Preserve existing memory, MEM/WB hold and RVFI semantics.'},\n{'file':'rtl/id_stage.sv','description':'Capture matching older MEM values into raw operand registers and parallel prepared ALU operand registers with PC/immediate selection. Keep prospective younger bypass flags and ordinary payload hold/validity-only flush behavior.'},\n{'file':'rtl/forward_unit.sv','description':'Split prospective forwarding into MEM-to-ID operand capture and the remaining younger EX/MEM bypass. Preserve writer age priority and x0; remove the older EX forwarding choice.'},\n{'file':'rtl/ex_stage.sv','description':'Use two-input raw and ALU operand muxes between prepared ID/EX values and the youngest EX/MEM result. Remove WB data from EX selection without changing arithmetic, division scheduling, branch behavior or RVFI operands.'},\n{'file':'rtl/core_pkg.sv','description':'Add prepared ALU operand fields and represent the reduced execution bypass selections while retaining raw operands and architectural metadata.'},\n{'file':'rtl/core.sv','description':'Wire logical fetch availability and selected-head tags to existing hazards/forwarding; connect qualified MEM result capture to ID and remove WB-to-EX data wiring. Preserve RF write-first behavior and exact nret=1 IO.'},\n{'file':'rtl/hazard_unit.sv','description':'Interpret instruction readiness as selected-head availability. Keep existing conservative raw-source late-result interlocks and all memory/divider/redirect priorities; document the independently advancing fetch stream.'},\n{'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'Check every producer/consumer class, same-rd priority, x0, WB write-first, misaligned-load suppression and held operands with both bus stalls. Preserve exact RVFI source/order/progress/arithmetic coverage while adapting internal mux and frontend scheduling assertions.'},\n{'file':'test/test_if_queue.py','description':'Check the one-slot frontend against an independent accepted-instruction FIFO, including bypass, fill, consume/refill, full hold, unready responses, saved prediction, reset and redirect. Verify no loss, duplication or wrong-path side effects.'}\n]\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(d,sort_keys=False,width=90,default_style=None))\njsonschema.validate(yaml.safe_load(p.read_text()),json.load(open('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json')))\nprint('Updated and schema valid:',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_81", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-002-r15s1.yaml')\nd=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text())\nd['title']='Retimed operand capture behind a one-entry elastic fetch slot'\nd['motivation']='''Structural r10s1 saved 207,170 CoreMark cycles with a one-entry fetch\nbuffer, but its 72.497 MHz median missed the acceptance margin. Larger\nbuffers and moving selection past decode did not resolve the timing cost.\nThe champion repeatedly exposes older WB load forwarding through EX\narithmetic and branch recovery as limiting paths. Retiming the older\nbypass and PC/immediate selection into operand capture can recover the\nclock margin needed to make a small elastic frontend worthwhile. This\nproposal restructures the fetch/decode/execute boundary while retaining\nall backend arithmetic, predictor behavior and retirement width.\n'''\nd['hypothesis']='''Build one elastic operand-admission path: one fall-through instruction\nslot ahead of decode, with completed older results and PC/immediate\nselection captured before the existing ID/EX boundary. EX then selects\nbetween a prepared operand register and the immediately older EX/MEM\nresult. Keep single issue, single retirement, the existing load/multiply\nbubble, and nine-cycle division. Do not add an execution stage.\n\nThe fetch slot holds only PC, instruction and the saved predicted_taken\nbit. Decode sees the slot when occupied, otherwise the live response.\nSelect payload solely from registered occupancy. Hazards and prospective\nforwarding compare the selected instruction's raw source fields, never\nthe independently advancing physical bus response. Logical availability\nis head_valid || io_imemReady. Preserve conservative raw-source interlocks;\nsource-use filtering and late store-data forwarding are not included.\n\nOutside reset/recovery, with stall denoting the selected decode head's\nordinary hold condition, accept a physical response when\nio_imemReady && (\"'!head_valid || !stall). Advance the request PC and its\nexisting prediction only on acceptance. On an empty consumed response,\npass directly to ID/EX without storing a second copy. On an empty held\nresponse, fill the slot. Consuming an occupied slot replaces it with an\naccepted response or empties it; a full held slot remains stable.\nSeparate wide payload capture from validity: head_load = head_valid XOR\nstall, and when head_load capture {request_pc, imem_data, prediction}.\nThis can sample invalid responses harmlessly. On a normal consuming edge\nhead_valid_next = head_valid && fetch_accept; on a held edge an accepted\nresponse sets head_valid, otherwise retain it. Reset clears occupancy\nand payload; redirect overrides the PC hold and clears slot/ID validity.\nWrong-path payload captures cannot execute. Predictions are saved on\nfetch acceptance rather than recomputed when dequeued.\n\nExpose the final pre-register MEM writeback value and valid/trap-qualified\nproducer tag. The value is the same load, reconstructed multiply or\nnormal result that WB will write after this edge. Implement the value\nselection with mutually exclusive masked sources: load_data when\nmem_to_reg, multiply_result when !mem_to_reg && is_multiply, otherwise\nin.alu_result. Keep real and ALTOPS multiplication selection consistent.\nProducer qualification is in.valid && trap_adjusted_reg_write && rd!=0;\na misaligned load must not override an older architectural writer.\n\nFor each decoded source, set capture_rs? when this MEM producer matches.\nCapture raw rs?_val = capture_rs? ? mem_capture_data : rf_rs?_data.\nThe register file keeps its existing WB write-first behavior, so the\nfallback includes an already-retiring producer. In addition capture two\nprepared ALU operands independently from these same original inputs:\n  alu_a_val = (PC & mask(is_auipc))\n            | (mem_capture_data & mask(!is_auipc && capture_rs1))\n            | (rf_rs1_data & mask(!is_auipc && !capture_rs1));\n  alu_b_val = (imm & mask(alu_src))\n            | (mem_capture_data & mask(!alu_src && capture_rs2))\n            | (rf_rs2_data & mask(!alu_src && !capture_rs2)).\nHere mask(c) replicates c to 32 bits. These exclusive capture terms avoid\nserial older-forwarding and ALU-source priority muxes. Keep raw values\nseparately for branches, stores, divider launch and RVFI. Use normal\nID/EX payload enables, independent of recovery; flush validity separately.\n\nRegister the prospective match against the current EX producer, using\nexisting trap-qualified next_reg_write and excluding multiply as today.\nFor each raw EX operand, choose EX/MEM.alu_result on this younger match,\notherwise the corrected captured rs?_val. For ALU operands, enable that\nsame younger bypass only when the operand is not PC/immediate, otherwise\nuse alu_a_val/alu_b_val. Each EX data selection is a two-input mux.\nRemove the MEM/WB data source from all EX raw and ALU operand muxes.\nThe youngest producer retains priority even when both older stages write\nthe same register. Do not bypass a load address or multiply partials:\nthe unchanged late-result interlock ensures the next advancing consumer\ncaptures the completed MEM result instead. x0 remains hardwired zero.\n\nA real data-memory hold freezes the consumer operand registers and masks\ntogether, with EX/MEM also held. Unready load data must never be captured\nas an accepted consumer. Keep MEM/WB retained-data behavior; no removal\nof its hold logic is required. Division captures fully resolved operands\nonce and retains them while older stages drain. Its completion and the\nfollowing consumer keep the existing forwarding/advancement schedule.\nInvalid stale producer payloads must not qualify for the new capture\nbypass. Preserve misalignment suppression and the fallback value when a\nwould-be writer traps. Keep raw RVFI source values even for fields that\nare not architectural execution operands.\n\nKeep nret=1 and every external port unchanged. Preserve decoder legality,\nall real/ALTOPS arithmetic, memory request semantics, exact-once ordered\nretirement, reset cancellation, wrong-path suppression and the unchanged\nformal liveness bound. Predictor, divider internals, multiplier latency,\nregister-file write timing and memory architecture remain unchanged.\nThis is a structural timing/buffering hypothesis; RVFI-only work cannot\nsupply the proposed fitness improvement.\n\nThe final masked-capture prototype passed eight full-RVFI architectural\ncosims: selftest and three random programs, each with and without both\nbus stalls. Canonical stalled CoreMark took 4,022,833 bracketed cycles,\nwith all CRCs, validation text and bounds checks passing. Vendor timing\nand complete formal qualification for this exact refinement are pending\nat this draft. A serial-capture version of the same one-slot design\nmeasured 70.826/73.307/73.307 MHz, median 73.307 MHz and 182.22730 iter/sec:\n4.464 percent above 174.44, narrowly below the required 4.6 percent.\nThat is supporting evidence for the architecture, not a qualifying score.\nThe masked capture is intended to remove the remaining selection depth;\ndo not inherit the earlier variant'\"'s timing or formal result.\n\nMeasure median Gowin Fmax over all three placement options times measured\niterations/cycle. At 4,022,833 cycles, acceptance needs more than\n73.402317 MHz and 182.46424 iter/sec. The five-percent forecast has low\nconfidence until the exact implementation is measured. Main risks are\nMEM result-to-ID capture delay, decode/head selection fanout, and the\nunchanged first divider recurrence becoming the frequency limit.\nRun the unchanged complete formal suite, real arithmetic tests and full\narchitectural cosim. Add directed tests of every fetch-slot transition,\nsaved predictions, reset/recovery while full, older WB and MEM writers,\nyounger same-rd priority, late results, trap-suppressed producers and\nheld operands. Preserve architectural checks when adapting internal\nforwarding-shape and fetch-scheduling assertions.\n'''\nd['expected_impact']={'fitness_delta_pct':5,'confidence':'low'}\nd['changes']=[\n{'file':'rtl/if_stage.sv','description':'Add one fall-through PC/instruction/prediction slot with independent response acceptance, logical head availability, and payload capture separated from occupancy/recovery. Preserve the existing predictor and no extra latency when empty.'},\n{'file':'rtl/mem_stage.sv','description':'Expose the final pre-register writeback value with exclusive masked load/multiply/normal-result selection and valid, nonzero, trap-qualified producer information. Preserve existing memory, MEM/WB hold and RVFI semantics.'},\n{'file':'rtl/id_stage.sv','description':'Capture matching older MEM values into raw operand registers and parallel prepared ALU operand registers with PC/immediate selection. Keep prospective younger bypass flags and ordinary payload hold/validity-only flush behavior.'},\n{'file':'rtl/forward_unit.sv','description':'Split prospective forwarding into MEM-to-ID operand capture and the remaining younger EX/MEM bypass. Preserve writer age priority and x0; remove the older EX forwarding choice.'},\n{'file':'rtl/ex_stage.sv','description':'Use two-input raw and ALU operand muxes between prepared ID/EX values and the youngest EX/MEM result. Remove WB data from EX selection without changing arithmetic, division scheduling, branch behavior or RVFI operands.'},\n{'file':'rtl/core_pkg.sv','description':'Add prepared ALU operand fields and represent the reduced execution bypass selections while retaining raw operands and architectural metadata.'},\n{'file':'rtl/core.sv','description':'Wire logical fetch availability and selected-head tags to existing hazards/forwarding; connect qualified MEM result capture to ID and remove WB-to-EX data wiring. Preserve RF write-first behavior and exact nret=1 IO.'},\n{'file':'rtl/hazard_unit.sv','description':'Interpret instruction readiness as selected-head availability. Keep existing conservative raw-source late-result interlocks and all memory/divider/redirect priorities; document the independently advancing fetch stream.'},\n{'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'Check every producer/consumer class, same-rd priority, x0, WB write-first, misaligned-load suppression and held operands with both bus stalls. Preserve exact RVFI source/order/progress/arithmetic coverage while adapting internal mux and frontend scheduling assertions.'},\n{'file':'test/test_if_queue.py','description':'Check the one-slot frontend against an independent accepted-instruction FIFO, including bypass, fill, consume/refill, full hold, unready responses, saved prediction, reset and redirect. Verify no loss, duplication or wrong-path side effects.'}\n]\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(d,sort_keys=False,width=90,default_style=None))\njsonschema.validate(yaml.safe_load(p.read_text()),json.load(open('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json')))\nprint('Updated and schema valid:',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "Updated and schema valid: cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-002-r15s1.yaml\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
