{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_13", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-002-r2s1.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261001-002-r2s1\ntitle: \"Restructure the iterative divider around two parallel radix-4 digit selectors\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion achieves fitness 84.60 at 37.999 MHz. Its reported worst\n  path starts at u_ex/u_div/remainder_q and spans 30.772 ns and 138 cells.\n  The successful structural attempt hyp-20261001-002-r1s1 removed full\n  combinational division, but each registered iteration still cascades\n  four binary restoring subtract/select steps followed by final-result\n  sign correction. This remaining recurrence limits the clock for every\n  instruction. The recorded CoreMark cycle count is 4,491,485 for both\n  the original baseline and this champion, making frequency the clearest\n  measured opportunity in this workload.\n\n  LESSONS.md confirms the benefit of bounded iterative division. The\n  earlier micro_opt hyp-20261001-001-r1s0 used 16 combinational radix-4\n  digit stages and reached only 5.936 MHz; its lesson is that halving\n  digit depth without registering the recurrence leaves a long path.\n  Apply parallel digit selection inside the champion's existing eight\n  registered iterations. Keep the nine-cycle execution bound required\n  by the unchanged ready-bus retirement-gap and formal liveness checks.\nhypothesis: |\n  Reorganize the shared blocking DIV/REM execution unit as an eight-cycle\n  recurrence with two true radix-4 quotient-digit selectors per cycle.\n  Each selector computes three trial subtractions in parallel and emits\n  two quotient bits. Thus four quotient bits still complete per cycle,\n  but the recurrence contains two serial carry-propagating subtraction\n  layers instead of four. This is one structural change to the divider's\n  registered arithmetic organization; its launch/completion protoco