{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_87","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport shutil\np=Path('cores/bench/test/test_div_unit.py');shutil.copyfile(p,'/srv/hwebench/tmp/867d4c2f/44967f25/control_flow_backup/test_div_unit.py')\ns=p.read_text().replace('PREFIX_BITS = (2, 8,','PREFIX_BITS = (3, 8,').replace('two_bit_launch_and_phase_boundaries','three_bit_launch_and_phase_boundaries').replace('for prefix in range(4):','for prefix in range(8):').replace('for divisor in range(6):','for divisor in range(10):').replace('(0, 1, 0x15555555, 0x3fffffff)','(0, 1, 0x0aaaaaaa, 0x1fffffff)').replace('(prefix << 30) | tail','(prefix << 29) | tail')\np.write_text(s)\nshutil.copyfile('/srv/hwebench/tmp/867d4c2f/44967f25/div35_rtl/div_unit.sv','cores/bench/rtl/div_unit.sv')\nPY\npytest -q cores/bench/test/test_div_unit.py cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py > /srv/hwebench/tmp/867d4c2f/44967f25/div35_tests.log 2>&1\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_88", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-002-r8s1.yaml')\np.write_text('''id: hyp-20261001-002-r8s1\ntitle: \\\"Rebalance divider launch and first recurrence to three plus five bits\\\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 174.44 at 73.790 MHz and 4,230,003 bracketed\n  CoreMark cycles. Prior structural improvements removed shared divider\n  feedback and pipelined multiplication. The current divider consumes\n  two bits during launch, then six in its first registered recurrence;\n  that first recurrence cascades three radix-4 digits plus local 3D\n  preparation. Fresh vendor experiments repeatedly expose divisor_q to\n  remainder_bank[1] among the limiting paths after other cones improve.\n  Rebalance work across that existing register boundary without adding\n  execution cycles or disturbing the successful dedicated phase banks.\nhypothesis: |\n  Change only the divider's internal bit partition from\n  2+6+4+4+4+4+4+4 to 3+5+4+4+4+4+4+4. Preserve all eight quotient/remainder\n  banks, seven recurrence edges, the final sign/transfer phase and exact\n  nine-cycle EX occupancy. This is a structural repartition of launch\n  versus first-phase arithmetic; leave the other pipeline stages,\n  branch predictor, multiplier, forwarding and memory system unchanged.\n\n  On accepted launch normalize operands exactly as today. Let A and D\n  be the unsigned magnitudes and N=A[31:29]. If any D[31:3] bit is set,\n  initialize q3=0 and r3=N. Otherwise compute three explicitly three-bit\n  restoring steps using D[2:0], a four-bit subtract/borrow result and an\n  initially zero remainder. Consume A[31], A[30], A[29] in order, append\n  each non-borrow decision and select the trial or difference. D=0 must\n  naturally produce q3=7 and r3=N. Capture Q[0]={A[28:0],q3} and\n  R[0]={29'b0,r3}, together with existing divisor, sign and operation\n  flags. Do not move full-width 3D preparation onto the launch path.\n\n  For phase zero set C=3, N=5, P=8 and W=10. Begin with one binary\n  restoring step, followed by the existing two parallel-trial radix-4\n  digits. Form the W-bit first trial by zero-extending\n  {R[0][2:0],Q[0][31]}, subtract zero-extended d1 using W+1 bits, select\n  by borrow, and shift the quotient by one. Feed this intermediate\n  quotient/remainder into two ordinary D/2D/3D radix-4 steps. Preserve\n  the existing 10-bit local d3 and the phase-wide D>=256 bypass; the\n  bypass shifts Q[0] by five and appends its five high bits to R[0].\n  Later phases retain their existing C,N,P,W values and recurrence.\n  The consumed-prefix sequence becomes 3,8,12,16,20,24,28,32.\n\n  Keep full 34-bit triple_divisor_q preparation parallel with phase\n  zero, so phases one through six still use registered exact 3D. Keep\n  the final registered unsigned magnitude, sign correction, divisor-zero\n  quotient override, INT_MIN/-1 behavior and completion retention.\n  Neither operand forwarding changes while busy nor completion stalls\n  may alter the captured request. Reset cancels every active operation.\n  Preserve original RVFI operands and the unmodified real/ALTOPS start,\n  busy, done and accept schedule; ALTOPS formulas remain unchanged.\n  Preserve nret=1, legality checks, memory handshakes, exact-once\n  retirement and the frozen formal retirement-progress bound.\n\n  The intended tradeoff moves one small restoring bit to launch and\n  replaces a radix-4 digit in the longest first recurrence with one\n  binary step. A temporary RTL prototype measured Gowin placement\n  options 0/1/2 at 68.454,74.223,74.223 MHz: median 74.223 MHz.\n  Stalled CoreMark retained exactly 4,230,003 bracketed cycles with\n  canonical CRCs, validation text and no out-of-bounds accesses.\n  Preliminary fitness is 175.46796, only 0.59 percent above 174.44.\n  This does not clear the required 182.46424 acceptance threshold;\n  the one-percent forecast is deliberately modest and is not a claim\n  of a qualifying replacement. Reproduce the implementation and vendor\n  measurement before drawing conclusions from this small difference.\n\n  Retain all existing exact arithmetic, prefix-state, reset, operand\n  mutation and completion-backpressure tests. Update their expected\n  prefix counts to the new schedule and exhaust all eight launch\n  prefixes against small divisors, especially D=7,8,9 and D=0. Check\n  first-phase binary/radix-4 boundaries and all later prefix bounds\n  with signed and unsigned full-width operands. Run unchanged formal,\n  real-arithmetic divider tests, pipeline/cosim gates and canonical\n  stalled CoreMark. The principal risks are added launch delay and\n  ordinary ALU or branch recovery limiting Fmax after the first divider\n  phase is shortened. No arithmetic-latency or correctness relaxation\n  is part of this hypothesis.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 1\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/div_unit.sv\n    description: |\n      Consume three narrow restoring bits at launch and five bits in\n      phase zero using one binary step then two radix-4 digits. Adjust\n      bank-zero packing and phase-zero C/N constants, retaining all\n      later phase banks, exact multiple widths, large-divisor bypasses,\n      sign/exception handling and nine-cycle real/ALTOPS scheduling.\n  - file: test/test_div_unit.py\n    description: |\n      Preserve arithmetic and protocol assertions, update expected\n      consumed-prefix counts to 3,8,12,16,20,24,28,32, and expand launch\n      coverage to eight prefixes with the D=8 boundary. Check mixed\n      binary/radix-4 recurrence states against independent division.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Preserve existing divider dependency, bus-stall, retirement and\n      progress checks; add directed launch-boundary operands through\n      each forwarding source and long operations after branch recovery.\n''')\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\njsonschema.validate(yaml.safe_load(p.read_text()),json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Updated and schema-validated divider hypothesis')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
