{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_71", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-002-r9s1.yaml')\nd={\n'id':'hyp-20261001-002-r9s1',\n'title':'Carry divisor state through the dedicated division phase banks',\n'category':'structural',\n'motivation':'''The champion scores 174.44 iter/sec at 73.790 MHz with 4,230,003 bracketed CoreMark cycles. Prior structural work successfully distributed quotient/remainder state across dedicated phase banks (r6s1), but all seven arithmetic phases still read one divisor register and most also read one shared 3D register. Fresh isolated timing experiments expose divisor_q to remainder_bank[1] and remainder_bank[7] paths after other paths are shortened. Retiming branch recovery or writeback selection did not improve fitness. Complete the divider's spatial dataflow by carrying its invariant arithmetic inputs with the partial result, without changing its nine-cycle schedule.''',\n'hypothesis':'''Replace shared divisor fanout with statically connected phase-local divisor and triple-divisor banks. Keep the existing two-bit launch, seven 6/4/4/4/4/4/4-bit recurrence phases, eight quotient/remainder banks, final sign correction and completion retention. This is one structural change to the divider's registered operand distribution; arithmetic, pipeline issue, forwarding, branch prediction, memory and retirement timing remain unchanged.\n\nUse divisor_bank[0:6], each 32 bits, and triple_divisor_bank[1:6], each 34 bits. Accepted launch stores the unsigned magnitude D in divisor_bank[0], alongside the existing initial Q/R bank and captured flags. Phase p reads only divisor_bank[p], its existing predecessor Q/R bank and, for p>=1, triple_divisor_bank[p]. Preserve every prefix width, exact D/2D/3D subtraction, borrow-based one-hot selection and large-divisor bypass.\n\nOn the enabled edge for phase zero, compute full 34-bit 3D from divisor_bank[0] in parallel with the existing narrow six-bit recurrence. Capture D and full 3D into divisor_bank[1] and triple_divisor_bank[1]. Phase zero continues using its own narrow local 3D expression; do not move full-width multiple generation onto launch or its dependent digit chain. On each enabled phase p=1..5, pass D and full 3D from bank p into bank p+1, alongside that phase's Q/R successor write. Phase six needs no successor divisor bank. No dynamic bank-read mux may enter any timed arithmetic path.\n\nReset every new bank. Use the existing active_q && \"'!complete_q && iteration_q==p enable for each phase transfer, exactly matching the Q/R bank update. Launch must retain start && !busy semantics. Keep operation, signs, zero-divisor handling, INT_MIN/-1 results, capture of fully forwarded operands and blocked completion exactly as today. Neither a new request nor changing external operands may affect an active transaction. The real and ALTOPS busy/done/accept schedule stays identical; ALTOPS values and decoder defaults are untouched. nret remains one, and no contract file changes are allowed.\n\nThe added registers trade storage for shorter local routes and lower fanout from divisor state. Distinct phase enables and sequential transfers express separate live state rather than identical same-edge copies that synthesis can trivially merge. The original divisor_q and triple_divisor_q names may remain observation-only aliases to divisor_bank[0] and triple_divisor_bank[1] for existing cocotb checks; they must not drive the phase arithmetic. Expect roughly 362 additional declared operand-storage bits before pruning, with some upper-bit pruning possible. Added clock/control routing and the unchanged first-phase arithmetic are the main risks; vendor timing must decide whether locality wins.\n\nAn isolated prototype already passed canonical CoreMark with both bus stalls at exactly 4,230,003 marker-bracketed cycles, all canonical CRCs, validation text and no out-of-bounds access. Selftest and three random programs passed full RVFI cosimulation both with and without stalls, eight passing trace cases. The frequency forecast below is not yet measured. Run the existing real and ALTOPS divider tests, retain their independent prefix invariants, and verify each active phase'\"'s local D and 3D against the captured request. Preserve reset, request mutation, signed/unsigned boundary and completion-backpressure coverage. Run the unchanged formal suite and existing pipeline tests, including the fixed ten-cycle ready-bus retirement gap.\n\nMeasure all three Gowin placement options and compute median Fmax times the validated iterations/cycle. With unchanged CoreMark cycles, acceptance needs more than 77.18243 MHz and fitness above 182.46424 iter/sec. Do not accept a mere movement of the critical path or an improvement below 4.6 percent.''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':8,'confidence':'medium'},\n'changes':[\n{'file':'rtl/div_unit.sv','description':'Pipeline D and full 34-bit 3D through phase-local banks using the existing recurrence enables. Replace all timed shared-operand reads with static local reads, preserve phase-zero local multiples and parallel full-3D preparation, and retain all arithmetic, launch, reset, sign, exception and completion schedules. Keep old probe names only as observation aliases if tests need them.'},\n{'file':'test/test_div_unit.py','description':'Retain existing exact real/ALTOPS arithmetic, prefix-state and protocol assertions. Add checks that each phase consumes D and 3D from the same captured request, including consecutive differing divisors, high-bit boundaries, zero divisor, reset during each phase and completion backpressure.'},\n{'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'Retain existing predictor/forwarding models, exact-cycle assertions and retirement-progress checks. Exercise consecutive division requests with distinct signed and unsigned divisors across WB/EX forwarding and both bus stalls, checking exact RVFI operands and one retirement per request.'}\n]}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(d,sort_keys=False,width=88))\njsonschema.validate(d,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Updated schema-valid structural hypothesis')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_71", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-002-r9s1.yaml')\nd={\n'id':'hyp-20261001-002-r9s1',\n'title':'Carry divisor state through the dedicated division phase banks',\n'category':'structural',\n'motivation':'''The champion scores 174.44 iter/sec at 73.790 MHz with 4,230,003 bracketed CoreMark cycles. Prior structural work successfully distributed quotient/remainder state across dedicated phase banks (r6s1), but all seven arithmetic phases still read one divisor register and most also read one shared 3D register. Fresh isolated timing experiments expose divisor_q to remainder_bank[1] and remainder_bank[7] paths after other paths are shortened. Retiming branch recovery or writeback selection did not improve fitness. Complete the divider's spatial dataflow by carrying its invariant arithmetic inputs with the partial result, without changing its nine-cycle schedule.''',\n'hypothesis':'''Replace shared divisor fanout with statically connected phase-local divisor and triple-divisor banks. Keep the existing two-bit launch, seven 6/4/4/4/4/4/4-bit recurrence phases, eight quotient/remainder banks, final sign correction and completion retention. This is one structural change to the divider's registered operand distribution; arithmetic, pipeline issue, forwarding, branch prediction, memory and retirement timing remain unchanged.\n\nUse divisor_bank[0:6], each 32 bits, and triple_divisor_bank[1:6], each 34 bits. Accepted launch stores the unsigned magnitude D in divisor_bank[0], alongside the existing initial Q/R bank and captured flags. Phase p reads only divisor_bank[p], its existing predecessor Q/R bank and, for p>=1, triple_divisor_bank[p]. Preserve every prefix width, exact D/2D/3D subtraction, borrow-based one-hot selection and large-divisor bypass.\n\nOn the enabled edge for phase zero, compute full 34-bit 3D from divisor_bank[0] in parallel with the existing narrow six-bit recurrence. Capture D and full 3D into divisor_bank[1] and triple_divisor_bank[1]. Phase zero continues using its own narrow local 3D expression; do not move full-width multiple generation onto launch or its dependent digit chain. On each enabled phase p=1..5, pass D and full 3D from bank p into bank p+1, alongside that phase's Q/R successor write. Phase six needs no successor divisor bank. No dynamic bank-read mux may enter any timed arithmetic path.\n\nReset every new bank. Use the existing active_q && \"'!complete_q && iteration_q==p enable for each phase transfer, exactly matching the Q/R bank update. Launch must retain start && !busy semantics. Keep operation, signs, zero-divisor handling, INT_MIN/-1 results, capture of fully forwarded operands and blocked completion exactly as today. Neither a new request nor changing external operands may affect an active transaction. The real and ALTOPS busy/done/accept schedule stays identical; ALTOPS values and decoder defaults are untouched. nret remains one, and no contract file changes are allowed.\n\nThe added registers trade storage for shorter local routes and lower fanout from divisor state. Distinct phase enables and sequential transfers express separate live state rather than identical same-edge copies that synthesis can trivially merge. The original divisor_q and triple_divisor_q names may remain observation-only aliases to divisor_bank[0] and triple_divisor_bank[1] for existing cocotb checks; they must not drive the phase arithmetic. Expect roughly 362 additional declared operand-storage bits before pruning, with some upper-bit pruning possible. Added clock/control routing and the unchanged first-phase arithmetic are the main risks; vendor timing must decide whether locality wins.\n\nAn isolated prototype already passed canonical CoreMark with both bus stalls at exactly 4,230,003 marker-bracketed cycles, all canonical CRCs, validation text and no out-of-bounds access. Selftest and three random programs passed full RVFI cosimulation both with and without stalls, eight passing trace cases. The frequency forecast below is not yet measured. Run the existing real and ALTOPS divider tests, retain their independent prefix invariants, and verify each active phase'\"'s local D and 3D against the captured request. Preserve reset, request mutation, signed/unsigned boundary and completion-backpressure coverage. Run the unchanged formal suite and existing pipeline tests, including the fixed ten-cycle ready-bus retirement gap.\n\nMeasure all three Gowin placement options and compute median Fmax times the validated iterations/cycle. With unchanged CoreMark cycles, acceptance needs more than 77.18243 MHz and fitness above 182.46424 iter/sec. Do not accept a mere movement of the critical path or an improvement below 4.6 percent.''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':8,'confidence':'medium'},\n'changes':[\n{'file':'rtl/div_unit.sv','description':'Pipeline D and full 34-bit 3D through phase-local banks using the existing recurrence enables. Replace all timed shared-operand reads with static local reads, preserve phase-zero local multiples and parallel full-3D preparation, and retain all arithmetic, launch, reset, sign, exception and completion schedules. Keep old probe names only as observation aliases if tests need them.'},\n{'file':'test/test_div_unit.py','description':'Retain existing exact real/ALTOPS arithmetic, prefix-state and protocol assertions. Add checks that each phase consumes D and 3D from the same captured request, including consecutive differing divisors, high-bit boundaries, zero divisor, reset during each phase and completion backpressure.'},\n{'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'Retain existing predictor/forwarding models, exact-cycle assertions and retirement-progress checks. Exercise consecutive division requests with distinct signed and unsigned divisors across WB/EX forwarding and both bus stalls, checking exact RVFI operands and one retirement per request.'}\n]}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(d,sort_keys=False,width=88))\njsonschema.validate(d,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Updated schema-valid structural hypothesis')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "Updated schema-valid structural hypothesis\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
