{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_91", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport json,yaml,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-002-r9s1.yaml')\ncontent='''id: hyp-20261001-002-r9s1\ntitle: \\\"Register the first divider phase's narrow 3D multiple at launch\\\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion reaches 174.44 iter/sec at 73.790 MHz and 4,230,003\n  bracketed CoreMark cycles. Prior structural work distributed the\n  divider's quotient/remainder banks and moved full-width 3D preparation\n  alongside the first recurrence. However, phase zero still computes\n  its own narrow 3D before three serial radix-4 digits. Recent vendor\n  experiments repeatedly expose divisor_q to remainder_bank[1] as a\n  limiting path. The r8s1 three-plus-five bit repartition gained only\n  0.59 percent. Retiming the narrow multiple itself across the launch\n  boundary is a distinct way to remove work from that first recurrence.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add one 10-bit prefix_triple_q register to the real divider datapath.\n  On start && \"'!busy, capture exactly\n    {2'\"'b0, divisor_magnitude[7:0]}\n    + {1'b0, divisor_magnitude[7:0], 1'b0}.\n  Reset this register to zero and otherwise retain it until another\n  accepted launch. In phase p=0, drive the existing W=10 d3 wire directly\n  from prefix_triple_q, replacing its combinational d1+d2 expression.\n  This is one structural retiming of narrow multiple preparation across\n  the existing operand-capture boundary. Do not change quotient-bit\n  allocation, radix, arithmetic phase banks or execution latency.\n\n  The width is deliberate: phase zero produces an eight-bit consumed\n  prefix. For D<256, 3D<=765 fits exactly in ten bits, so the saved value\n  is the exact multiple needed by all three first-phase radix-4 digits.\n  For D>=256, retain the existing |divisor_q[31:8] large-divisor bypass\n  of the entire recurrence. In that case its truncated multiple must\n  never select the architectural next quotient or remainder. Do not\n  replace the full divisor with its low byte or change the bypass test.\n\n  Keep the existing full 34-bit triple_divisor_q generation during\n  iteration zero, parallel with the first recurrence. Phases one through\n  six continue to use this registered full-width multiple exactly as\n  before. Full-width 3D must not move into operand capture; only the\n  ten-bit first-phase value moves. Use explicitly narrow zero-extended\n  operands for the new addition, avoiding a 32/34-bit multiply or adder\n  followed by truncation. Use the existing signed/unsigned magnitude\n  result so negative signed divisors, including INT_MIN, remain correct.\n\n  Preserve the original 2+6+4+4+4+4+4+4 consumed-bit partition, nine-cycle\n  EX occupancy, sign/exception handling, completion retention and\n  start/busy/done/accept protocol. Launch captures the fully forwarded\n  request exactly once; changing source operands during execution must\n  not alter prefix_triple_q. Reset cancels execution and clears its new\n  state. Keep all real and ALTOPS formulas and scheduling identical to\n  the champion. No changes to forwarding, ordinary ALU, multiplication,\n  predictor, memory buses, decoder legality, RVFI, nret=1 or formal\n  properties/depths are part of this hypothesis.\n\n  The intended timing trade is a short extra launch-side adder in\n  exchange for removing local multiple generation from the first\n  three-digit recurrence. The launch-side sum only consumes the low\n  eight magnitude bits and does not depend on upper-bit carry output.\n  An unchanged forwarding/ALU/address path can become the new limit;\n  removing one divider adder does not guarantee a qualifying gain.\n\n  A prototype of exactly this retiming measured Gowin placement options\n  0/1/2 at 71.606, 74.330 and 74.330 MHz, giving median 74.330 MHz.\n  Option zero used 5,422 LUT4s and 1,223 registers, versus 5,422 and\n  1,214 for the champion; LUT RAM, BSRAM and DSP counts were unchanged.\n  Canonical CoreMark with both bus stalls retained exactly 4,230,003\n  marker-bracketed cycles, with all CRCs, validation text and bounds\n  checks passing. Calculated fitness is 175.72092 iter/sec, only\n  0.7343 percent above 174.44. This measured candidate DOES NOT exceed\n  the required 182.46424 acceptance threshold. The one-percent forecast\n  below reflects this small measured gain, not a claim of replacement.\n  Worst reported paths moved into existing forwarded ALU/address logic.\n\n  The prototype passed the unchanged real and ALTOPS divider suites and\n  existing real/ALTOPS pipeline suites, including independent prefix\n  arithmetic, captured operands, predictor behavior, exact-cycle checks,\n  reset, completion backpressure and the ten-cycle ready-bus retirement\n  bound. Selftest and three random programs passed full RVFI cosimulation\n  both with and without stalls, eight passing trace cases. Complete\n  formal qualification remains required; do not infer it from simulation\n  or alter the frozen checks to accommodate this change.\n\n  Preserve all existing tests. Add directed first-phase cases around\n  D=0,1,2,3,255,256,257 and signed equivalents, every two-bit launch\n  prefix, radix-4 decision boundaries, maximum first-byte prefixes and\n  large divisors whose low byte aliases a small divisor. Independently\n  check saved narrow 3D and the first successor Q/R state against the\n  captured magnitude and integer division. Cover request mutation,\n  reset and consecutive differing divisors. Reproduce the full vendor\n  median and validated CoreMark metric during implementation; retain\n  the champion unless the unchanged 4.6-percent threshold is exceeded.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 1\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/div_unit.sv\n    description: |\n      Add resettable 10-bit prefix_triple_q, written only on accepted\n      launch from explicitly zero-extended divisor_magnitude[7:0].\n      Feed it directly to phase-zero d3. Preserve the full 34-bit\n      triple preparation during phase zero, later-phase arithmetic,\n      large-divisor bypasses, bit partition and all real/ALTOPS timing.\n  - file: test/test_div_unit.py\n    description: |\n      Retain exact arithmetic, per-bank prefix invariants and handshake\n      coverage. Add launch and phase-zero checks for the saved narrow\n      triple, D=255/256 boundaries, low-byte aliases of large divisors,\n      signed magnitudes, zero divisor, source mutation, reset and\n      consecutive requests. Keep the existing nine-cycle assertions.\n'''\np.write_text(content)\nd=yaml.safe_load(content)\njsonschema.validate(d,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nassert d['id']=='hyp-20261001-002-r9s1'\nassert d['category']=='structural'\nassert type(d['expected_impact']['fitness_delta_pct']) is int\nassert -50<=d['expected_impact']['fitness_delta_pct']<=50\nprint('Final hypothesis written and schema-valid:',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
