{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_95","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python3 -B - <<'PY'\nfrom tools.eval.gowin import build_all,summarize\nimport json,pathlib\nr=build_all('.', 'cores/bench/generated/single_read_predictor/rtl','fpga/core_bench_si.sv','cores/bench/generated/single_read_predictor')\npathlib.Path('cores/bench/generated/single_read_predictor/results.json').write_text(json.dumps(r,indent=2))\nprint(json.dumps(summarize(r),indent=2))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"{\n  \"placement_failed\": false,\n  \"fmax_mhz\": 66.395,\n  \"seeds\": [\n    65.286,\n    66.395,\n    66.395\n  ],\n  \"perturbations\": [\n    [\n      0,\n      65.286,\n      20\n    ],\n    [\n      1,\n      66.395,\n      19\n    ],\n    [\n      2,\n      66.395,\n      19\n    ]\n  ],\n  \"lut4\": 5142,\n  \"ff\": 1095,\n  \"lutram\": 44,\n  \"bsram\": 4,\n  \"dsp\": 2,\n  \"logic_levels\": 20,\n  \"critical_path\": {\n    \"from\": \"cpu/u_ex/u_div/divisor_q_3_s0\",\n    \"to\": \"cpu/u_ex/u_div/remainder_bank[1]_1_s0\",\n    \"slack_ns\": -10.317,\n    \"arrival_ns\": 20.612,\n    \"logic_cells\": 34,\n    \"modules\": [\n      \"clock_ibuf\",\n      \"cpu/u_ex/u_div\"\n    ]\n  }\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_97", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-003-r11s2.yaml <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261001-003-r11s2\ntitle: \\\"Single-read RAM agree predictor with forwarded training counters\\\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  Prior predictor shrinking and one-bit RAM lost accuracy or failed to\n  improve the whole-core clock. Recovery-path prototypes in this slot\n  also failed to beat the champion. Preserve the existing 64-entry,\n  two-bit agree policy while replacing its per-entry flops and training\n  fanout with a native single-read distributed RAM. A two-read RAM\n  prototype did not infer RAM in Gowin; carrying the training counter\n  with its instruction removes the second read port entirely.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace only the physical storage and update architecture of the\n  64-entry agree predictor. Use one inferred 64x2 distributed RAM,\n  indexed for its sole asynchronous read by fetch PC[7:2], with one\n  synchronous write port. Preserve ordinary binary saturating states,\n  weak-agreement initialization 2'b10, BTFNT bias, direct B/J target\n  generation, and the existing saved predicted_taken bit and recovery.\n  Do not add global history, a BTB, return prediction or new pipeline\n  stages, and do not alter arithmetic or memory execution.\n\n  Carry a two-bit train_counter alongside each fetched instruction\n  through the combinational IF/ID bundle and into ID/EX. EX returns\n  this saved counter to IF together with the existing train_valid,\n  train_index and train_agree event. Compute new_counter by saturating\n  increment or decrement of the saved counter, and write new_counter\n  at train_index exactly when the existing advancing branch event is\n  accepted. There is no asynchronous training read from the RAM.\n\n  Crucially, preserve serial update semantics with counter forwarding:\n  out.train_counter = init_done && train_valid &&\n                      train_index == pc[7:2]\n                      ? new_counter : fetched_counter.\n  Here fetched_counter is the pre-edge RAM word, or 2'b10 during\n  initialization. Use fetched_counter[1], never the forwarded value,\n  to form this fetch's predicted_taken. Thus prediction uses the same\n  pre-edge state as the champion, while the saved training snapshot\n  includes any older branch's same-edge write. Back-to-back branches\n  aliasing one index must apply both updates in order, including\n  opposing updates and saturation. Without this bypass the younger\n  branch can overwrite an older update with a stale counter.\n\n  This snapshot is sufficient because the current core has exactly\n  one EX instruction and no registered fetch queue. No other branch\n  can train between an instruction's acceptance into ID/EX and its\n  eventual EX advancement. Hold its saved counter with the payload\n  during dmem and divider holds; capture under the existing \"'!stall\n  payload enable. Validity-only flushes suppress later training from\n  wrong-path snapshots without introducing redirect into the wide\n  payload enable. Do not feed counter forwarding into fetch validity,\n  branch direction prediction, hazard detection or redirect generation.\n\n  Do not reset the RAM array in a loop. Reset a six-bit initialization\n  index and init_done=0; disable writes on reset. During the next 64\n  non-reset edges write 2'\"'b10 to successive rows, setting init_done on\n  the row-63 write. Suppress branch training writes and use static\n  BTFNT prediction and counter snapshot 2'b10 during initialization.\n  Continue normal fetch, execution and retirement throughout scrub.\n  After initialization select train_index/new_counter/train_valid for\n  the single write port. A reset during scrub or training restarts it.\n  Uninitialized contents must never affect prediction or saved counters.\n  The row-63 transition must leave a just-accepted branch with the\n  correct initial counter. This cold-start behavior is explicit; after\n  initialization the table follows the original two-bit transitions.\n\n  Keep train_valid's original advancement, legality and alignment\n  qualification. Preserve all current EX recovery equations, exact\n  immediate targets, actual RVFI next PCs, prediction metadata holds,\n  bus-stall priorities, memory side effects, nret=1 ports, decoder\n  legality, arithmetic including ALTOPS, and bounded progress. No\n  program-specific state or address constants are permitted.\n\n  Confirm that vendor synthesis actually infers RAM16 storage for the\n  64x2 table and eliminates the 128 per-entry counter flops. Check\n  predictions against an independent counter-array model, including\n  same-index read/write, consecutive alias updates, saturation,\n  reset during scrub, the scrub/dynamic boundary and stalled branches.\n  Exercise dynamic prediction well past 64 clocks: the standard short\n  formal window alone cannot cover the initialized RAM state. Adapt\n  representation and initialization assertions in the pipeline model\n  while retaining architectural, operand, training-event, side-effect,\n  exact-once retirement and progress checks. Run unchanged formal and\n  full-RVFI cosim, canonical stalled CoreMark, and median Gowin timing.\n\n  The expected gain is an unmeasured forecast. Main risks are the\n  EX-to-counter-snapshot bypass path, RAM inference, and the unchanged\n  ALU/divider paths limiting Fmax. Acceptance still requires fitness\n  strictly above 182.46424 iter/sec; if cycles remain 4,230,003 this\n  requires median Fmax above 77.18243 MHz. Do not claim a winning\n  improvement from area reduction alone.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Replace 64 local counter flops with one single-read 64x2 distributed\n      RAM, a shared saturating write datapath, deterministic 64-cycle\n      initialization and same-index forwarding into the outgoing training\n      snapshot. Predict from the pre-edge read, retaining existing direct\n      target logic and PC/recovery priorities.\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: |\n      Add a two-bit train_counter to IF/ID and ID/EX; retain all existing\n      prediction, validity, architectural and retirement metadata.\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Capture the counter snapshot under the existing payload enable and\n      hold it through stalls. Preserve validity-only squash and reset.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Expose in.train_counter with the existing qualified training event.\n      Preserve branch comparisons, target arithmetic, actual next PC,\n      redirect generation, execution handshakes and arithmetic unchanged.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Wire the two-bit saved training counter from EX to IF without\n      altering external IO, hazard, forwarding or RVFI behavior.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Update packed-bundle offsets and the independent predictor oracle\n      for the scrub interval and counter snapshots. Retain architectural\n      and operand assertions. Check once-only training, same-index\n      forwarding, held snapshots, reset, wrong-path suppression and\n      dynamic predictor operation beyond initialization.\n  - file: test/test_predictor.py\n    description: |\n      Add directed and randomized checks against a separate 64-counter\n      model for all saturating transitions, pre-edge predictions,\n      consecutive same-index updates of either polarity, aliasing,\n      arbitrary holds, every scrub/reset phase and the row-63 boundary.\nYAML\npython3 -B - <<'PY'\nimport json,yaml,jsonschema\np='cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-003-r11s2.yaml'\nd=yaml.safe_load(open(p));jsonschema.validate(d,json.load(open('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json')))\nprint('Final architecture YAML validates')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
