{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_103","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python3 -B - <<'PY'\nfrom test.cosim.run_cosim import run_reference,run_verilator,compare\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport json\np=Path('/srv/hwebench/clones/gpt-6-astra_xhigh-v2-rep1/bench/programs'); sim='cores/bench/generated/single_read_predictor/obj_dir/cosim_sim'\nfor name in ['selftest','random1','random2','random3']:\n ref,done,why=run_reference(str(p/(name+'.elf')))\n for flags in [(),('--istall','--dstall')]:\n  tr,complete,rc,stderr=run_verilator(sim,str(p/(name+'.elf')),sim_flags=flags)\n  r=compare(ref,tr,done,complete,why,rc,stderr)\n  print(name,flags,json.dumps(r),flush=True)\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"selftest ('--istall', '--dstall') {\"passed\": true, \"retired\": 115}\nrandom1 () {\"passed\": true, \"retired\": 11037}\nrandom1 ('--istall', '--dstall') {\"passed\": true, \"retired\": 11037}\nrandom2 () {\"passed\": true, \"retired\": 11094}\nrandom2 ('--istall', '--dstall') {\"passed\": true, \"retired\": 11094}\nrandom3 () {\"passed\": true, \"retired\": 12059}\nrandom3 ('--istall', '--dstall') {\"passed\": true, \"retired\": 12059}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_104","type":"agent_message","text":"The RAM prototype inferred storage correctly and saved 280 LUT4s and 119 registers, but its 66.395 MHz median Fmax regressed. None of the tested predictor changes cleared the threshold. I’m retaining the registered alternate-destination proposal and recording its measured result: about −3%, with no claim that it beats the champion."}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_105", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-003-r11s2.yaml <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261001-003-r11s2\ntitle: \\\"Register the alternate successor for direct-branch recovery\\\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The champion's reported worst path runs from a forwarding mask through\n  EX branch resolution into the fetch PC. The current recovery datapath\n  uses the late actual_taken result both to detect a miss and to select\n  its 32-bit destination. Exact direct-target prediction makes that\n  second dependency unnecessary: a direction miss always needs the\n  alternative to the prediction already accepted at fetch. Prior\n  counter shrinking, Gray encoding and parallel predicate experiments\n  did not clear the threshold. Profiling also found only 13,735 JALRs\n  in the measured region, limiting the upside of an indirect predictor.\nhypothesis: |\n  Precompute and register one alternate successor address with each\n  fetched instruction. In if_stage, reuse the existing predicted_target\n  and PC+4 arithmetic and define\n  out.recovery_pc = predicted_taken ? (pc + 32'd4) : predicted_target.\n  Add a 32-bit recovery_pc to IF/ID and ID/EX. Capture it under the\n  existing \"'!stall ID/EX payload enable and hold it through stalls.\n  Reset the bundle normally; flushing need only clear validity and\n  the existing prediction bit, not the wide recovery_pc payload.\n\n  In EX replace only recovery target selection with\n  redirect_target = in.ctrl.is_jalr ? jump_target : in.recovery_pc.\n  Keep the existing advancing && (actual_taken != in.predicted_taken)\n  redirect equation and all its reset, validity and stall qualification.\n  Do not change branch comparisons, branch_taken, actual_taken,\n  misalignment logic, branch training or actual EX/MEM pc_next.\n  All controls selecting recovery data are now ID/EX register outputs.\n  The late forwarded branch comparison controls whether to recover,\n  without also selecting or generating a direct recovery address.\n  This is one predictor-recovery architecture change, with no added\n  pipeline stage or misprediction cycle.\n\n  A predicted-not-taken branch that actually takes uses the registered\n  exact branch target. A predicted-taken branch that falls through uses\n  registered PC+4. Retain the 64-entry agree predictor, aligned exact\n  B/J prediction and unpredicted JALR. An aligned JAL is predicted\n  correctly. Misaligned direct transfers are never predicted taken\n  and retain the existing trap/linear-PC behavior. JALR continues to\n  select its actual forwarded, bit-zero-cleared jump_target and uses\n  the existing alignment suppression. recovery_pc for nontransfers\n  and redirect_target while redirect is false are unused; do not\n  substitute either for the architectural next PC reported through RVFI.\n\n  Keep recovery limited to fetch PC selection and validity. Do not put\n  redirect on raw instruction data, source-index paths, or recovery_pc\n  payload enables. Preserve recovery priority over instruction-bus\n  stalls, deferral during older dmem stalls and execute holds, exact-once\n  memory effects, forwarding, arithmetic and ALTOPS, decoder legality,\n  nret=1 ports, ordered retirement and unchanged bounded progress.\n  Keep full 32-bit target semantics; do not specialize addresses to\n  CoreMark or the memory bounds.\n\n  An isolated prototype of this exact registered-target change passed\n  all 38 pipeline tests and six multiply/ALTOPS pipeline tests after\n  updating only packed-bundle widths and field offsets for the added\n  metadata. Canonical CoreMark with both bus stalls retained exactly\n  4,230,003 marker-bracketed cycles, all five canonical CRCs, the required\n  validation text, normal completion and no out-of-bounds accesses.\n  Gowin placement options 0/1/2 measured 70.253, 71.597 and 71.597 MHz.\n  Option zero used 5,306 LUT4s and 1,245 registers, versus the champion'\"'s\n  5,422 and 1,214, with unchanged RAM and DSP counts. Median fitness is\n  169.25993 iter/sec, approximately 2.97 percent BELOW the champion.\n  The limiting reported path moved to the first divider recurrence.\n\n  These measurements do not qualify this prototype as a replacement.\n  The negative forecast below records the measured limitation rather\n  than claiming that removing a recovery dependency guarantees a faster\n  whole core. The intended timing benefit remains the earlier recovery\n  address, but unchanged divider and forwarded-ALU paths can dominate.\n  Do not combine unrelated arithmetic, table or memory changes to rescue\n  this slot. Keep the champion unless the final implementation measures\n  strictly above 182.46424 iter/sec; at unchanged cycles this requires\n  median Gowin Fmax above 77.18243 MHz.\n\n  Run the unchanged formal suite and full-RVFI cosimulation on the final\n  implementation; the prototype's pipeline and benchmark checks do not\n  replace those gates. In particular prove that whenever redirect is\n  asserted, redirect_target equals the actual architectural successor.\n  Retain exact-cycle prediction, training, operand and side-effect\n  assertions, adapting only packed-layout references to the new field.\n  Exercise both direction-miss cases, all legal branch comparisons,\n  forwarded and held operands, direct and indirect jumps, misaligned\n  targets, instruction stalls concurrent with recovery, reset and\n  divider completion. Reproduce canonical CoreMark and all three\n  vendor timing options before evaluating acceptance.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: -3\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Produce recovery_pc as the alternate to the current direct prediction,\n      reusing predicted_target and PC+4. Keep prediction policy, training,\n      raw instruction delivery and fetch PC update priorities unchanged.\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: |\n      Add one 32-bit recovery_pc field to IF/ID and ID/EX. Preserve existing\n      predicted_taken and valid semantics and every architectural/RVFI field.\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Capture recovery_pc under the ordinary \"'!stall payload enable, retain\n      it during holds and reset it with the bundle. Keep validity-only\n      recovery and avoid a redirect-dependent payload enable.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Select redirect_target from registered recovery_pc for direct transfers\n      and actual jump_target for JALR using the registered is_jalr control.\n      Preserve redirect, actual next PC, comparisons, target arithmetic,\n      alignment/trap handling, training and arithmetic datapaths.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Update packed IF/ID and ID/EX widths and instruction/operand offsets\n      for recovery_pc, preserving all existing architectural, predictor,\n      exact-cycle and operand assertions. Check the registered alternate\n      successor and its hold behavior, both direct miss directions, JALR,\n      misalignment, reset, bus stalls and once-only recovery/training.\nYAML\npython3 -B - <<'\"'PY'\nimport json,yaml,jsonschema\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-003-r11s2.yaml')\nd=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text());jsonschema.validate(d,json.load(open('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json')))\nassert d['id']=='hyp-20261001-003-r11s2' and d['category']=='predictor'\nassert type(d['expected_impact']['fitness_delta_pct']) is int\nassert all(x['file'].startswith(('rtl/','test/test_')) for x in d['changes'])\nprint('Validated',p,'bytes',p.stat().st_size)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
