{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_6", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor l in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(l)\n if d.get('category')\"'!='\"'predictor': continue\n h=d.get('hypothesis',{})\n print('\\\\n',d['id'],d['title'],d.get('fitness'),d.get('cycles'))\n for k in ('motivation','hypothesis'):\n  s=h.get(k,'') if isinstance(h,dict) else h\n  if any(t in s.lower() for t in ['profil','gshare','gray','local history','miss']):\n   print(k+':',s)\n print('notes:',d.get('implementation_notes','')[:12000])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "\n hyp-20261001-003-r1s2 Small bimodal predictor with immediate direct targets 13.32 4202246\nnotes: \n\n hyp-20261001-003-r2s2 Recover branch bubbles with a stall-safe bimodal predictor 92.92 4202246\nnotes: \n\n hyp-20261001-003-r3s2 Bimodal prediction with a short validity-only recovery path 112.81 4202246\nnotes: \n\n hyp-20261001-003-r4s2 Compact 64-entry agree predictor with backward/forward branch bias 122.48 4194434\nmotivation: The champion already has exact B/J target prediction and a 128-entry\nbimodal direction table. Prior predictor attempts establish the value of\nlocal counter updates and recovery confined to PC selection and validity.\nA fresh reference trace of the canonical 2K, ten-iteration CoreMark run\ncontains 529,588 bracketed conditional branches and only 13,735 JALRs.\nSequential trace replay estimates 54,457 direction misses for the existing\nbimodal table. Even eliminating those misses offers little cycle headroom\nrelative to 4,202,246 measured cycles; a larger history predictor is unlikely\nto justify its FPGA cost. Instead, encode agreement with each branch's\nbackward-taken/forward-not-taken bias, allowing a smaller table to retain\naccuracy while reducing predictor storage and selection logic.\n\nAn isolated RTL prototype of the exact 64-entry direct-indexed agree design\nbelow measured 4,194,434 stalled, marker-bracketed CoreMark cycles and median\nGowin Fmax 51.374 MHz, versus 4,202,246 cycles and 47.405 MHz for the champion.\nIts preliminary fitness is 122.4814 iterations/second, 8.57 percent above\n112.81. This supports a frequency-oriented predictor change rather than a\nclaim that direction accuracy alone can deliver a large speedup.\n\nnotes: \n\n hyp-20261001-003-r5s2 Register bank-decoded training events for the 64-entry agree predictor 121.53 4194434\nmotivation: The current 64-entry agree predictor already removes most recoverable\nbranch bubbles. Prior experiments established local saturating updates\nand validity-only recovery as useful, while the most recent improvement\nwas dominated by Gowin Fmax rath\n…[32905 of 35977 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:ba6a945d42475105]…\nvider and forwarded-ALU paths can dominate.\nDo not combine unrelated arithmetic, table or memory changes to rescue\nthis slot. Keep the champion unless the final implementation measures\nstrictly above 182.46424 iter/sec; at unchanged cycles this requires\nmedian Gowin Fmax above 77.18243 MHz.\n\nRun the unchanged formal suite and full-RVFI cosimulation on the final\nimplementation; the prototype's pipeline and benchmark checks do not\nreplace those gates. In particular prove that whenever redirect is\nasserted, redirect_target equals the actual architectural successor.\nRetain exact-cycle prediction, training, operand and side-effect\nassertions, adapting only packed-layout references to the new field.\nExercise both direction-miss cases, all legal branch comparisons,\nforwarded and held operands, direct and indirect jumps, misaligned\ntargets, instruction stalls concurrent with recovery, reset and\ndivider completion. Reproduce canonical CoreMark and all three\nvendor timing options before evaluating acceptance.\n\nnotes: \n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
