{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_9", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor line in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d.get('category')=='predictor':\n  print(d['id'], d['title'], 'fitness',d.get('fitness'),'cycles',d.get('cycles'))\n  h=d.get('hypothesis',{})\n  s=(h.get('hypothesis','') if isinstance(h,dict) else h)+'\\\\n'+d.get('implementation_notes','')\n  for p in s.split('\\\\n\\\\n'):\n   if any(x in p.lower() for x in ['jalr','return','46,','misses','ras ','stack']): print(p[:3500])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261001-003-r1s2 Small bimodal predictor with immediate direct targets fitness 13.32 cycles 4202246\nAdd one fetch-time direct-control-flow predictor: a 128-entry, two-bit\nsaturating bimodal direction table indexed by PC[8:2], plus exact immediate\ntargets for legal conditional branches and JAL. Reset counters to weakly\ntaken (2'b10). Predict an aligned legal branch taken when its counter MSB\nis set, and predict an aligned JAL unconditionally. Compute B/J immediates\nfrom the raw imem_data and add them to the current fetch PC. All other\ninstructions, including JALR and reserved branch encodings, predict PC+4.\nUse no BTB, global history, return stack, or additional pipeline stage.\nCarry one predicted_taken bit with the accepted instruction into ID/EX.\nThis is sufficient because every predicted target is the instruction's\nexact PC-relative target, and indirect jumps are never predicted. EX keeps\nthe existing forwarded-operand comparisons, target calculation, alignment\ntraps, and RVFI pc_next semantics. Define actual_taken as a taken branch or\njump with no target-alignment fault. Recover only when a valid, advancing\nEX instruction has actual_taken != predicted_taken. Recover to the resolved\ntarget when actually taken, otherwise to that instruction's PC+4. Correctly\npredicted branches and JAL must neither flush nor insert a bubble; aligned\nJALR still redirects in EX, and a misaligned transfer still traps exactly\nas in the baseline. Avoid a new 32-bit predicted-target comparison.\nTrain once per valid, legal, non-faulting conditional branch advancing out\nof EX, using its own PC index and resolved direction. Increment or decrement\nthe current indexed counter with saturation, not a counter snapshot saved\nat fetch. Do not train on JAL, JALR, bubbles, squashed instructions, or held\ninstructions. A same-index lookup and update may observe the pre-edge\ncounter; correctness depends on the saved prediction bit, never a reread\nof the table in EX. Reset only predictor state in addition to existing\nreset behavior. Keep the sing\n…[13651 of 16723 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:b60159d65e962759]…\nion-policy timing expectations only where delayed training\nactually changes the predicted direction. The isolated prototype passed\nall 38 pipeline cases and six ALTOPS multiply pipeline cases after adapting\nthe independent event oracle. Preserve the lookup/update crossing assertion\nwith a directed cold forward-taken branch to an alias at PC+256, so lookup\ncoincides with the delayed weak-agree to weak-disagree write. Selftest and\nall three random programs passed full RVFI comparison both with and without\nstalls, eight cases total. All 53 unchanged single-retirement ALTOPS formal\nchecks also passed, including register consistency, PC continuity, unique\nretirement, legality and liveness. These ran in isolated staging with the\noriginal wrapper, properties and depths. Real-arithmetic formal-deep was\nnot run; arithmetic is unchanged. Reproduce these formal and full-RVFI\ncosim gates and canonical CoreMark CRC, UART, bounds and marker validation\non the final implementation before evaluating all three vendor options.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"agent_message","text":"The prior predictor changes mostly shifted the timing bottleneck, and conditional-branch misses offer little remaining headroom. I’m focusing on return-address prediction: every JALR currently incurs a recovery bubble, so correctly predicting returns could save cycles."}}
