{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_53", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-003-r13s2.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261001-003-r13s2\ntitle: \"Predict function returns with a small resolved-call return stack\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The champion predicts direct branches and JAL but unconditionally recovers\n  every JALR in EX. Earlier predictor timing experiments repeatedly exposed\n  unchanged ALU or divider limits; r12s2 gained only 0.83 percent. Its history\n  also reports approximately 46,000 direction misses in 4,230,003 CoreMark\n  cycles, leaving little benefit from a more elaborate direction table.\n  Function returns remain a separate source of compulsory recovery bubbles.\n  A small return-address stack can remove these bubbles while preserving the\n  successful 64-entry agree predictor and the current pipeline depth.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add an eight-entry return-address stack trained only by valid, legal,\n  aligned control transfers advancing from EX. Predict canonical returns\n  JALR x0,0(x1) and JALR x0,0(x5) from a valid aligned stack top. Keep all\n  other JALR instructions unpredicted and retain the existing direct B/J\n  predictor exactly. This is one return-prediction change, not a direction\n  predictor replacement, fetch queue, arithmetic change or extra stage.\n\n  Store full RV32 return addresses with their implied zero alignment bits;\n  use eight 30-bit entries and a bounded occupancy count. A shift-stack\n  implementation exposes entry zero directly to fetch without an indexed\n  target-read mux. Push the advancing call's PC+4 for JAL with rd in {x1,x5}.\n  For JALR apply the standard link-register hints: push if rd is a link\n  register and rs1 is not; pop if rs1 is a link register and rd is not;\n  push only if both are the same link register; pop then push if they are\n  different link registers. No hint means no mutation. A full push discards\n  the oldest entry; an empty pop is inert. Pop-then-push replaces the top\n  when nonempty and creates one entry when empty. Reset clears occupancy.\n  Illegal or misaligned transfers and nonadvancing instructions do not\n  mutate the stack. Never use a program addre\n…[3916 of 6988 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:2c34e9548006680d]…\nive targets, traps and RVFI metadata.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Wire the EX return-stack event and link address to fetch without\n      changing top-level ports, hazards, forwarding or retirement width.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Add an independent bounded-stack oracle and architectural tests for\n      nested calls, alternate x5 links, JALR hint combinations, tail calls,\n      overflow/underflow, immediate and consecutive returns, and nonstandard\n      link manipulation causing a taken-to-taken target miss. Cover fully\n      forwarded return operands, bit-zero clearing, bit-one misalignment,\n      illegal JALR, wrong-path calls/returns, stalls, divider holds and reset.\n      Check exact-once updates, RVFI continuity, absence of wrong-path memory\n      effects and zero-bubble correct predictions. Preserve all existing\n      architectural and liveness assertions, adapting only policy-dependent\n      expectations that previously assumed every JALR must redirect.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
