{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_76", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-003-r13s2.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261001-003-r13s2\ntitle: \"Keep two-bit agree hysteresis in a pipelined distributed-RAM predictor\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The current 64-entry agree predictor uses locally updated flip-flop\n  counters, replicated update decoding and a 64-way lookup mux. Earlier\n  small-table experiments lost accuracy to aliases; r7s2's native one-bit\n  RAM lost hysteresis. Neither result tests preserving all 64 two-bit\n  counters in distributed RAM with a registered training transaction.\n  Registering training alone was insufficient, but it can also isolate\n  the shared read-modify-write path needed for compact two-bit RAM storage.\n  This proposal changes that storage architecture while retaining the\n  established prediction policy, recovery latency and table capacity.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace the 64 locally updated two-bit agreement counters in if_stage\n  with one logical 64x2 distributed-RAM table, two asynchronous read\n  addresses and one synchronous write. The fetch read uses PC[7:2]; the\n  training read uses a registered event index. Infer native Gowin LUT RAM\n  with an explicit distributed_ram attribute and no reset assignment to\n  the memory array. Allow synthesis to replicate storage if necessary\n  for the two reads, but retain one coherent logical write stream.\n  Preserve two-bit binary saturating counters, weak-agree initialization\n  2'b10, backward/forward bias, all 64 entries and the original PC index.\n  Do not replace them with one-bit outcomes or a smaller table.\n\n  Register each qualified EX training event as valid, index[5:0] and\n  agreement. Consume the previous cycle's event by asynchronously reading\n  the current counter at its saved index, applying the ordinary saturating\n  increment/decrement and writing on the next edge. Accept one new event\n  every cycle. Validity clears when there is no new event; held frontend\n  or memory payloads must not replay writes. EX keeps its existing valid,\n  advancing, legal and nonfaulting branch qualification. A queued event\n  still applies during a current\n…[3779 of 6851 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:b3ad594348cec5b0]…\ndelta_pct: 6\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Replace the locally updated flop counters with a 64x2 distributed\n      RAM, asynchronous fetch and registered-event training reads, and a\n      coherent synchronous saturating-update port. Add an eight-bit\n      training event and 64-write initialization sequencer with static\n      BTFNT fallback. Preserve predictor indexing, hysteresis, targets,\n      accepted prediction metadata and same-cycle recovery behavior.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Extend the independent predictor oracle for delayed training and\n      reset scrubbing. Check every initialization write and transition\n      into dynamic mode, all saturating states, write/read collisions,\n      consecutive aliases, event cancellation and exact-once consumption\n      under stalls/reset. Exercise dynamic operation beyond the formal\n      window and preserve full RVFI, operand, memory-side-effect, legality\n      and progress assertions.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
