{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_59", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-003-r14s2.yaml')\nh={\n'id':'hyp-20261001-003-r14s2',\n'title':'Register predictor recovery with separate successor targets and a recovery barrier',\n'category':'predictor',\n'motivation':'''Prior predictor storage changes and latency-preserving feedback retiming\nleft either EX recovery or the backend limiting Fmax. Direction accuracy\nhas limited remaining headroom: an architectural CoreMark trace has\n46,342 conditional misses and 13,735 JALRs inside the markers, against\n4,230,003 champion cycles. This proposal instead cuts late EX feedback\nwith a registered recovery event, accepting one extra cycle on a miss.\nSeparate target registers avoid selecting a wide recovery address with\nthe late forwarded branch predicate before that register boundary.''',\n'hypothesis':'''Keep the current 64-entry agree predictor and exact direct B/J prediction.\nRegister the advancing EX recovery event for use by IF and the hazard\nunit in the following cycle. Capture four distinct recovery fields:\nqualified mismatch valid, actual_taken, the resolved transfer target,\nand PC+4. The transfer target is is_jump ? jump_target : branch_target,\nwithout an actual_taken-controlled fallthrough mux before its register.\nSelect registered transfer target versus registered PC+4 using registered\nactual_taken when driving IF's recovery address. These are recovery\nmetadata registers; keep the existing single fetch-PC register.\n\nRegister each qualified EX training event's valid, six-bit PC index and\nagreement bit on the same boundary. Apply the event to the existing\nper-entry saturating counters on the following edge. Clear both event\nvalids when no new event arrives; do not hold a valid pulse through a\nstall or discard an older training event during recovery. Preserve the\ncurrent local counter update implementation, initial weak-agree state,\nPC[7:2] indexing and saved predicted_taken bit.\n\nA pending recovery must kill the extra younger instruction that reached\nID/EX before the registered event became visible. Feed EX a copy of\nID/EX with valid masked by \"'!recovery_valid. This mask must suppress\nnext_reg_write, divider start, further redirects and training. Raw data\nand decoded payload remain untouched. Route only registered recovery to\nIF and the hazard unit; do not add an immediate EX-to-ID flush bypass.\nRegistered recovery overrides an instruction-bus stall, installs the\nselected recovery PC, and clears younger ID/EX validity on that edge.\n\nDuring this recovery cycle retain the resolving control transfer in\nEX/MEM and prevent its transfer into MEM/WB: use\n    ex_stall = existing_dmem_stall || recovery_valid\n    mem_hold_wb = existing_hold_mem_wb || recovery_valid.\nThe previously older WB instruction still retires once, and mem_hold_wb\nclears WB validity while retaining forwarding data. On the next edge the\ncontrol transfer advances once and EX/MEM receives the invalid younger\nbubble. This is a one-cycle recovery barrier in the actual pipeline,\nincluding link-register writeback, not an RVFI-only delay. It does not\nadd another cycle to fetching the corrected path. It keeps a recovered\nbranch followed immediately by a nine-cycle division within the existing\nten-cycle ready-bus retirement-gap limit. Without this barrier a directed\nbranch-to-DIV sequence produces an unacceptable eleven-cycle gap.\n\nThe recovery event can only be captured when the resolving transfer\nadvances past any older dmem hold. Therefore EX/MEM contains that branch\nor jump during recovery, with no memory side effects; the older memory\nrequest was already accepted. Assert this condition and verify that no\nwrong-path store, load, multiply or DIV/REM launches or survives. Retain\nreset priority, event cancellation on reset, all legal/illegal and\nmisaligned transfer semantics, JALR bit-zero clearing and link values.\nRVFI reports resolved pc_next and actual operands with one retirement\nand monotonic order, never predicted successors. Keep nret=1 and all\nexternal ports, arithmetic and formal/progress bounds unchanged.\n\nThis is one predictor feedback-pipeline change. Do not combine it with\na fetch queue, new predictor policy, target cache, return stack, branch\ncomparator rewrite, trap-control rewrite or arithmetic optimization.\nThe expected benefit is lower load and shorter logic from EX resolution\nto the high-fanout PC/flush/training sinks. The cost is one additional\nrecovery cycle per miss and more recovery metadata registers.\n\nAn isolated prototype measured Gowin placement options 0/1/2 at\n74.479, 73.648 and 73.648 MHz, median 73.648 MHz, with 5,414 LUT4s,\n1,288 registers and 40 LUT RAMs in option zero. The worst reported path\nremained in the first divider recurrence. This does not establish the\nrequired timing gain. The forecast below is conservative and must not\nbe interpreted as a qualifying replacement; the champion stays unless\nthe final implementation exceeds 182.46424 iterations/second.\n\nValidate the delayed event protocol with an independent accepted-event\nqueue and the original binary counter oracle. Cover back-to-back branch\ntraining, same-index aliases, both direction misses, JALR, reset with a\npending event, simultaneous instruction/data stalls, and younger stores\nor divisions on the discarded path. Check recovery followed immediately\nby each DIV/REM operation against the unchanged ten-cycle progress\nassertion. Update only obsolete cycle/policy snapshots, retaining every\narchitectural, trap, exact-once and arithmetic assertion. Run unchanged\nformal, full RVFI cosim, canonical stalled CoreMark with all five CRCs,\nUART validation, bounds and markers, and median vendor timing.'\"''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':-2,'confidence':'low'},\n'changes':[\n{'file':'rtl/core.sv','description':'''Separate raw EX feedback wires from registered recovery and training.\nCapture recovery valid, actual direction, transfer target and PC+4 in\nseparate registers; choose the recovery address after the boundary.\nMask EX input validity during registered recovery and wire the one-cycle\nEX/MEM and MEM/WB recovery barrier. Route registered feedback to IF and\nhazards without an immediate flush bypass. Preserve top-level nret=1 IO.'''},\n{'file':'rtl/ex_stage.sv','description':'''Expose actual_taken separately and provide the unconditional resolved\nbranch/jump transfer target for recovery capture, excluding the late\nactual_taken-controlled PC+4 mux. Keep qualified mismatch, training,\narchitectural pc_next, traps, forwarding and arithmetic unchanged.\nExisting invalid-input handling and stall retention implement the\ncore-wired recovery mask and resolving-instruction barrier.'''},\n{'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'''Model one-cycle queued recovery/training and compare counter transitions\nagainst the independent binary oracle. Assert the resolving transfer is\nheld once, younger EX cannot start or train, and no wrong-path memory or\nregister side effects survive. Exercise all stalls, faults, reset and\nback-to-back events; retain RVFI ordering and the original ten-cycle\nprogress bound, especially recovered-branch-to-DIV/REM sequences.'''}]}\nclass D(yaml.SafeDumper):pass\nD.add_representer(str,lambda d,s:d.represent_scalar('tag:yaml.org,2002:str',s,style='|' if '\\\\n' in s else None))\np.write_text(yaml.dump(h,Dumper=D,sort_keys=False,width=90))\njsonschema.validate(yaml.safe_load(p.read_text()),json.load(open('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json')))\nprint('Final architecture written and schema validated.')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
