{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_81","type":"agent_message","text":"The strongest completed prototype is banked predictor training: it preserved CoreMark cycles and passed CRC validation, but measured 121.53 iter/sec—0.78% below the champion. I’ll record that result candidly; the final bank-size checks may refine the choice, but no tested candidate has cleared acceptance."}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_82", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport json,yaml,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-003-r5s2.yaml')\nd={\n'id':'hyp-20261001-003-r5s2',\n'title':'Register bank-decoded training events for the 64-entry agree predictor',\n'category':'predictor',\n'motivation':'''The current 64-entry agree predictor already removes most recoverable\nbranch bubbles. Prior experiments established local saturating updates\nand validity-only recovery as useful, while the most recent improvement\nwas dominated by Gowin Fmax rather than fewer direction misses. In this\nsession, the unchanged champion reproduced its 51.374 MHz median exactly.\nSmaller tables, one-bit agreement, shared confidence and LUT RAM storage\nall failed to beat its fitness. The strongest completed predictor\nprototype instead retimed the training distribution into eight banks,\nkeeping all 64 independent two-bit counters and their direction lookup.\nThis is the single proposed angle: isolate late EX training feedback\nfrom counter update decoding with a registered bank selection.\n''',\n'hypothesis':'''Keep the existing 64-entry agree table indexed by PC[7:2], exact B/J\ntarget prediction, weak-agreement reset state and locally saturating\ncounters. Add one stage only to predictor training, with eight registered\none-hot bank enables, a three-bit registered row index, and one registered\nagreement bit. At every non-reset edge set bank_q to train_valid ?\n(8'b1 << train_index[5:3]) : 8'b0. Capture row_q=train_index[2:0] and\nagree_q=train_agree only when train_valid is asserted. On reset clear\nall bank enables, row and agreement state, and reset every counter to\n2'b10 as before.\n\nCounter entry e consumes the PREVIOUS cycle's event when bank_q[e/8]\nis set and row_q equals e%8. Increment its current local counter toward\n3 for agree_q=1, decrement toward 0 otherwise. Preserve the original\nbinary saturating-counter implementation; no shared counter read mux,\nstate encoding change, capacity reduction or direction hash is proposed.\nThe new register boundary splits EX resolution and high-bank decoding\nfrom the local row decode and saturating counter transition. It costs\n12 additional state bits while reducing combinational control sharing\nbetween the frontend table and late EX feedback.\n\nThe event register must accept one resolved branch every cycle and apply\none pending event every cycle, including consecutive aliases. Clear bank\nvalidity on cycles without a new event; holding it would train repeatedly.\nAn already captured event applies despite current imem stalls, dmem stalls,\ndivider activity or a younger redirect. It represents an older branch\nthat already advanced. Reset cancels pending training with priority over\nall table writes. Keep existing EX train_valid, train_index and train_agree\nqualifications unchanged. Do not add training backpressure to the CPU.\n\nFetch reads the current pre-edge table state. Delaying training can change\nnearby predictions, but the accepted instruction still carries its actual\npredicted_taken bit, so existing EX mismatch recovery remains authoritative.\nNo bypass of a pending update, speculative history or rollback is needed.\nKeep PC priorities, legal/aligned direct targets, unpredicted JALR,\nvalidity-only squashes, raw decode inputs and all pipeline payload holds.\nDo not modify arithmetic, ALTOPS scheduling, decoder defaults, trap behavior,\nRVFI ordering, memory handshakes, nret=1 or any evaluation contract.\n\nAn isolated prototype of exactly this eight-bank implementation measured\n50.713, 50.975 and 50.975 MHz across the three vendor placement options;\nmedian Fmax was 50.975 MHz. Area was 3,197 LUT4s and 751 registers, versus\n3,277 and 739 for the champion, with unchanged 8 LUT RAMs, 6 BSRAMs and\n6 DSPs. Canonical stalled CoreMark retained exactly 4,194,434 bracketed\ncycles, completed with both markers, no OOB, all five expected CRCs and\nthe required validation text. Calculated fitness was 121.5301 iter/sec,\n0.78 percent BELOW 122.48. This prototype therefore does not establish\nan improvement and does not clear the 128.11408 acceptance threshold.\nThe forecast is deliberately negative rather than claiming an unsupported\nfive-percent gain. This is the best completed predictor-only candidate\nfrom the investigation, not a measured winning replacement.\n\nAfter implementation, reproduce the median Gowin measurement and run the\nunchanged formal and architectural cosim gates. The preparation experiment\nwas a timing/CoreMark prototype, not a completed formal qualification.\nThe main limitation is that the measured critical path still traverses\nregister-file/forwarding and execute arithmetic outside the predictor;\ntraining retiming may not improve that limiting path sufficiently.\n''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':-1,'confidence':'low'},\n'changes':[\n{'file':'rtl/if_stage.sv','description':'''Add an eight-bank one-hot training event register, a three-bit row and\none agreement bit. Apply previous-cycle events to the unchanged 64 local\nbinary saturating counters using entry/8 and entry%8. Reset cancels the\nqueue and resets counters; a missing new event clears all bank enables.\nDrain pending events independently of current frontend/backend stalls.\nKeep fetch lookup, immediate targets and PC/validity behavior unchanged.\n'''},\n{'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'''Extend the independent prediction oracle with one pending training event.\nKeep checking current EX eligibility, but apply the previous event to\nreference counters only after pre-edge prediction checks; compare DUT\ncounter state after the edge. Track resolved and applied events separately\nand drain the final event before asserting exact-once update counts.\nCover every bank/row boundary, consecutive same-index and cross-bank\nbranches, saturation, opposite-bias aliases, delayed threshold crossings,\nreset with an event pending, and updates during imem/dmem/divider holds\nor younger recovery. Retain all existing legality, misalignment, accepted\nprediction metadata, wrong-path suppression, RVFI and liveness checks.\n'''}]}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(d,sort_keys=False,default_flow_style=False,width=90))\njsonschema.validate(yaml.safe_load(p.read_text()),json.load(open('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json')))\nprint('Wrote and validated',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
