{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_8", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor line in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n r=json.loads(line)\n if r.get('category')=='predictor':\n  print('\\\\nID:',r['id'],'title:',r['title'],'fitness:',r.get('fitness'),'cycles:',r.get('cycles'))\n  h=r.get('hypothesis',{})\n  if isinstance(h,dict):\n   print(h.get('hypothesis',''))\n  print('Notes:',r.get('implementation_notes',''))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "\nID: hyp-20261001-003-r1s2 title: Small bimodal predictor with immediate direct targets fitness: 13.32 cycles: 4202246\nAdd one fetch-time direct-control-flow predictor: a 128-entry, two-bit\nsaturating bimodal direction table indexed by PC[8:2], plus exact immediate\ntargets for legal conditional branches and JAL. Reset counters to weakly\ntaken (2'b10). Predict an aligned legal branch taken when its counter MSB\nis set, and predict an aligned JAL unconditionally. Compute B/J immediates\nfrom the raw imem_data and add them to the current fetch PC. All other\ninstructions, including JALR and reserved branch encodings, predict PC+4.\nUse no BTB, global history, return stack, or additional pipeline stage.\n\nCarry one predicted_taken bit with the accepted instruction into ID/EX.\nThis is sufficient because every predicted target is the instruction's\nexact PC-relative target, and indirect jumps are never predicted. EX keeps\nthe existing forwarded-operand comparisons, target calculation, alignment\ntraps, and RVFI pc_next semantics. Define actual_taken as a taken branch or\njump with no target-alignment fault. Recover only when a valid, advancing\nEX instruction has actual_taken != predicted_taken. Recover to the resolved\ntarget when actually taken, otherwise to that instruction's PC+4. Correctly\npredicted branches and JAL must neither flush nor insert a bubble; aligned\nJALR still redirects in EX, and a misaligned transfer still traps exactly\nas in the baseline. Avoid a new 32-bit predicted-target comparison.\n\nPrediction advances the PC only when the current fetch is actually accepted\ninto ID/EX: imemReady, no load-use stall, no dmem stall, and no recovery.\nLatch prediction metadata with exactly the same enables as the instruction;\nbubbles clear it and a held ID/EX entry retains it. Recovery has priority\nover an imem stall or a younger load-use condition. During an older dmem\nstall, defer both recovery and predictor training until EX can advance;\nkeep the unresolved instruction and its metadata intact. Thus a held branch\ncannot re\n…[18380 of 21452 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:cfee4bca050f34ba]…\n LUT RAMs, 6 BSRAMs and\n6 DSPs. Canonical stalled CoreMark retained exactly 4,194,434 bracketed\ncycles, completed with both markers, no OOB, all five expected CRCs and\nthe required validation text. Calculated fitness was 121.5301 iter/sec,\n0.78 percent BELOW 122.48. This prototype therefore does not establish\nan improvement and does not clear the 128.11408 acceptance threshold.\nThe forecast is deliberately negative rather than claiming an unsupported\nfive-percent gain. This is the best completed predictor-only candidate\nfrom the investigation, not a measured winning replacement.\n\nAfter implementation, reproduce the median Gowin measurement and run the\nunchanged formal and architectural cosim gates. The preparation experiment\nwas a timing/CoreMark prototype, not a completed formal qualification.\nThe main limitation is that the measured critical path still traverses\nregister-file/forwarding and execute arithmetic outside the predictor;\ntraining retiming may not improve that limiting path sufficiently.\n\nNotes: \n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
