{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_41", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-003-r6s2.yaml')\nh={\n'id':'hyp-20261001-003-r6s2',\n'title':'Reduce destructive branch aliases with folded indexing of the 64-entry agree predictor',\n'category':'predictor',\n'motivation':'''The current 64-entry agree predictor uses only PC[7:2], so branches\nseparated by multiples of 256 bytes always share a counter. Previous\ntraining retiming left CoreMark cycles unchanged and regressed timing;\nsmaller direct-index tables also failed to improve the prior champion.\nThe remaining simple predictor angle is to reduce destructive aliasing\nwithout growing the table or adding a target-recovery path.\n\nProfiling the canonical timed region found 529,588 conditional branches\nand only 13,735 JALR returns among 2,922,016 instructions. This limits the\npotential cycle gain from a return stack. Architectural trace replay gave\n46,325 direction misses for the current table and 41,175 with the proposed\nfolded index. Replay alone is not a cycle-accurate measurement; the isolated\nRTL prototype below supplies actual stalled CoreMark and vendor timing.\n''',\n'hypothesis':'''Keep all 64 independent two-bit agreement counters and change their index\nto fetch_index = pc[7:2] XOR pc[13:8]. Compute train_index from the resolving\nbranch's own in.pc[7:2] XOR in.pc[13:8]. This is exactly one architectural\npredictor change: XOR-fold two adjacent word-address fields so branches\nwith identical low PC bits need not interfere. The table remains untagged,\n64 entries wide and six-bit indexed. Do not hard-code branch addresses,\nbenchmark phases, program signatures or instruction-specific initial state.\n\nPreserve weak-agreement reset 2'b10, the existing binary saturating state\nencoding, and per-entry local updates. Increment when branch_taken equals\nthe B-immediate sign and decrement otherwise. Fetch still predicts a legal\nconditional branch taken exactly when the selected counter MSB equals the\nimmediate sign and its exact target is aligned. Preserve unconditional\naligned JAL prediction, unpredicted JALR, the existing immediate target\nadder, and the actual predicted_taken bit saved with the instruction.\nDo not add global history, a target table, a return stack, new pipeline\nstages, delayed training or a second indexed training read mux.\n\nTrain once per valid legal non-faulting conditional branch advancing from\nEX under the unchanged \"'!reset, !stall and !execute_busy qualifications.\nUse the current local counter at the folded index of that branch'\"'s PC,\nnever the fetch PC or a fetch-time counter snapshot. Keep nonblocking\npre-edge lookup/update semantics and local saturation. Held instructions,\nsquashed slots, jumps and traps cannot train. Lookup and update must use\nthe same fold even when different branch PCs alias after hashing.\n\nAll PC acceptance and recovery behavior stays unchanged: reset, redirect,\naccepted predicted next PC, then hold. Keep exact direct targets, one-bit\nEX direction-mismatch recovery, validity-only squashes, raw instruction\nand source-index paths, ID/EX metadata holds, load-use interlocks, divider\nscheduling, memory handshakes and retained forwarding data. Preserve the\nsingle-channel nret=1 interface, RVFI ordering and resolved pc_next, decoder\nlegality, arithmetic and ALTOPS behavior and every evaluation contract.\n\nAn isolated prototype of exactly this six-bit fold completed canonical\nstalled CoreMark in 4,190,330 marker-bracketed cycles, versus 4,194,434 for\nthe champion. Both timing markers, all five expected CRCs, the required\nUART validation text and no out-of-bounds accesses were observed. Gowin\nplacement options 0/1/2 measured 58.552, 59.314 and 59.314 MHz, giving a\n59.314 MHz median. Option-0 area was 3,458 LUT4s and 702 registers, with\n40 LUT RAMs, four BSRAMs and six DSPs. The worst reported path remained in\nthe unchanged divider. Calculated prototype fitness is 141.54971 iter/sec,\napproximately 0.697 percent above 140.57. This is the best completed\npredictor-only prototype from this investigation, but it DOES NOT clear\nthe required 147.03622 iter/sec acceptance threshold. The integer forecast\nis therefore one percent with low confidence, not an unsupported five\npercent gain. These are preliminary timing/CoreMark measurements, not a\ncompleted formal qualification or an accepted replacement.\n\nAfter implementation, update the independent predictor oracle and directed\nalias cases, retaining their architectural assertions. Cover every index,\nPCs separated by 256 bytes that no longer alias, deliberately constructed\nfold collisions, opposite branch biases, saturation and simultaneous\nsame-index lookup/update. Preserve reset, stale prediction, wrong-path,\nimem/dmem stall, divider and misalignment tests. Run unchanged formal and\narchitectural cosim gates, then reproduce median Gowin Fmax and canonical\nstalled CoreMark. Main limitations are the small recoverable branch-bubble\nbudget, added XOR delay and placement sensitivity with a divider-limited\ncritical path; no accuracy-only or single-placement result establishes\nacceptance.\n''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':1,'confidence':'low'},\n'changes':[\n{'file':'rtl/if_stage.sv','description':'''Change only the selected agreement index from pc[7:2] to\npc[7:2] XOR pc[13:8]. Keep the 64 local binary two-bit counters,\nreset state, immediate targets, predecode, PC priorities and updates.\n'''},\n{'file':'rtl/ex_stage.sv','description':'''Drive six-bit train_index with in.pc[7:2] XOR in.pc[13:8], matching\nfetch hashing. Preserve train_valid, train_agree, alignment checks,\nrecovery and all execute/hold/RVFI behavior. No interface-width change.\n'''},\n{'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'''Change the independent reference lookup and update indices to the\nsame six-bit PC fold. Adapt predictor-specific alias addresses and\nexpected states without relaxing architectural assertions. Cover\nlow-index conflicts that separate under folding and new folded\ncollisions, every index, opposite-bias aliases, counter saturation,\npre-edge lookup/update, reset, held and squashed branch events.\nRetain existing legality, alignment, bus, divider and RVFI checks.\n'''}]}\nclass Dumper(yaml.SafeDumper): pass\ndef string(d,s): return d.represent_scalar('tag:yaml.org,2002:str',s,style='|' if '\\\\n' in s else None)\nDumper.add_representer(str,string)\np.write_text(yaml.dump(h,Dumper=Dumper,sort_keys=False,width=90))\njsonschema.validate(yaml.safe_load(p.read_text()),json.load(open('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json')))\nprint('Wrote and validated',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
