{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_45","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport json\nfrom tools.eval.gowin import build_all,summarize\np=Path('/srv/hwebench/tmp/867d4c2f/e8a29c2f/predictor-r7s2')\nr=build_all(Path.cwd(),p/'snapshot-rtl','fpga/core_bench_si.sv',p/'snapshot-timing')\n(p/'snapshot-timing.json').write_text(json.dumps(r,indent=2));print(json.dumps(summarize(r),indent=2))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"{\n  \"placement_failed\": false,\n  \"fmax_mhz\": 60.426,\n  \"seeds\": [\n    61.745,\n    60.426,\n    60.426\n  ],\n  \"perturbations\": [\n    [\n      0,\n      61.745,\n      11\n    ],\n    [\n      1,\n      60.426,\n      11\n    ],\n    [\n      2,\n      60.426,\n      11\n    ]\n  ],\n  \"lut4\": 4828,\n  \"ff\": 951,\n  \"lutram\": 44,\n  \"bsram\": 4,\n  \"dsp\": 6,\n  \"logic_levels\": 11,\n  \"critical_path\": {\n    \"from\": \"cpu/u_mem/reg_q.ctrl.mem_to_reg_s0\",\n    \"to\": \"dmem_2_dmem_2_0_0_s\",\n    \"slack_ns\": -11.196,\n    \"arrival_ns\": 21.49,\n    \"logic_cells\": 11,\n    \"modules\": [\n      \"clock_ibuf\",\n      \"cpu/u_wb\",\n      \"cpu/u_ex\",\n      \"cpu/u_ex/u_alu\",\n      \"cpu/u_ex\"\n    ]\n  }\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_49", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-003-r7s2.yaml')\nd={\n'id':'hyp-20261001-003-r7s2',\n'title':'Single-read LUT-RAM agree predictor with forwarded training snapshots',\n'category':'predictor',\n'motivation':'''The current 64-entry agree predictor has good direction accuracy, but its\nlocally updated counters consume 128 flip-flops plus decode/update logic.\nPrior training retiming and eight-entry downsizing failed to clear the\nacceptance margin; the frequency limit remained in arithmetic. An independent\nsequential trace of canonical CoreMark found 529,588 conditional branches and\nabout 46,342 misses for the current table, so additional history has little\ncycle headroom. Preserve two-bit prediction quality while moving predictor\nstate into the FPGA's small distributed memories. A conventional table with\nseparate fetch and training reads failed to infer that compact implementation;\nremoving the training read is the essential architectural change.''',\n'hypothesis':'''Implement the same 64-entry, PC[7:2]-indexed, two-bit saturating agree\npredictor as four independent 16x2 distributed-RAM banks. Fetch selects row\nPC[5:2] in all banks and bank PC[7:6]. Each bank has one asynchronous read\nand one synchronous write; use synthesizable inferred RAM with the existing\nGowin distributed_ram attribute, not vendor-only logic absent from formal.\nRetain backward-taken/forward-not-taken bias, weak-agreement initial value\n2'b10, exact aligned B/J immediate targets, unconditional aligned JAL and\nunpredicted JALR. Do not change predictor capacity or add history.\n\nEliminate the second indexed read formerly needed to update a counter.\nCarry a two-bit training counter snapshot through if_id_t and id_ex_t.\nEX computes the ordinary saturating increment/decrement from this snapshot\nand branch_taken == in.imm[31], returning a two-bit train_counter together\nwith the existing qualified train_valid and train_index = in.pc[7:2].\nThe bank selected by train_index[5:4] writes train_counter to row [3:0].\nThis makes the table a single-read predictor rather than a two-read\nread-modify-write array that the vendor maps back into flip-flops.\n\nPreserve ordered updates across adjacent branches that alias. Fetch direction\nmust always use the pre-edge table MSB. Separately, when initialization is\ncomplete, train_valid is high and train_index == fetch PC[7:2], forward\ntrain_counter into the younger instruction's training snapshot instead of\ncapturing the stale table value. This bypass feeds only the two-bit metadata,\nnever the current predicted_taken direction, instruction data or PC mux.\nWithout this distinction, back-to-back aliases either lose an update or\nchange the prediction timing. Hold the snapshot with the ID/EX payload on\nload/divider/data-memory holds; invalid and squashed payloads cannot train.\nThe single EX resolver means no other training event can overtake a held\nbranch. Saturation always uses the latest ordered state after this bypass.\n\nAvoid a bulk reset on the RAM arrays. Reset a four-bit scrub index to zero\nand an init_done flag to zero. On each of the next sixteen non-reset edges,\nwrite 2'b10 to that row in all four banks; set init_done on the edge that\nwrites row 15. During this interval ignore training writes, force conditional\npredictions to the existing static backward/forward bias and export snapshot\n2'b10. Continue executing instructions normally, with JAL prediction and EX\nrecovery fully active: initialization must never stall architectural progress.\nAfter initialization, enable the dynamic lookup and ordinary training. Reset\nhas priority, disables writes on the reset edge and restarts the whole scrub,\nincluding reset during scrub or simultaneous prediction/training. Uninitialized\nRAM contents must not influence PC selection or metadata before init_done.\n\nKeep train_valid's existing valid/legal/non-faulting/advancing qualification,\nreset/redirect/accept/hold PC priority, actual predicted_taken metadata,\nvalidity-only squashes, raw decode/source-index paths, operand forwarding,\nload-use interlocks, divider occupancy, memory handshakes, arithmetic and\nALTOPS scheduling unchanged. Keep nret=1, every top-level port, decoder\nlegality, RVFI fields/order and all correctness/evaluation contracts intact.\n\nValidate the bank mapping and snapshot bypass against an independent model\nof the original saturating table after initialization. Cover consecutive\nsame-index branches, distinct PCs aliasing one index, opposite static biases,\nall four counter states and saturation, simultaneous lookup/update, stalls\nbetween lookup and resolution, squashed fetches, reset during each scrub row,\nand the last-scrub-to-first-training boundary. Run sequences well beyond\nsixteen cycles so testing actually exercises the initialized RAM; retain all\narchitectural assertions and update only predictor representation expectations.\nRun unchanged formal and architectural cosim, canonical stalled CoreMark with\nall CRC/UART/bounds/marker checks, and all three Gowin placement options.\n\nThe forecast is provisional. The main risks are RAM inference, an EX-to-ID\nmetadata bypass timing path, initialization corner cases, and the unchanged\nload-forwarding/ALU path limiting Fmax despite the predictor area reduction.\nAcceptance requires fitness strictly above 161.04216 iter/sec, not merely a\nsmaller predictor. Report actual median Fmax times bracketed iterations/cycle;\nno unmeasured frequency gain or completed formal qualification is claimed.''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':5,'confidence':'low'},\n'changes':[\n {'file':'rtl/if_stage.sv','description':'''Replace local counter flops with four single-read 16x2 distributed-RAM\nbanks, sixteen-cycle parallel-bank scrub and static prediction fallback.\nExport a two-bit training snapshot with same-index resolved-update forwarding,\nwhile keeping direction lookup strictly pre-edge. Accept computed train_counter\nwrites through one write port per bank and retain all fetch/redirect priorities.'''},\n {'file':'rtl/core_pkg.sv','description':'''Add a two-bit predictor_counter training snapshot to if_id_t and id_ex_t.\nKeep predicted_taken as the actual fetch direction, separate from the snapshot.'''},\n {'file':'rtl/id_stage.sv','description':'''Capture predictor_counter with the ordinary accepted payload and retain it\nunder holds. Existing valid-bit flushes suppress training of squashed payloads;\ndo not introduce late redirect gating into payload capture.'''},\n {'file':'rtl/ex_stage.sv','description':'''Compute saturating train_counter from in.predictor_counter and resolved\nagreement. Preserve train_valid qualification and the resolving PC index,\nbranch/jump recovery, arithmetic, metadata and all side-effect gating.'''},\n {'file':'rtl/core.sv','description':'''Wire the two-bit EX-to-IF train_counter in place of the old one-bit\ntrain_agree feedback. Preserve all top-level ports and other wiring.'''},\n {'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'''Adapt the independent prediction monitor to initialization, banked storage\nand forwarded training snapshots. Validate cycle-by-cycle counter equivalence\nafter initialization, alias forwarding without direction bypass, reset/scrub\nboundaries and arbitrary holds. Retain RVFI, legality, alignment, operand,\nretirement, divider and bus-stall assertions; update packed-bundle offsets.'''},\n]}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(d,sort_keys=False,width=88,default_flow_style=False))\njsonschema.validate(d,json.load(open('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json')))\nprint('Wrote complete schema-valid hypothesis:',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
