{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_57", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-003-r7s2.yaml')\nd={'id':'hyp-20261001-003-r7s2','title':'Single-bit agree prediction in native distributed RAM','category':'predictor','motivation':'''The current predictor uses 64 locally updated two-bit counters. Prior\ntraining retiming and table downsizing did not overcome the arithmetic timing\nlimit. Canonical CoreMark has only about 46,342 conditional direction misses\nin an independent sequential trace, so a larger history predictor offers\nlittle cycle headroom. A one-bit last-agreement table eliminates the indexed\ntraining read and saturating-counter logic, allowing the entire predictor\nstate to map to four native LUT-RAM primitives. This is a predictor storage\nand update architecture change, with an explicit prediction-accuracy cost.''','hypothesis':'''Replace the 64 two-bit agreement counters with one 64x1 distributed-RAM\nlast-agreement table. Keep fetch_index = pc[7:2] and train_index = in.pc[7:2].\nEvery qualified EX branch event writes train_agree directly into that entry;\nthere is no training read, hysteresis, counter arithmetic, history register,\nnew pipeline stage or prediction-metadata expansion. A branch predicts taken\nwhen the selected agreement bit equals the B-immediate sign, still qualified\nby legal branch encoding and exact target alignment. Preserve aligned JAL\nprediction and unpredicted JALR.\n\nUse synthesizable inferred distributed RAM with one asynchronous indexed\nfetch read and one synchronous write port. Form one write address, one write\ndata and one write enable outside the memory process, then write only through\nthat port. Do not reset the array in a loop, which prevents RAM inference.\nReset a six-bit initialization index and an init_done flag. On each of the\nnext 64 non-reset edges write agreement=1 to the indexed row, setting done\non the edge that initializes row 63. Suppress training writes during scrub;\nuse static backward-taken/forward-not-taken predictions until done. Fetch,\nJAL prediction, EX recovery and architectural retirement continue normally;\ninitialization must not stall the CPU. Reset disables the write port on its\nedge and restarts the complete scrub even when asserted during initialization\nor a simultaneous training event. Uninitialized table contents cannot affect\nprediction before init_done. Initialize every entry identically for every\nprogram, without program-specific addresses or signatures.\n\nAfter scrub, write_index=train_index, write_data=train_agree and\nwrite_enable=\"'!reset && train_valid. Before completion use init_index,\nconstant one and !reset instead. Fetch reads the pre-edge bit, including on\na same-index update. Use the unchanged qualified training event, so invalid,\nillegal, faulting, held and squashed instructions cannot update. Repeated\nsame-index events each apply once and naturally leave the last agreement.\n\nPreserve exact immediate targets, actual predicted_taken metadata, existing\none-bit direction mismatch recovery, reset/redirect/accept/hold PC priority,\nvalidity-only squashes, raw instruction/source-index paths, ID/EX holds,\nload-use interlocks, divider scheduling, forwarding, memory handshakes,\narithmetic and ALTOPS behavior. Keep nret=1, all external ports, decoder\nlegality, RVFI fields/order and every evaluation contract unchanged.\n\nAn isolated prototype completed the canonical ten-iteration CoreMark run\nwith both bus stalls in 4,223,556 marker-bracketed cycles, versus 4,194,434:\na 0.694 percent cycle increase. The unchanged evaluation helper accepted all\nfive CRCs, required UART validation text, bounds, completion and both markers.\nGowin placement options 0/1/2 measured 61.254, 63.306 and 63.306 MHz, versus\nthe champion'\"'s 64.576 MHz median. Option-zero resources were 4,904 LUT4s,\n949 registers and 44 LUT RAMs, compared with 5,180, 1,070 and 40. Four BSRAMs\nand six DSPs remained. The critical path still traversed retained load data\nselection, EX forwarding and the ALU into the data-memory interface.\n\nCalculated prototype fitness is 149.88791 iter/sec, 2.645 percent BELOW the\n153.96 champion. This does not clear the 161.04216 acceptance threshold.\nForecast minus three percent with low confidence rather than claim a measured\nwinning improvement. The smaller predictor circuit is real, but the current\narithmetic-limited design does not turn that reduction into sufficient Fmax.\nThese are preliminary timing and CoreMark results, not a completed formal\nqualification or an accepted replacement.\n\nAfter implementation reproduce median Gowin Fmax and bracketed cycles and\nrun the unchanged formal and architectural cosim gates. Update the independent\npredictor oracle for last-agreement semantics, all 64 initialization writes,\npre-edge lookup/update and exact-once training. Exercise reset during every\nscrub phase, the transition to dynamic mode, repeated aliases, opposite-bias\nbranches, loop exits/re-entry and arbitrary imem/dmem/divider holds. Execute\nwell beyond 64 cycles so initialized dynamic prediction is covered even\nthough the standard short formal window remains in static fallback. Preserve\nall architectural assertions; representation-specific tests must check the\nnew policy without discarding legality, alignment, ordering or stall coverage.\nPrincipal risks are the extra loop-exit misses, destructive aliases, reset\nscrub behavior and an unchanged arithmetic timing bottleneck.''','expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':-3,'confidence':'low'},'changes':[{'file':'rtl/if_stage.sv','description':'''Replace the 64 local two-bit counters with a 64x1 single-read distributed\nRAM updated directly from train_agree. Add a 64-cycle reset scrub with static\nprediction fallback and one common write port. Keep PC indexing, pre-edge\nlookup semantics, direct targets, fetch acceptance and recovery unchanged.'''},{'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'''Update the independent prediction oracle and storage observations for the\none-bit table and initialization. Cover every scrub row and reset boundary,\nloop re-entry, same-index lookup/update, destructive and opposite-bias aliases,\nstalled/squashed events and post-initialization operation. Retain every\narchitectural, RVFI, operand, divider, alignment and memory assertion.'''}]}\nclass Dumper(yaml.SafeDumper): pass\ndef represent_str(dumper,value):\n return dumper.represent_scalar('tag:yaml.org,2002:str',value,style='|' if '\\\\n' in value else None)\nDumper.add_representer(str,represent_str)\np.write_text(yaml.dump(d,Dumper=Dumper,sort_keys=False,width=90))\njsonschema.validate(d,json.load(open('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json')))\nprint('Saved measured best completed candidate:',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
