{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_3", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat schemas/hypothesis.schema.json cores/bench/LESSONS.md'", "aggregated_output": "{\n  \"$schema\": \"http://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#\",\n  \"type\": \"object\",\n  \"required\": [\"id\",\"title\",\"category\",\"motivation\",\"hypothesis\",\"expected_impact\",\"changes\"],\n  \"properties\": {\n    \"id\":       {\"type\": \"string\", \"pattern\": \"^hyp-[0-9]{8}-[0-9]{3}(-r[0-9]+s[0-9]+)?$\"},\n    \"title\":    {\"type\": \"string\", \"minLength\": 5},\n    \"category\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"enum\": [\"micro_opt\",\"structural\",\"extension\",\"predictor\",\"memory\"]},\n    \"motivation\":  {\"type\": \"string\", \"minLength\": 20},\n    \"hypothesis\":  {\"type\": \"string\", \"minLength\": 20},\n    \"expected_impact\": {\n      \"type\": \"object\",\n      \"required\": [\"fitness_delta_pct\",\"confidence\"],\n      \"properties\": {\n        \"fitness_delta_pct\": {\"type\": \"integer\"},\n        \"confidence\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"enum\": [\"low\",\"medium\",\"high\"]}\n      }\n    },\n    \"changes\": {\n      \"type\": \"array\", \"minItems\": 1,\n      \"items\": {\n        \"type\": \"object\",\n        \"required\": [\"file\",\"description\"],\n        \"properties\": {\n          \"file\":        {\n            \"type\": \"string\",\n            \"description\": \"Must be under rtl/ (any extension) or be a test/test_*.py cocotb suite. Anything else is rejected by the runtime sandbox check.\",\n            \"pattern\": \"^(rtl/[^.].*|test/test_[^/]+\\\\.py)$\"\n          },\n          \"description\": {\"type\": \"string\"}\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-002-r1s1 (improvement, +598.02%): Replacing combinational / and % in the ALU with a shared bounded iterative DIV/REM unit lets clock-period savings across all instructions outweigh the extra division cycles.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-003-r1s2 (regression, +9.90%): Local saturating updates in the 128-entry bimodal table avoid a second indexed counter read mux and shared write-data fanout, raising median Gowin Fmax from 5.204 to 5.596 MHz at unchanged CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-001-r2s0 (regression, +14.79%): Capturing 3D once and using parallel D/2D/3D trial subtractions in div_unit.sv cuts serial subtract/select depth from four restoring bits to two rad\n…[3553 of 6625 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:506ab69b37e3a3b6]…\nlates memory addressing from the general ALU/multiplier result mux, raising median Gowin Fmax from 64.576 to 69.104 MHz without changing CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-002-r7s1 (improvement, +13.30%): Registering 17x17 multiply partial products at EX/MEM and reconstructing even low-half MUL in MEM lets the multiplier timing cut outweigh the ~0.85% CoreMark cycle cost of load-use-style dependency bubbles.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-003-r7s2 (regression, -5.51%): Replacing 64 two-bit agree counters with a 64x1 last-agreement distributed RAM saved LUTs and registers, but removing hysteresis increased CoreMark cycles while the retained-load/forwarding/ALU critical path prevented a compensating Fmax gain.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-003-r8s2 (regression, -3.51%): Separating predicted and recovery PC registers kept CoreMark cycles unchanged but moved the worst path to agree-counter training and reduced Gowin Fmax, showing that removing the late EX-to-PC input mux can merely relocate the timing bottleneck.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
