{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_63", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-003-r9s2.yaml')\nd={\n'id':'hyp-20261001-003-r9s2',\n'title':'Gray-encode agree counters to shorten predictor training feedback',\n'category':'predictor',\n'motivation':'''The champion scores 174.44 iter/sec at 73.790 MHz and 4,230,003\nmarker-bracketed CoreMark cycles. Prior one-bit RAM prediction lost\nhysteresis, and table shrinking, training banking and split PC storage did\nnot clear acceptance. Preserve the successful 64-entry two-bit agree\npredictor's behavior while changing its state representation. Current\nbinary counters synthesize increment/decrement, saturation and update\nselection on the late EX branch-result path. Recent timing reports expose\nagree-counter training among the limiting paths after recovery is shortened.\n''',\n'hypothesis':'''Replace each locally updated binary saturating counter with two bits:\nprediction_q, the predicted agreement with backward-taken/forward-not-taken,\nand weak_q, whether that prediction has weak confidence. This is a Gray\nencoding of the SAME four-state machine, not a one-bit last-outcome\npredictor. Map old binary states 00,01,10,11 to (prediction_q,weak_q)\n00,01,11,10 respectively. Reset both bits to one, exactly reproducing the\ncurrent weak-agreement reset value 2'b10.\n\nFor each of the existing 64 entries, retain precisely the current local\nwrite condition train_valid && train_index == entry. On a qualified update,\nset weak_q <= train_agree \"'^ prediction_q. Update prediction_q <= train_agree\nonly if the OLD weak_q is one; otherwise hold prediction_q. Both assignments\nuse pre-edge state, so a weak prediction which changes direction remains\nweak, and an outcome matching the old prediction makes the state strong.\nAn unselected entry holds both bits; reset dominates any simultaneous write.\nDrive agreements[entry] directly from prediction_q. Keep the existing\nPC[7:2] index, branch bias, legality/alignment predecode, immediate B/J target\narithmetic, training timing, single PC register and accepted predicted_taken\nmetadata exactly unchanged. Use legal SystemVerilog identifiers such as\nweak_q; weak alone is a reserved keyword.\n\nThis encoding removes counter addition/subtraction and terminal-state\ncomparisons from training. The direction bit can take the late training\noutcome directly through its weak-state-qualified enable, while the\nconfidence bit needs only an XOR with the old direction and the existing\nentry write enable. Only one state bit changes on each adjacent transition.\nKeep per-entry storage and update logic; do not introduce a shared indexed\ntraining read mux, additional training latency, memory inference, another\npredictor component or any pipeline changes. The state remains 128 bits.\n\nThe mapping is a bijection and all eight state/outcome transitions must\nmatch the original saturating-counter machine exactly. Therefore identical\nreset and training events imply identical predictions forever, including\nfetch/training index collisions, held fetches and destructive aliases.\nKeep the existing independent binary-counter oracle unmodified: test the\nnew representation against that different representation. Preserve every\nexisting exact-cycle, operand, trap, architectural and progress assertion.\nAdd exhaustive transition coverage, saturation in both directions, reset\ncoincident with update, non-selected entry retention, and update/lookup\nalias cases. Retain stall-qualified training and recovery, original RVFI,\nnret=1, divider/multiply scheduling and all external IO unchanged.\n\nRun unchanged formal, pipeline tests and full RVFI cosim. Run canonical\nCoreMark with both memory stalls, validate all CRCs, UART text, bounds and\ntiming markers, and compare its bracketed cycle count with 4,230,003. Measure\nall three Gowin placement options. Acceptance requires fitness above\n182.46424 iter/sec, or median Fmax above 77.182428 MHz if cycles are unchanged.\nThe eight-percent forecast is unmeasured. Risks are that Gowin already\nsimplifies the binary machine equally well, update-enable fanout remains\nlimiting, or unchanged divider/ALU/PC paths cap the gain. Exact equivalence\nof predictor behavior does not establish a physical timing improvement.\n'\"''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':8,'confidence':'medium'},\n'changes':[\n{'file':'rtl/if_stage.sv','description':'''Replace each local binary two-bit saturating counter with prediction_q\nand weak_q using the exact Gray-state mapping and update equations.\nKeep the existing local entry write decode, reset priority, agreements\nread vector, predictor lookup and all fetch/recovery behavior unchanged.\n'''},\n{'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'''Retain the independent binary-counter predictor model and all existing\nchecks. Add explicit coverage of all four confidence/direction states,\neight transitions, both saturated endpoints, reset/update priority,\nunselected-entry retention and training/read aliases under bus stalls.\n'''}]}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(d,sort_keys=False,width=88,default_flow_style=False))\njsonschema.validate(d,json.load(open('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json')))\nprint('Revised schema-valid predictor hypothesis:',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
