{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_99", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-003-r9s2.yaml');d=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text())\nd['title']='Compact 32-entry Gray-encoded agree predictor'\nd['motivation']='''The champion scores 174.44 iter/sec at 73.790 MHz and 4,230,003\nmarker-bracketed CoreMark cycles. Prior eight-entry prediction increased\naliasing, while one-bit RAM lost useful hysteresis. Use a middle-sized\n32-entry agree predictor with full two-bit hysteresis and a Gray state\nencoding that simplifies its late training feedback. This bounded predictor\nreplacement targets lookup fan-in, training fanout and local update logic\ntogether while preserving the existing pipeline and recovery architecture.\n'''\nd['hypothesis']='''Replace the 64 binary agree counters with 32 locally updated two-bit\nGray-encoded agree entries. This is one compact predictor design, with no\nBTB, return stack, history predictor, additional pipeline stage or change\nto recovery timing. Use fetch_index = pc[6:2] \"'^ {4'\"'b0,pc[7]}.\nKeep the existing six-bit EX train_index = in.pc[7:2] and all core ports;\ninside if_stage fold it identically as train_index[4:0] \"'^\n{4'\"'b0,train_index[5]}. Use the five-bit folded index in each of the 32\nlocal write-enable comparisons and the 32-to-1 prediction lookup.\n\nStore prediction_q, the predicted agreement with backward-taken/forward-\nnot-taken, and weak_q, whether that prediction has weak confidence. Map\nordinary saturating states 00,01,10,11 to (prediction_q,weak_q)\n00,01,11,10 respectively. Reset both bits to one, reproducing the existing\nweak-agreement initial state 2'b10. On a qualified local training event,\nset weak_q <= train_agree \"'^ prediction_q. Update prediction_q <= train_agree\nonly when the OLD weak_q is one; otherwise retain prediction_q. Both use\npre-edge state. Unselected entries retain both bits, and reset wins over\ntraining. Use legal identifiers such as weak_q, since weak is reserved.\nDrive agreements[entry] directly from prediction_q.\n\nAll eight local state/outcome transitions are exactly equivalent to the\noriginal two-bit saturating counter. Confidence remains weak when a weak\nprediction changes direction; an outcome matching the old prediction makes\nit strong. This retains hysteresis while replacing increment/decrement and\nterminal-state decoding with one confidence-qualified direction update and\none XOR. There are 64 state bits, half the current table'\"'s storage, and a\n32-way lookup. Keep storage and update logic local to each entry; do not\nintroduce RAM, a shared indexed training read or delayed training events.\n\nRetain the existing branch bias, legal B/J predecode, alignment checks,\nimmediate target calculation, predicted_taken capture, direct JAL prediction\nand unpredicted JALR. Leave the single PC register, EX actual resolution,\nvalidity-only recovery, raw instruction/source paths, load/multiply hazards,\nDIV/REM schedule and memory handshake behavior unchanged. Keep nret=1,\nall RVFI fields, exact-once retirement and every hard invariant. New aliases\nmay change predictions and cycle counts, but may never change architectural\nresults, accepted-training discipline or redirect priority over fetch stalls.\n\nKeep the independent binary-counter test oracle. Adjust only its table\nsize, reset length, folded index and observed-state decoding: reconstruct\nbinary state as {prediction_q,prediction_q XOR weak_q}. Its saturating\ntransition algorithm, architectural checks, exact-cycle requirements for\nunchanged datapaths and stall/flush assertions stay intact. Test all eight\ntransitions and both saturated endpoints, both PC[7] fold values,\ndestructive aliases, simultaneous lookup/update, reset/update priority,\nheld branches, wrong-path instructions and data/instruction stalls.\n\nAn isolated prototype passed the eight existing full-RVFI trace cases:\nselftest plus three random programs, each with and without both bus stalls.\nCanonical stalled CoreMark completed in 4,245,129 bracketed cycles with all\nrequired CRCs, validation text and no out-of-bounds access. That is a\n0.358-percent cycle increase from 4,230,003. Placement option one measured\n73.540 MHz and 5,217 LUT4s, versus the champion's 5,422 LUT4s. At that\nfrequency the estimated fitness is 173.23384 iter/sec, 0.691 percent below\n174.44. This is not a qualifying improvement; the forecast is deliberately\nnegative rather than claiming an unobserved gain. Complete all three\nplacement options and the unchanged formal suite during implementation.\n\nAcceptance still requires median Fmax times measured iterations/cycle to\nexceed 182.46424 iter/sec. The main risks are aliasing from halved capacity\nand unchanged divider/ALU/recovery paths limiting Fmax after table logic\nshrinks. Preserve the champion unless that unchanged threshold is actually\nexceeded. No correctness relaxation or benchmark-specific indexing is part\nof this hypothesis.\n'''\nd['expected_impact']={'fitness_delta_pct':-1,'confidence':'low'}\nd['changes'][0]['description']='''Replace the 64 binary agree counters with 32 local prediction_q/weak_q\nentries. Apply PC[6:2] XOR zero-extended PC[7] consistently to fetch and\nthe existing six-bit training interface. Use the specified Gray-state\nmapping and exact hysteresis equations. Preserve reset, train-valid\nqualification, fetch targets, recovery, legality and alignment behavior.\n'''\nd['changes'][1]['description']='''Keep the independent binary saturation algorithm and all architectural,\ncycle, operand and progress assertions. Adapt table/reset dimensions,\nindex folding and DUT-state decoding to the new representation. Cover\nevery transition, saturation, both fold-bit values, destructive aliases,\nreset/training priority and lookup/update collisions under stalls.\n'''\nclass Dumper(yaml.SafeDumper): pass\ndef represent(dumper,value): return dumper.represent_scalar('tag:yaml.org,2002:str',value,style='|' if '\\\\n' in value else None)\nDumper.add_representer(str,represent)\np.write_text(yaml.dump(d,Dumper=Dumper,sort_keys=False,width=88))\njsonschema.validate(d,json.load(open('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json')))\nprint('Wrote final candidate with measured limitations:',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
